window.BT_INDEX={"docs":[{"file":"01-overview.html","num":"01","title":"Overview"},{"file":"02-requirements.html","num":"02","title":"Requirements"},{"file":"03-target-architecture.html","num":"03","title":"Target architecture"},{"file":"04-accounts-access-and-operations.html","num":"04","title":"Accounts, access & operations"},{"file":"05-data-and-brokers.html","num":"05","title":"Data & brokers"},{"file":"06-instruments-and-baskets.html","num":"06","title":"Instruments & baskets"},{"file":"07-strategies.html","num":"07","title":"Strategies"},{"file":"08-exits-and-risk.html","num":"08","title":"Exits & risk"},{"file":"09-learning-engine.html","num":"09","title":"Learning engine"},{"file":"10-trade-selection.html","num":"10","title":"Trade selection"},{"file":"11-strategy-lab.html","num":"11","title":"Strategy Lab"},{"file":"12-portfolio-monitoring.html","num":"12","title":"Portfolio monitoring"},{"file":"13-reports-and-ui.html","num":"13","title":"Reports & UI"},{"file":"14-ai-assistant.html","num":"14","title":"AI assistant"},{"file":"15-smart-insights-and-automation.html","num":"15","title":"Smart insights & automation"},{"file":"16-configuration.html","num":"16","title":"Configuration"},{"file":"17-deployment-and-operations.html","num":"17","title":"Deployment & operations"},{"file":"18-roadmap.html","num":"18","title":"Roadmap"},{"file":"19-risks-and-compliance.html","num":"19","title":"Risks & compliance"},{"file":"20-extended-modules.html","num":"20","title":"Extended modules"},{"file":"21-glossary.html","num":"21","title":"Glossary"}],"sections":[{"d":0,"h":"1. What xDrishti is","a":"1-what-xdrishti-is","t":"Name: xDrishti — from Sanskrit drishti (दृष्टि), \"vision, insight\": seeing where your edge is, and where it is not. Short forms: xd (CLI), xd- (containers), XDrishti. (.NET projects), xdrishti (database, Keychain paths). A personal, self-learning trade-planning and portfolio-monitoring system for Indian markets (NSE), running entirely on your Mac in Podman containers. Data: your 1-minute CSV files bootstrap history; the Dhan Data API keeps every tracked instrument up to date after market close and streams live prices only for what you're actively trading or holding . All timeframes (5m … 1h, 1D, 1W, 1M) are aggregated from 1-minute bars. Every evening it: 1. fetches the day's 1-minute data for all tracked instruments and aggregates all timeframes, 2. syncs your broker accounts and grades how previous recommendations played out, 3. updates what it has learned about every setup cell (strategy × timeframe × side × style × exit × regime), 4. scans your baskets (e.g. MyChoice, Best stocks) for tomorrow's candidate trades, 5. estimates each candidate's probability of success and expected reward (in R-multiples), 6. keeps only the best few that fit your risk limits (e.g. max 3 new entries), sized per broker account, 7. publishes a next-day trade plan with entry trigger, stop, exit method, size and reasons. At any time you can: - add your broker accounts and choose what each is used for (data, trading, portfolio), - review and approve the proposed next-day trades and AI suggestions (nothing is armed without you), - manage the instruments you work on and group them into baskets , - monitor your portfolio across accounts — when each trade was added, return %, XIRR , total value — grouped by basket, account, strategy or sector, - evaluate results by basket, by instrument, by individual trade or across all trades , - submit your own strategies in the Strategy Lab and get an honest, tested verdict. You execute (or skip) the trades. The system learns from every outcome."},{"d":0,"h":"2. Core principles","a":"2-core-principles","t":"Principle Consequence 1 Predict whether a setup works, not where price goes Every output is a conditional trade plan with a probability and expected value 2 The setup cell is the unit of learning Exit methods (1:2, 1:3, trailing, pyramiding…) are learned per cell and fitted per trade 3 Rules generate, ML filters, risk rules decide Transparent strategies → calibrated ML probability → deterministic portfolio selection 4 LLM is an analyst, never the trader All numbers come from the deterministic engine; LLM explains, answers, drafts 5 Every recommendation is graded Including rejected candidates — proves the selector adds value 6 Configuration over code Instruments, baskets, timeframes, strategies, exits, limits, accounts — all configurable 7 Honest evaluation Walk-forward out-of-sample only, costs always on, paper stage before live 8 Single code path Backtest, Strategy Lab, nightly scan and grading run the same engine code 9 One source of bars Everything aggregates from 1-minute data (CSV or Dhan) with the same rules 10 Live only where it matters Real-time data only for the active set; everything else end-of-day 11 Local and cheap No cloud dependency; ≈ ₹590/month running cost (Dhan Data API)"},{"d":0,"h":"3. Trading styles","a":"3-trading-styles","t":"Style Entry timeframes Holding Forced exit Intraday 5m, 10m, 15m, 30m, 1h Same session Square-off time (e.g. 15:15) Swing 1h, 1D 1–5 sessions End of session N (N ≤ 5) Long-term holdings — Your choice Not managed by system exits (monitored in the portfolio) Both BUY and SELL. Overnight shorts use futures (cash shorts can't be carried overnight in India)."},{"d":0,"h":"4. Fixed decisions","a":"4-fixed-decisions","t":"Topic Decision Use Personal only — one user, only your own broker accounts; no recommendations to others Market NSE: cash stocks + index/stock futures; options in a later phase Historical data Your 1-minute CSV files + Dhan historical API for gaps Market-data API Dhan Data API only (EOD for tracked instruments, live for the active set) Accounts Multiple accounts (Dhan; other brokers later), each with roles : Data · Trading · Portfolio — one account can hold several; exactly one primary Data account Access Login required (username + password; password re-entry for sensitive actions); single owner; every screen and API protected Operating model Configure once, services run it — background services do the work whether or not you're logged in Insights Pre-trade checklist, portfolio Action center per account, price alerts, data quality gate, report auto-insights Learning visibility & autonomy Learning screen : progress vs a frozen-model counterfactual, lessons (problem → fix → result), plan-vs-actual execution gap, tweak & re-run experiments; smart rules (no-trade days, equity-curve throttle, meta-labelling, learned entry windows, correlation-aware plan); grading on real fills; automatic loss tags; weekly digest; autonomy levels with auto-approval so you only review what matters Decisions Human in the loop — system/AI propose next-day tickets and instrument/basket suggestions; you approve, modify or reject Instruments Master list → tracked instruments → baskets (manual, rule-based, system) Portfolio Holdings, positions & trade history from Dhan + CSV/manual; FIFO lots; XIRR ; buckets Execution v1 manual (system auto-journals your fills); assisted → semi-auto → auto in a later phase Frontend React (TypeScript) Backend .NET (ASP.NET Core, modular monolith) Database PostgreSQL with TimescaleDB extension ML ML.NET inside the .NET solution — no Python LLM Local open model in a GPU-enabled Podman container; optional Hermes Agent front-end after PoC Deployment Podman Desktop on Apple M4 Pro / 48 GB; no cloud Full rationale: 03-target-architecture.md."},{"d":0,"h":"5. Decisions","a":"5-decisions","t":"Recorded 2026-10-09 (story P0-12). Numbers and names below are working values: capital, accounts and baskets are entered in the application (ADR 0002), never in the repository. Question Decision Status Q1 CSV format — one sample file to set up the import profile (column names, timestamp format, bar start or end, raw or adjusted prices) Not provided yet Open — story P0-14 stays blocked; needed before P1-07 Q2 Trading capital and risk per trade Defaults stay: 0.5% per trade, 1.5% daily loss limit (doc 16). Capital is entered per account in the accounts wizard Decided in P1-16 Q3 Initial baskets and their purpose Working names as configured in doc 16: Best stocks (intraday + swing), MyChoice (intraday), MyLongTerm (swing, BUY only); baskets are created in the app Decided in P1-15 (baskets are app data, not a blocker) Q4 Several accounts: replicate or distribute Replicate (default; allocation.mode stays configurable) Decided Q5 Portfolio history depth Start with the Dhan trade-history window ; import older lots from CSV / contract notes later (P2-03) Decided Q6 ISP static IP Later — needed only before automated orders (P12); revisit at the P11 exit Decided Q7 Repository hosting Local-only git; no remote or CI dependence for now; back the code up (doc 17 §1) Decided Q8 Podman machine size Keep 10 CPUs / 24 GB / 120 GB — verified by the xd-llm benchmark (P0-15, ADR 0005); revisit before tick data (P13) Decided, verified"},{"d":0,"h":"6. Definition of success (v1)","a":"6-definition-of-success-v1","t":"- Your CSV history imported; all tracked instruments updated every evening; all timeframes consistent. - Portfolio shows every holding/trade with added date, return %, XIRR and value, grouped by bucket/basket/account. - Nightly run completes unattended in < 30 min and publishes ≤ max new entries per day tickets per account. - Each ticket: symbol, side, style, timeframe, trigger & validity window, stop, exit policy, size, P(win), expected R, plain-English reason. - Results viewable for any date range by all trades / basket / instrument / trade / strategy / account. - Your own strategies tested via the Strategy Lab without code changes. - After ≥ 12 weeks of paper trading: positive expectancy after costs, consistent with backtest expectations."},{"d":0,"h":"7. Document map","a":"7-document-map","t":"Doc Content 02 Requirements Functional & non-functional requirements 03 Target architecture Tech stack, components, solution structure, data model, APIs 04 Accounts, access & operations Account roles, login, service-level operation, human-in-the-loop approvals 05 Data & brokers CSV import, aggregation, Dhan EOD & live (active set), storage, quality 06 Instruments & baskets Master list, tracked instruments, baskets, evaluation lenses 07 Strategies Strategy anatomy, conditional plans, library, features, regimes 08 Exits & risk R-multiples, exit policies, pyramiding, sizing, limits, costs 09 Learning engine Cell stats, meta-model, walk-forward, drift, hypothesis loop 10 Trade selection From N candidates to top 3, per-account allocation 11 Strategy Lab Runtime strategy rules, testing pipeline, verdict reports 12 Portfolio monitoring Holdings, trades, returns, XIRR, buckets, live values 13 Reports & UI React screens and report catalogue 14 AI assistant Local LLM roles, tools, guardrails, Hermes Agent decision 15 Smart insights & automation Pre-trade checklist, portfolio Action center, alerts, data quality gate, auto-insights 16 Configuration Full trading config reference 17 Deployment & operations Podman topology, secrets, backups, costs 18 Roadmap Phases and exit criteria 19 Risks & compliance Pitfalls, safeguards, SEBI notes 20 Extended modules Auto-execution, options, tick/depth data, fundamentals 21 Glossary Terms"},{"d":1,"h":"1. Functional requirements","a":"1-functional-requirements","t":""},{"d":1,"h":"FR-1 Data","a":"fr-1-data","t":"1. Import 1-minute OHLCV from CSV files (upload, watched folder, CLI) using saved column-mapping profiles; preview, validate, upsert with source precedence, report and undo per import job. 2. Aggregate 1-minute bars into 5m/10m/15m/30m/1h (09:15-aligned), 1D, 1W, 1M with identical rules for CSV and Dhan data; refresh after every import/fetch. 3. End-of-day fetch from the Dhan Data API for all tracked instruments (today's 1-minute + daily), gap backfill, instrument master refresh. 4. Live data only for the active set (open positions, armed tickets, context indices, optional pinned basket) via Dhan WebSocket; holdings via quote polling; live bars replaced by official bars at EOD. 5. Adjust for splits/bonus; keep raw prices for tradable levels. 6. Store context: indices, India VIX, breadth, sector indices, calendar, expiries, lot sizes, corporate actions. 7. Data-quality checks; fail closed (no plan) on critical failures. 8. Data quality window : per-instrument validation of completeness, bar sanity, spikes, stored-vs-recomputed aggregates and derived-vs-official daily, with inspect, repair and run history."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-2 Instruments & baskets","a":"fr-2-instruments-baskets","t":"1. Master list from Dhan's instrument master, searchable and filterable. 2. Tracked instruments: add/remove (single or bulk); activation triggers history backfill (CSV, then Dhan). 3. Baskets: manual, rule-based (dynamic) and system baskets; an instrument can be in many baskets; membership history kept. 4. Baskets define scopes for scans, Strategy Lab tests, reports, portfolio grouping and live watch. 5. Evaluation lenses: all trades, by basket, by instrument, by individual trade, by strategy, by account."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-3 Brokers & accounts","a":"fr-3-brokers-accounts","t":"1. Pluggable broker adapters behind common interfaces (Dhan in v1; others later). 2. Any number of accounts, each with its own risk profile, styles, instruments and mode. 3. Each account has one or more roles — Data, Trading, Portfolio; exactly one primary Data account (optional standby with automatic failover); role changes audited and protected by password re-entry. 4. Read funds, positions, holdings, trade history and ledger per account (read-only in v1). 5. Auto-journal: match broker fills to recommendations. 6. Token lifecycle per account (Dhan 24 h tokens via API key/secret)."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-3b Access & operating model","a":"fr-3b-access-operating-model","t":"1. Login required for every screen and API (username + password; no second factor — personal, LAN-only), idle timeout, lockout, password re-entry for sensitive actions. 2. All configuration and actions are persisted as desired state and executed by background services under a service identity — independent of any logged-in session or open browser. 3. Missed schedules (machine asleep) are caught up on start; jobs are idempotent and retried. 4. Services screen: status, schedule, last/next run, run now, pause/resume. 4. Service schedules editable in the UI (type, time, days, window, interval, run-after, retries, timeout, catch-up, parameters) with dependency & timing validation; automatic data failover to a standby account."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-3c Human-in-the-loop decisions","a":"fr-3c-human-in-the-loop-decisions","t":"1. Next-day tickets are proposed ; you approve, modify (re-validated) or reject; unreviewed tickets lapse at the review cutoff; only approved tickets are armed. 2. AI/engine suggestions (track instrument, basket membership, promotions, config changes) go to an inbox for approval, with reasons and expiry. 3. Reports compare system proposals vs your decisions. 4. Pre-trade checklist per ticket (data quality, holdings conflict, sector exposure, event risk). 5. Optional auto-approval at the cutoff for tickets above a probability threshold with every check passing (autonomy level supervised or higher); auto-approved tickets are marked and audited."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-4 Portfolio monitoring","a":"fr-4-portfolio-monitoring","t":"1. Build transactions, FIFO lots and positions from broker trade history, CSV imports and manual entries. 2. Per position/trade: added date, days held, quantity, average cost, LTP, value, unrealised/realised P&L, return %, day change, XIRR , CAGR, weight. 3. Per group (bucket, basket, account, strategy, sector, total): invested, value, returns, XIRR , benchmark XIRR and alpha, drawdown. 4. Portfolio buckets (exclusive, additive) and basket lens (overlapping). 5. Live values during market hours; daily valuation snapshots; alerts. 6. Per-account tiles and an Action center of positions needing a decision (rules in doc 15); price alerts evaluated by services."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-5 Strategies & signals","a":"fr-5-strategies-signals","t":"1. Strategies declare timeframes, sides, styles, setup, trigger, entry, initial stop, filters, invalidation. 2. Multi-timeframe filters (bias from higher timeframe). 3. Output = conditional trade plans (trigger + validity window + invalidation)."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-6 Exits & risk","a":"fr-6-exits-risk","t":"1. Exit policy library: fixed R:R, partials, ATR/structure/EMA trailing, pyramiding, time stop, session exit, max hold (≤ 5 sessions), event exit. 2. Exit chosen per setup cell (walk-forward), fitted per trade (volatility, structure, obstacles). 3. Fixed-fractional sizing with lot rounding; portfolio and per-account limits."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-7 Learning","a":"fr-7-learning","t":"1. Walk-forward backtest of every strategy × timeframe × side × exit policy. 2. Per-cell statistics (Bayesian, recency-weighted, regime-conditioned). 3. Calibrated meta-model P(success) per candidate; must beat baselines. 4. Daily grading of primary, reserve and rejected recommendations. 5. Drift detection, auto-pause, re-qualification; cell lifecycle. 6. Hypothesis loop: one-variable experiments, tested offline and in shadow before promotion. 7. Learning changelog explaining every automatic change. 8. Learn from actual executions : grade on fills, decompose plan-vs-actual gap, per-liquidity slippage model, capacity score (doc 09 §9). 9. Lessons for every detected problem (what went wrong → diagnosis → fix → result) with revert (doc 09 §10). 10. Learning screen showing progress against a frozen-model counterfactual, learning health, setups and lessons (doc 09 §11). 11. Tweak & re-run : change learning knobs and replay walk-forward with honest comparison rules; shadow → apply with password; automatic rollback (doc 09 §12). 12. Autonomy levels (advisory / supervised / autonomous within limits) to reduce manual work while risk increases, new strategies, live execution and account changes always need you (doc 09 §13). 13. Smart rules learned from history and applied nightly: market gate (no-trade / reduced days), equity-curve throttle, meta-labelling (take / size), learned entry windows, correlation-aware plan (doc 09 §14). 14. Automatic loss tags on every losing trade, linked to lessons; weekly learning digest ."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-8 Selection & plan","a":"fr-8-selection-plan","t":"1. Eligibility filters, duplicate merge (confluence), ranking by conservative expected R. 2. Constraints: max new entries/day, max open positions, heat, sector, correlation, net exposure, loss limits. 3. Per-account allocation (replicate or distribute) and sizing. 4. Primary plan + reserve list; optional pre-open gap validation."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-9 Strategy Lab","a":"fr-9-strategy-lab","t":"1. Submit strategies at runtime: rules file, form, plain English (LLM-drafted, user-confirmed), C plugin; choose a basket (or instruments) as the test universe. 2. Validate (schema, look-ahead lint), quick test, full walk-forward test, robustness checks. 3. Verdict (PASS / MARGINAL / FAIL) with reasons, KPIs, charts, comparisons, trade replay. 4. Test ledger with multiple-testing-adjusted thresholds; promotion only through paper stage."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-10 Reports & UI","a":"fr-10-reports-ui","t":"1. Next-day plan per account, journal, portfolio, performance for any date range with lenses and filters. 2. Reports landing overview with auto-insights; multi-basket comparison; pivot builder. 3. Visual analytics: equity/drawdown, heatmaps, exit comparison, MAE/MFE, calibration, selector effectiveness, Monte Carlo, learning changelog, cell lifecycle, data quality, discipline. 4. Config editor with validation, diff and versioned activation. 5. Exports: PDF/HTML/CSV. 6. Responsive layout from 360 px phones to 4K / ultrawide monitors."},{"d":1,"h":"FR-11 AI assistant","a":"fr-11-ai-assistant","t":"1. Daily briefing and weekly review written from structured data. 2. Natural-language questions over data via read-only tools; numbers only from tool results. 3. Strategy drafting for the Strategy Lab; hypothesis proposals."},{"d":1,"h":"2. Non-functional requirements","a":"2-non-functional-requirements","t":"ID Requirement NFR-1 Fully local on macOS (Apple Silicon) in Podman containers; no cloud dependency NFR-2 EOD fetch + nightly cycle < 45 min for 200 tracked instruments; full re-learn < 4 h; CSV import ≥ 1 M rows/min NFR-2b Live updates < 2 s from tick to screen for the active set; live feed limited to the active set NFR-3 Reproducible: any plan/test regenerable from data snapshot + config version + model version NFR-4 No look-ahead: enforced by engine design and automated tests NFR-5 Explainable: reasons and statistics attached to every recommendation NFR-6 Config validated on load (JSON Schema); invalid config never activates NFR-7 Fail closed: incomplete data or failed stage ⇒ no plan, clear error NFR-8 Secrets never in code, config, logs or LLM prompts NFR-9 Personal use: single owner; login (password); LAN-only access; audit of logins, changes and approvals NFR-11 Services keep running when no one is logged in; UI is only a control plane NFR-12 Responsive UI from 360 px phones to 3840 px monitors with no horizontal page scroll NFR-10 Running cost ≈ ₹590/month (Dhan Data API); all software open source / free"},{"d":2,"h":"1. Architecture at a glance","a":"1-architecture-at-a-glance","t":"Style: a .NET modular monolith (one codebase, clear module boundaries) deployed as a few containers, with a React single-page app, PostgreSQL + TimescaleDB as the only database, ML.NET for machine learning (no Python), and a local LLM — all on Podman Desktop. Why a modular monolith (not microservices): one developer, one machine, shared engine code across backtest / Strategy Lab / nightly scan / grading. Modules can be split into services later if ever needed."},{"d":2,"h":"2. Technology decisions","a":"2-technology-decisions","t":""},{"d":2,"h":"2.1 Frontend — React","a":"21-frontend-react","t":"Concern Choice Reason Framework React 19 + TypeScript + Vite Your choice; fast dev build UI kit MUI (Material UI) + MUI X Data Grid (community) Mature components, dense data tables Price charts TradingView Lightweight Charts (open source) Candles with entry/stop/target/trail overlays, trade replay Analytics charts Apache ECharts (echarts-for-react) Heatmaps, calendar, Monte Carlo fans, scatter, large data Server state TanStack Query Caching, refetch, pagination Routing React Router — Forms React Hook Form + Zod Strategy form builder, config forms Code editor Monaco + monaco-yaml with JSON Schema Strategy rules & config editing with live validation Real-time @microsoft/signalr Live prices & P&L for the active set, job progress, notifications File upload react-dropzone + chunked upload CSV imports with preview API client Generated from OpenAPI (orval) Typed client, no hand-written DTOs Tests Vitest + Testing Library; Playwright for E2E —"},{"d":2,"h":"2.2 Backend — .NET","a":"22-backend-net","t":"Concern Choice Reason Runtime .NET 10 (LTS) , C Your choice; LTS support window Web ASP.NET Core (Minimal API endpoint groups), OpenAPI Lightweight, typed Real-time SignalR Progress & notifications to React Data access EF Core + Npgsql for domain tables; Npgsql binary COPY / Dapper for bulk bars EF for productivity, raw speed for time series Background jobs Hangfire + Hangfire.PostgreSql Cron schedules, retries, queues, built-in dashboard; no extra infra Validation FluentValidation; JsonSchema.Net for YAML config/strategy One schema shared with the React editor YAML YamlDotNet Trading config & strategy rules Strategy DSL Own parser (Superpower) → compiled LINQ expression trees Safe (no eval), fast, look-ahead-checked Indicators Own streaming implementations (XDrishti.Indicators, built in P3-01, ADR 0031); cross-checked in the tests against Skender.Stock.Indicators 2.7.3 (MIT, test-only, exact pin) Point-in-time control, speed Numerics MathNet.Numerics Statistics, Monte Carlo, Bayesian updates; own XIRR solver (Newton–Raphson + bisection) CSV parsing Sep or CsvHelper (streaming) + Npgsql binary COPY Fast bulk import of 1-minute files Spreadsheet reading ExcelDataReader 3.7.0 (MIT), Infrastructure/Portfolio/SpreadsheetReader behind ISpreadsheetReader (P2-17, ADR 0029) Dhan's downloaded reports come as legacy .xls (BIFF8) and .xlsx; read natively, no conversion step; the Application layer never references the package Live feed System.Net.WebSockets client + binary packet parser (Dhan little-endian format) Active-set streaming in xd-feed Machine learning ML.NET (LightGBM / FastTree trainers, PAV calibration, feature contributions) Train and score in-process; one language, one code path LLM client Microsoft.Extensions.AI (OpenAI-compatible) Tool calling against the local model MCP server ModelContextProtocol C SDK Read-only tools for the assistant / Hermes HTTP resilience Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience (Polly) Broker API retries, rate limits Auth ASP.NET Core Identity (single user, password) Personal use; cookie auth CLI System.CommandLine (xd tool) Strategy Lab and ops from terminal Logging/telemetry Serilog → Seq ; OpenTelemetry metrics/traces Local, searchable logs Tests xUnit, Testcontainers (real Postgres/Timescale), Verify snapshots Engine correctness & look-ahead tests"},{"d":2,"h":"2.3 Data — PostgreSQL","a":"23-data-postgresql","t":"Concern Choice Reason Database PostgreSQL 17+ Your choice Time series TimescaleDB extension (official image) Hypertables + native compression for 1-min bars/ticks; continuous aggregates build 5m/10m/15m/30m/1h bars from 1m with 09:15 alignment; refreshed after CSV imports and EOD fetch Queue Hangfire tables in Postgres; LISTEN/NOTIFY for events No separate broker (Redis/RabbitMQ) needed Analytics Plain SQL + materialised views for report aggregates Single database to back up and query Backups pg dump nightly + restic to external SSD Simple, verifiable Estimated size: 1-min bars for 200 symbols × 8 years ≈ 150 M rows → 5–10 GB with Timescale compression."},{"d":2,"h":"2.4 ML — ML.NET only (no Python)","a":"24-ml-mlnet-only-no-python","t":"Our ML need is modest and well-defined: a binary classifier on tabular features ( 10⁵–10⁶ rows, 50–150 features) answering \"does this candidate hit target before stop?\", plus calibration and explanations. ML.NET covers all of it: Need ML.NET capability Gradient-boosted trees LightGbm trainer (Microsoft.ML.LightGbm); FastTree (ML.NET's own GBDT) as fallback Baselines LbfgsLogisticRegression, SdcaLogisticRegression Probability calibration Calibrators incl. PAV (isotonic) and Platt; plus own reliability-curve check Per-prediction explanation CalculateFeatureContribution (supported for LightGBM/FastTree/linear models) Global importance Permutation Feature Importance (PFI) Metrics AUC, log-loss, Brier score via evaluation APIs + own walk-forward metrics Persistence Model .zip in the models volume + research.model versions row; champion/challenger = DB pointer Flow: features and triple-barrier labels are computed by the same .NET code used for scanning → a weekly Hangfire job trains, calibrates and validates per walk-forward fold → the model is saved and registered → PredictionEngine/batch transform scores candidates in-process during the nightly run. Why not Python: no second language/runtime, no extra container, no model-export hop, identical feature code for training and scoring. What we give up — exact SHAP values, Optuna-style tuning, notebook ecosystem — is not needed: feature contributions + PFI explain decisions, and the design deliberately avoids heavy tuning (few parameters, plateaus). Platform check (Phase 0 spike): containers on Apple Silicon are linux/arm64. Verify the LightGBM native library loads there (if not, build lib lightgbm.so from source in the worker image); fall back to the managed FastTree trainer otherwise. The model sits behind an IProbabilityModel interface, so the trainer can change without touching Planning or Learning."},{"d":2,"h":"2.5 AI","a":"25-ai","t":"Concern Choice Model server llama.cpp server (pinned RamaLama image) in a container with GPU (libkrun); OpenAI-compatible API; started on demand (ADR 0005) Model Qwen3-30B-A3B at 4-bit (alternate gpt-oss-20b), 16K default / 32K supported context; no separate small model Integration Assistant endpoint in xd-api (Microsoft.Extensions.AI) + xd-mcp read-only tool server Optional Hermes Agent as hardened front-end after PoC (doc 14)"},{"d":2,"h":"2.6 Infrastructure","a":"26-infrastructure","t":"Concern Choice Containers Podman Desktop , libkrun machine (GPU for LLM), compose.yaml with profiles Proxy Caddy — serves the React build, local TLS, routes /api, /hubs, /hangfire Secrets macOS Keychain → Podman secrets → .NET KeyPerFile configuration provider Logs Seq (free single-user) Backups restic → external SSD"},{"d":2,"h":"3. Modules (inside the .NET solution)","a":"3-modules-inside-the-net-solution","t":"Module Responsibility Market Bars, aggregation, context, calendar, corporate actions, data quality Data import CSV profiles, import jobs (preview, validate, upsert, undo), folder watcher Catalog Instrument master, tracked instruments, baskets (manual / rule / system), membership history Live Active-set computation, WebSocket subscriptions, live 1-minute bars, quote polling, live P&L & alerts Brokers Adapters (Dhan in v1; others later; NSE files) implementing IMarketDataProvider, ILiveFeed, IAccountReader, ITokenProvider, (later) IOrderExecutor Portfolio Transactions, FIFO lots, positions, buckets, valuations, returns, XIRR, benchmark, alerts Indicators & Features Streaming indicators, multi-timeframe features, regime labels Strategies Built-in strategies, DSL parser/compiler, plugin host Simulation Event-driven bar-by-bar engine: conditional entries, exits, pyramids, gaps, costs, slippage, MAE/MFE Learning Walk-forward runner, cell stats (Bayesian), exit selection, drift, lifecycle, hypothesis loop, ML.NET training & scoring, execution feedback, lessons, experiments (replay), autonomy rules Planning Scanner, scorer, selector, per-account allocator, pre-open check Trading Accounts, sync, journal, fill matching, outcomes grading, (later) execution Lab Strategy submissions, test runs, verdicts, test ledger Reporting Report queries with lenses (all / basket / instrument / trade / strategy / account), aggregates, exports Assistant LLM chat, tool routing, briefings Workflow Proposals, approvals (plan tickets, suggestions), review cutoff, suggestion inbox, pre-trade checklist Insights Portfolio action-center rules, price alerts, report auto-insights (doc 15) Data quality Validation runs (completeness, sanity, spikes, stored vs recomputed aggregates, derived vs official daily), repairs, quality score Scheduling Service schedules (DB-stored), dependency & timing rules, run-after chains, catch-up, failover Platform Config versions, auth (Identity, password re-entry), audit, notifications, commands, service runs Single code path: Simulation + Strategies + Features are used identically by the Strategy Lab, walk-forward learning, the nightly scanner and the outcome grader."},{"d":2,"h":"4. Repository & solution structure","a":"4-repository-solution-structure","t":"One repository (monorepo) holds the code, configuration, deployment and design material. xDrishti/ ├── backend/ .NET 10 solution │ ├── XDrishti.slnx solution (src/, tests/ folders) │ ├── global.json SDK pin + Microsoft Testing Platform │ ├── Directory.Build.props shared build settings, analyzers, nullable, warnings-as-errors │ ├── Directory.Packages.props central package versions │ ├── Dockerfile multi-stage: targets api, worker (chiseled, non-root) │ ├── contracts/XDrishti.Api.json OpenAPI contract generated at build (source of frontend types) │ ├── src/ │ │ ├── XDrishti.Domain/ entities & value objects: Bar, Instrument, Signal, TradeTicket, RMultiple, Cell… │ │ ├── XDrishti.Application/ use cases, interfaces (ports), pipeline orchestration │ │ ├── XDrishti.Infrastructure/ EF Core, Npgsql COPY, Timescale, secrets, LLM client, model store, default seed │ │ ├── XDrishti.Brokers.Dhan/ REST + WebSocket feed + instrument master │ │ ├── XDrishti.Brokers.Nse/ optional reconciliation files │ │ ├── XDrishti.DataImport/ CSV profiles, parsers, import jobs │ │ ├── XDrishti.Catalog/ instruments, tracked list, baskets │ │ ├── XDrishti.Portfolio/ lots, positions, valuations, XIRR │ │ ├── XDrishti.Indicators/ streaming indicators, TimeframeBuilder, FeatureFrame (pure; references Domain only; P3-01) │ │ ├── XDrishti.Strategies/ built-ins + DSL (parser, compiler, look-ahead lint) + plugin host │ │ ├── XDrishti.Simulation/ │ │ ├── XDrishti.Learning/ cells, meta-model, smart rules, experiments, lessons │ │ ├── XDrishti.Planning/ │ │ ├── XDrishti.Hosting/ shared host defaults: KeyPerFile secrets, Serilog, app info │ │ ├── XDrishti.Api/ host: endpoints, SignalR hubs, auth, OpenAPI; migrate command │ │ ├── XDrishti.Worker/ host: Hangfire server, scheduled services │ │ ├── XDrishti.Mcp/ host: MCP read-only tool server │ │ ├── XDrishti.Cli/ xd command-line tool │ │ ├── XDrishti.Feed/ host: market-hours live feed for the active set (later: depth recorder) │ │ └── XDrishti.Execution/ host (later): order service │ └── tests/ Domain/Application unit, Architecture (layer rules), Api.IntegrationTests (Testcontainers) ├── frontend/ React 19 + TypeScript + Vite + MUI; Dockerfile builds xd-proxy (Caddy + app) │ └── src/{app, features/<name , shared/{api,ui,lib}, theme, test} ├── data/ local runtime data, git-ignored: inbox/ (CSV drop, mounted into xd-worker), exports/, models/ ├── deploy/ compose.yaml (+ compose.dev.yaml), caddy/Caddyfile, db/init, xd-up.sh / xd-down.sh ├── scripts/ check.sh (all gates), gen-api.sh, dev-api.sh, build-docs.sh, serve.sh ├── docs/ │ ├── design/ these design documents (Markdown source of truth) │ ├── adr/ architecture decision records │ ├── engineering/ engineering standards (SOLID, modularity, testing, performance, DoD) │ ├── prototype/ clickable UX prototype (reference for the React app) │ └── site/ generated HTML version of docs/design └── tools/ docs-site builder, prototype vendoring Trading configuration and strategies are not repository folders : they live in the database (versioned, validated, audited) and are managed in the app; built-in defaults ship inside the backend and are seeded on first start (ADR 0002). Dependency rule: Domain ← Application ← (Infrastructure, Brokers. , engine modules) ← hosts. Engine modules (Indicators, Strategies, Simulation, Learning, Planning) have no I/O dependencies — they're pure and fully unit-testable."},{"d":2,"h":"4.1 XDrishti.Indicators as built (P3-01, ADR 0031)","a":"41-xdrishtiindicators-as-built-p3-01-adr-0031","t":"The first engine module. A pure library that turns a stream of closed 1-minute bars into indicator values, closed higher-timeframe bars and session context; back-tests and live trading use the same code. Part What it is IIndicator Update(in Bar), Value, IsReady, WarmUp, Reset(); constant (or amortised constant) work per bar, 0 bytes allocated in steady state. A value that is not ready is NaN with IsReady = false (the two always agree) IndicatorCatalog The 19 indicators with parameters (name, kind, min, max, default; at most 4), warm-up as a function of the spec (counted in bars of the spec's own timeframe), session behaviour (Continuous / PerSession), whether the source price is read, whether it is intraday-only, and a factory. The validator and lint of the DSL read it TimeframeBuilder Builds closed 5m, 10m, 15m, 30m, 1h and 1D bars with the rules of the stored aggregates (doc 05 §4); Push returns 0, 1 or 2 closed bars per minute; optional TradingCalendar; late bars are ignored and counted (Ignored) FeatureFrame Created once from a list of FeatureSpec (name, timeframe, price source, up to four parameters); validates them before any bar is fed, shares one instance between equal specs, builds only the needed builders, and exposes the value as of the last closed bar of each timeframe by a numeric handle. Counts the incomplete higher-timeframe bars it consumed (IncompleteBarsConsumed) References and rules. The project references XDrishti.Domain only (for Bar, Timeframe, SessionClock, TradingCalendar). Architecture tests keep it that way: no Application, Infrastructure, Hosting, EF Core, ASP.NET Core, Npgsql, Serilog, System.IO, System.Net, System.Data, System.Diagnostics, Environment, Random or TimeProvider; no DateTime.Now/UtcNow/Today (scanned in the compiled code); no mutable static field; every public class sealed or static. The only package outside the base class library that is involved is Skender.Stock.Indicators, and only in XDrishti.Indicators.Tests. Guarantees pinned by tests. Each indicator equals an independent batch reference over seeded random series; sma, ema, rsi and atr also agree with Skender (differences: the ATR seed, and an RSI over a series that never moves is 50 here and 100 in Skender); the prefix property (the output for the first k bars is the same when more bars are fed) over 220 seeds; the frame never shows the forming bar; two runs are bit-identical; Reset() returns every indicator to its first state. Throughput. The benchmarks are explicit tests, not part of scripts/check.sh (a solution run lists them as skipped). From backend/, in Release: dotnet test --project tests/XDrishti.Indicators.Tests -c Release -- --explicit only --filter-class \" ThroughputBenchmarks \" --report-trx --results-directory <dir On an Apple-silicon Mac (best of 5 passes of 1,000,000 steps, through IIndicator): sma(20) 104 M steps/s, ema(20) 359, rsi(14) 109, atr(14) 209, highest(20) 64, lowest(20) 55, vwap 172, rel volume(20) 199, or high(15) 208, gap pct 182; TimeframeBuilder 109 M bars/s (5m) to 251 M (1D); a 12-feature frame 22 M steps/s, that is 0.045 µs per 1-minute step (targets: 10 M and 1 µs)."},{"d":2,"h":"5. Data model (PostgreSQL schemas)","a":"5-data-model-postgresql-schemas","t":"Schema Key tables market instruments, instrument broker ids, master refreshes, bars 1m (hypertable, source csv/dhan), bars 1m live, bars 1d, continuous aggregates bars 5m…bars 1h, context daily, calendar, corporate actions, (later) ticks, depth catalog tracked instruments, baskets, basket members (valid from/to), index membership data import profiles, import jobs, import issues portfolio transactions, lots, lot closures, positions, buckets, cash flows, valuations daily, corporate action adjustments research sim runs, sim trades (with MAE/MFE, R, exit reason, feature snapshot), cell stats, exit choices, regimes, training sets, model versions, hypotheses, learning changelog, lessons, experiments, execution gap (per trade: plan vs fill), autonomy settings, smart rules, meta models, entry windows, loss tags, digests trading accounts, account roles, account snapshots, plans, plan items (primary/reserve/rejected), outcomes, journal entries, fills, (later) orders, order events lab strategies, strategy versions, lab runs, lab results, test ledger config config versions (YAML, hash, author, activated at), active config ops commands, service schedules, service runs, validation runs, validation results, data quality issues, audit log, notifications insights alerts, action items (status: open / snoozed / done), action thresholds workflow approvals, suggestions auth ASP.NET Core Identity tables (owner user, password hash, lockout) ai conversations, tool calls (audit) hangfire job storage Every decision-relevant row carries as of timestamps and the config version / model version used."},{"d":2,"h":"6. API surface (REST + SignalR)","a":"6-api-surface-rest-signalr","t":"Area Endpoints (examples) Instruments & baskets GET /api/instruments?q=&type=&sector= · POST /api/tracked · DELETE /api/tracked/{id} · GET/POST/PUT /api/baskets · POST /api/baskets/{id}/members · GET /api/baskets/{id}/preview (rule) Data import POST /api/imports (upload) · POST /api/imports/{id}/preview · POST /api/imports/{id}/commit · POST /api/imports/{id}/undo · GET/PUT /api/import-profiles Portfolio GET /api/portfolio?groupBy=&asOf= (groupBy: bucket, basket, account, strategy, sector) · GET /api/portfolio/positions/{id} · POST /api/portfolio/transactions (manual/CSV) · GET /api/portfolio/history?from=&to= Live GET /api/live/active-set · PUT /api/live/pins Plans GET /api/plans?date=&account= · GET /api/plans/{id} · POST /api/plans/{id}/preopen-check Journal GET /api/journal?from=&to= · PATCH /api/journal/{id} (taken/skipped/notes) Reports GET /api/reports/{type}?lens=&from=&to=&basket=&strategy=&tf=&side=&exit=&regime=&account= · GET /api/reports/{type}/export?format= Learning GET /api/cells · GET /api/cells/{key} · GET /api/changelog · POST /api/changelog/{id}/revert · GET/POST /api/hypotheses · GET /api/learning/journey · GET /api/lessons · GET /api/execution-gap · POST /api/experiments (replay job, SignalR progress) · POST /api/experiments/{id}/shadow · POST /api/experiments/{id}/apply · GET/PUT /api/autonomy · GET/PUT /api/smart-rules · GET /api/smart-rules/evidence · GET /api/digest/latest · GET /api/loss-tags Strategy Lab POST /api/lab/strategies · POST /api/lab/validate · POST /api/lab/runs · GET /api/lab/runs/{id} · POST /api/lab/runs/{id}/promote Config GET /api/config · POST /api/config/drafts · POST /api/config/drafts/{id}/validate · POST /api/config/drafts/{id}/activate Auth POST /api/auth/login · POST /api/auth/logout · POST /api/auth/reauth Accounts GET/POST/PUT /api/accounts · PUT /api/accounts/{id}/roles (step-up) · POST /api/accounts/{id}/test · POST /api/accounts/{id}/sync Approvals GET /api/plans/{date}/review · POST /api/plans/items/{id}/{approve, modify, reject} · POST /api/plans/{date}/approve-all Suggestions GET /api/suggestions?status= · POST /api/suggestions/{id}/accept · POST /api/suggestions/{id}/reject Services GET /api/services · PUT /api/services/{id}/schedule (validated against dependency rules) · POST /api/services/{id}/run · POST /api/services/{id}/pause · GET /api/services/{id}/runs Data quality GET /api/quality?status= · GET /api/quality/{symbol} (checks, stored vs recomputed) · POST /api/quality/run · POST /api/quality/{symbol}/repair Insights GET /api/portfolio/actions?account=&severity= · POST /api/portfolio/actions/{key}/{snooze, done} · GET/POST/DELETE /api/alerts · GET /api/reports/insights Risk profiles GET/POST/PUT/DELETE /api/risk-profiles (step-up) Data GET /api/data/quality · GET /api/data/coverage Assistant POST /api/assistant/chat (streaming) Real-time SignalR /hubs/events: live prices & P&L (active set), alerts, job/import progress, nightly status. Built in P2-10a (ADR 0024 §7, spec): the hub with server methods Subscribe(scope) / Unsubscribe and client events pricesChanged and feedStatus, plus GET /api/live/status (see below); alerts, job progress and nightly status use the same hub later"},{"d":2,"h":"7. Key flows","a":"7-key-flows","t":""},{"d":2,"h":"Daily timeline (trading day)","a":"daily-timeline-trading-day","t":"Time (IST) What runs 09:00–15:35 xd-feed: live data for the active set; quote polling for holdings; live P&L & alerts 09:10 Pre-open check arms approved tickets (after the 09:05 review cutoff) 15:50 EOD fetch: today's 1-minute + daily for all tracked instruments, gap backfill, aggregates refresh 16:30 Account sync: holdings, positions, trade history, ledger → portfolio & auto-journal; daily valuation 18:30 Nightly pipeline (below) → next-day plan"},{"d":2,"h":"Nightly pipeline (Hangfire recurring job, 18:30 IST)","a":"nightly-pipeline-hangfire-recurring-job-1830-ist","t":"Weekly: retrain/recalibrate (ML.NET job), exit re-selection, hypothesis loop. Monthly: full walk-forward review."},{"d":2,"h":"CSV import","a":"csv-import","t":"Upload or drop files → profile detection & preview in React → commit → Hangfire job parses (streaming), validates, maps symbols, bulk-COPYs into bars 1m, refreshes aggregates for the affected range → import report via SignalR → instruments auto-tracked. Details: 05-data-and-brokers.md §3."},{"d":2,"h":"Live active set","a":"live-active-set","t":"Planning (armed tickets) + Portfolio (open positions, holdings) + context indices + pins → xd-feed diffs the set and (un)subscribes on the Dhan WebSocket → ticks → live 1-minute bars + LTP → Portfolio/alerts → SignalR to React. Built in P2-10 (ADR 0024): xd-feed → ticks → last price per instrument (every 2 s) into the holdings, market.live quotes and the price alerts → NOTIFY live prices → (P2-10a) xd-api reads what changed and pushes it over SignalR. The feed and the API never call each other; the database is the only meeting point. Live 1-minute bars are not built yet. Built in P2-10a - the hub /hubs/events in xd-api. Owner session cookie only (the same session middleware as the REST API, including the 30-minute idle timeout; a connection without one is refused with 401, and a connection is closed within 30 seconds after its session ends by logout, idle timeout or the 12-hour limit; an open connection does not keep a session alive, the screen's own requests do), same origin only (a request with another Origin is refused with 403; no CORS, no wildcard), WebSockets only, no anonymous method. A browser connects with skipNegotiation and the WebSockets transport (otherwise the negotiate POST needs the X-CSRF-Token header like every POST). Server methods: Subscribe(scope) where scope = {accountId?, bucketId?, holdingIds?} (the screen's scope with the meaning it has for GET /api/holdings/summary, plus the holdings shown; the server turns them into instruments, at most 1,000 instruments and 1,000 holding ids per connection) answers {subscribed, truncated, failed}; Unsubscribe(). A second Subscribe replaces the first. Client events: pricesChanged {asOf, instruments:[{instrumentId, lastPrice, priceAt, source}]} - first the current price of every followed instrument, then only changes; at most one burst a second per connection, at most 500 instruments and 32 KB per message (larger changes come as several messages); source is live, quote or sync. feedStatus {state, subscribed, lastTickAt} - on connect, on a change of state or subscribed (looked at every 5 seconds) and at least every 15 seconds, from the xd-feed heartbeat; state is the feed's own word or not running. GET /api/live/status returns the same object. How it works: a hosted service in xd-api listens on NOTIFY live prices (its own database connection, reopened by itself with a growing wait of 1 to 30 seconds after a database restart; a notification lost meanwhile is made up by one read when listening resumes), reads the current price of the instruments that connections follow (the holdings' price columns and market.live quotes) and sends each connection what differs from what it was last sent; a read also happens every 30 seconds without a notification, because quote and sync prices are written without one. xd-api runs and stays healthy without the feed (the status then says not running). The types of the hub are generated from the C contract (XDrishti.Application/Live/Push) by scripts/gen-api.sh into frontend/src/shared/realtime/hubContract.generated.ts. Caddy proxies /hubs/ to xd-api (WebSocket upgrade is automatic in reverse proxy)."},{"d":2,"h":"Strategy Lab run","a":"strategy-lab-run","t":"React editor → POST /api/lab/runs → Hangfire job in xd-worker (plugins in xd-sandbox) → progress over SignalR → results in lab schema → report page. Details: 11-strategy-lab.md."},{"d":2,"h":"8. Security","a":"8-security","t":"- Login required everywhere : single owner, ASP.NET Core Identity (password), idle timeout, lockout, password re-entry for sensitive actions for sensitive actions; HTTPS via Caddy on LAN; optional WireGuard for remote access (doc 04 §2). - Service identity : background services act on persisted desired state, not on user sessions; every action records the configuring actor for audit. - Secrets per container via Podman secrets; only xd-worker/xd-feed (data/account tokens) and later xd-exec (order tokens) receive broker secrets. - xd-mcp and the LLM use a read-only database role ; no tool can change config, plans or orders. - xd-sandbox has no network and a read-only data mount. - Audit log for config activations, promotions, approvals and (later) orders."},{"d":3,"h":"04 — Accounts, Access & Operating Model","a":"","t":"Four rules shape how xDrishti is used day to day: 1. Personal use only — one owner, only the owner's broker accounts. 2. Login required — nobody else can open or use the application. 3. Configure once, services run it — what you set up is executed by background services on the machine, not by your browser session. 4. Human in the loop — the system and the AI propose ; you confirm the decisions that matter."},{"d":3,"h":"1. Broker accounts and their roles","a":"1-broker-accounts-and-their-roles","t":"You can register any number of broker accounts. Each account is given one or more roles : Role What the account is used for Requirements Data Market data: instrument master, historical & end-of-day 1-minute data, live feed for the active set, quotes Dhan Data API subscription on this account Trading Receives next-day plan tickets (sized to its capital and risk profile); auto-journal of fills; later order execution Risk profile, capital source, allowed styles/instruments Portfolio Holdings (and later positions, trades, ledger) are imported with acquisition dates, synced by hand or automatically and monitored (ADR 0012) Connected account; optional refresh schedule; gets its own default bucket automatically One account can hold several roles at once, for example: Account Data Trading Portfolio Notes : : : : : : Dhan · Main ✅ primary ✅ ✅ Pays the ₹499 Data API; trades and is monitored Dhan · Long-term — — ✅ Monitoring only (holdings, XIRR) Dhan · Swing ✅ standby ✅ ✅ Can take over data if the main account's token fails Rules enforced by the system - Exactly one primary Data account ; optionally one standby that takes over automatically if the primary's token or subscription fails (only if it also has an active Data API subscription). - A Trading account must have a risk profile; plan allocation only goes to accounts with the Trading role. - Portfolio views include only accounts with the Portfolio role (filterable per account). - Removing a role stops the related services for that account; history is kept. - Every role change is audited and needs re-authentication (password). Per-account settings: display name, broker (Dhan only, ADR 0009), Dhan client id (shown masked: first 4 + last 2), the Dhan API key and secret (write-only, encrypted — see below), the day the API key was generated, roles, mode (paper / manual live; automated modes in a later phase), risk profile, capital source, sync schedule, token status & last successful sync. Dhan accounts (ADR 0008, narrowed by ADR 0009): Dhan is the only broker and the documented API Key & Secret consent flow the only way to connect. Adding an account asks for what Dhan needs; the setup steps and the redirect URL are shown in the wizard and on the Connection tab (Dhan facts are constants in the UI): Dhan Identity Client id — required (e.g. 1000000001) Secrets (write-only) API key (app id), API secret (app secret) — valid 12 months ; the owner gives the day it was generated (default today, India time); warning 30 days before expiry Setup steps shown web.dhan.co → My Profile → Access DhanHQ APIs → switch on API key → app name, Redirect URL (copy button), Postback optional Notes shown A static IP is needed only for placing orders (not for data/portfolio); 25 consents a day The redirect URL is <Brokers PublicBaseUrl /api/brokers/dhan/callback (default base http://localhost:8080); the UI reads it from GET /api/brokers/dhan → {redirectUrl}. Another broker later means a new decision (the broker column and the connector port stay). Connecting an account (P1-16c, P1-16d, ADR 0008, 0009) follows Dhan's documented flow (details in doc 05 §8): 1. Connect — POST /api/accounts/{id}/connect (step-up) → {authorizeUrl, expiresAt}. The API asks Dhan for a consent (generate-consent, at most 25 per account and India day ; the 26th answers 429 accounts.consent limit with retryAfterSeconds until midnight IST) and returns Dhan's login page. A pending sign-in (accounts.pending connects) is stored: bound to the session, valid 10 minutes , usable once ; a newer sign-in of the same session closes an older one. 2. The browser opens Dhan's login page (consentApp-login) in the same tab; the owner signs in there with Dhan's own 2FA. 3. Callback — Dhan redirects to GET /api/brokers/dhan/callback?tokenId=…. The callback needs the signed-in session (the session cookie is SameSite=Lax so it comes along with this top-level redirect) and the session's open pending sign-in (Dhan sends no state), uses it up at once, exchanges "},{"d":3,"h":"1.1 Accounts screen","a":"11-accounts-screen","t":"View Content Accounts (list + detail) Left: every account with roles, token health, holdings value. Right: tabs Overview (value, P&L, funds, token, sync) · Roles · Trading (mode, risk profile, capital source/amount, styles, allowed instruments per style, per-account overrides of risk %, max entries, max open, daily loss) · Portfolio (include in totals; auto refresh: Off / Daily at HH:MM IST, and the First import block: the first import date (default 1 Jan 2025), a calendar that stays editable — saving imports all holdings Dhan lists with that date the first time, and later re-dates the lots still on the default date; per-holding dates are edited on the Portfolio screen; no bucket choice here — imports go to the account's own bucket, ADR 0015. Later changes arrive with the refresh; the Portfolio screen only has Sync now ) · Data (primary/standby, subscription & renewal, live-feed budget, failover test) · Connection (client id masked, name, colour, notes; secret status — set / last changed, Dhan key expiry — with Replace (write-only dialog, step-up); Dhan's setup steps and redirect URL; connection status chip and expiry with Connect / Reconnect and Disconnect ) · Activity (audit for this account) Add account wizard 1 Dhan app (client id, API key and secret — masked, never pre-filled —, day the key was generated, setup steps, redirect URL with copy button) → 2 Identity → 3 Roles → 4 Role settings → 5 Review & create (secrets shown as \"•••• set\"; confirm with your password; Create and connect starts the Dhan login) Header accounts menu Every account with colour dot, name and status chip (expiry when connected), and Connect / Reconnect for each account that is not connected (P1-16d) The role matrix (all accounts × roles; tick to change, step-up; pick the primary Data account) and the shared risk profiles (Standard, Conservative, …: risk per trade, max new entries/day, max open positions, daily loss, max per sector, max heat; which accounts use each) live under Settings (/settings, tabs Risk profiles · Account roles ), not on the Accounts page. Automatic failover: if the primary Data account's token or subscription fails, the services switch to the standby Data account, notify you and record it in the audit log."},{"d":3,"h":"2. Access control (login)","a":"2-access-control-login","t":"Control Design Login E-mail + password on a centred sign-in panel; a locked session shows Welcome back · Unlock (password only, survives reload); single fixed owner account, no sign-up : provisioned by xd-migrate from the Keychain items xdrishti/auth/owner-email\\ owner-name\\ owner-password (created with scripts/auth-secrets.sh) if none exists. Wrong e-mail and wrong password give the same 401 auth.invalid credentials. No second factor: the app is personal and reachable only on this machine / home LAN (a passkey can be added later if remote access is ever enabled) Password storage PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512, ≥ 210,000 iterations, random salt (parameters stored with the hash; upgraded at the next login); never stored or logged in plain text. Changed in the app (My account, below) or with scripts/auth-secrets.sh + deploy/xd-up.sh --owner-reset (the reset also signs out every session) Sessions Server-side, revocable, stored hashed in auth.sessions; opaque cookie xd.sid (HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax — Strict until ADR 0008: Dhan's sign-in redirect is a top-level cross-site GET that must carry it —, Secure on HTTPS); idle timeout 30 min (sliding, last-seen written ≤ once a minute); absolute timeout 12 h; CSRF header X-CSRF-Token on every POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE; sign-out revokes the session; \"lock\" button (later) Lockout 5 consecutive failures → 15-minute lock (423 auth.locked + retryAfterSeconds; a correct password is refused during the lock); login route also limited to 10 attempts/minute per client address (429); every sign-in event (success, failure, lock, logout, idle/absolute expiry, owner provisioning/reset) in auth.audit events, readable via GET /api/auth/audit Step-up auth Password re-entry for sensitive actions: account roles, broker secrets and connection, trading risk, risk profiles, removing accounts, config activation, enabling execution, deleting data. POST /api/auth/step-up {password} elevates the current session for 5 minutes (auth.sessions.elevated until; ends at logout/expiry), endpoints answer 403 auth.step up required without it; wrong passwords count toward the same lockout as sign-in (StepUpSucceeded / StepUpFailed in the audit trail) My account (P2-11) The owner changes their own details in the app at /account (user menu → My account , also in the ⌘K palette). GET /api/auth/account → {email, displayName, createdAt, passwordChangedAt}. POST /api/auth/change-password {currentPassword, newPassword} verifies the current password (a wrong one counts toward the same lockout as sign-in and step-up, 423 when locked), requires 10–1024 characters different from the current one (400 auth.password invalid / auth.password unchanged, field message on newPassword), stores a new PBKDF2 hash, signs out every other session and keeps the current one (PasswordChanged in the audit trail). PUT /api/auth/profile {displayName, email} needs step-up (403 auth.step up required without it), validates name (≤ 128) and e-mail (an @, no whitespace or control characters, ≤ 256, stored lower-cased), writes ProfileUpdated (changed field names only) and leaves other sessions valid; saving unchanged values writes nothing. The page also lists the last 10 sign-in events (GET /api/auth/audit?take=10). Passwords are never logged, audited or kept after the request Recovery No recovery codes: the owner resets the password from the Keychain (xd-up.sh --owner-reset); there is no e-mail reset. A password or e-mail changed in the app is not written back to the Keychain: --owner-reset re-reads the Keychain items and overwrites the in-app values, so update them with scripts/auth-secrets.sh after a change if a reset may be needed later Every endpoint protected Closed by default (fallback authorization policy): REST, OpenAPI/API docs, SignalR, Hangfire dashboard and file downloads require a session; only /health/ , POST /api/auth/login and GET /api/auth/session are anonymous. The Dhan callback GET /api/brokers/dhan/callback passes routing without a session but check it them"},{"d":3,"h":"3. Operating model — configure once, services run it","a":"3-operating-model-configure-once-services-run-it","t":"The UI is a control plane : it records what you want. Background services are the execution plane : they do the work on the machine, whether or not you are logged in or the browser is open. - Every action you take is stored as desired state (e.g. \"INFY is tracked\", \"basket MyChoice contains …\", \"ticket T-123 approved\") plus an audit record (who, when, what changed). - Services run under a service identity , not your session. Logging out, closing the browser or the phone has no effect on scheduled work. - Services reconcile desired vs actual state: on every run and on start-up. If the Mac was asleep at 15:50, the end-of-day fetch runs as soon as the services are back (catch-up for missed schedules). - Jobs are idempotent, retried with back-off and resumable; failures raise in-app notifications. You do this (once) Services then do this (automatically) Track an instrument Backfill history (CSV, then Dhan), add to end-of-day fetch, build all timeframes Create a basket / add instruments Include in the next scan scope, reports and portfolio grouping Assign the Data role to an account Switch market-data jobs and the live feed to that account Assign the Portfolio role Nightly sync of holdings, trades, ledger; valuations; XIRR Assign the Trading role Allocate and size plan tickets for that account Approve tomorrow's tickets Pre-open check, arm tickets, subscribe live feed, trigger/stop/target alerts Drop CSV files in the inbox Import, validate, aggregate, report Activate a config version Apply from the next scheduled run Accept an AI suggestion Apply the change (e.g. add instrument to basket) and log it Services overview (default schedules — all editable on the Services screen): Service / job Default schedule Runs as Live feed (active set) Window 09:00–15:35, trading days; built as the service live-feed (P2-10, ADR 0024): xd-feed supervises its own window and writes its own run rows, the Services screen lists, edits, pauses and resumes it through the stored row (the feed re-reads it every 15 s), Run now opens the connection for Live:ManualRunMinutes (30) outside the window, and its status comes from the feed's heartbeat xd-feed Holdings quote refresh Every 5 min, 09:15–15:30 on trading days; built as the service holdings-quotes (P2-06, ADR 0019; the first interval service: retries 0, timeout 2 min, no catch-up, overlapping ticks dropped); since P2-16 (ADR 0027) a poll that fails because Dhan did not answer or limited the rate is recorded as skipped (the next 5-minute poll is its own retry) and fails the run — which the bell's failed-service notice shows — only after failing on two days in a row; a refused token fails it at once xd-api today (xd-feed later) Pre-open check & arming 09:10 (after the 09:05 review cutoff and NSE pre-open price discovery, before the 09:15 open) xd-worker Price alerts & action center Every 1 min, 09:15–15:30; price alerts are built (P2-09, ADR 0022) and, since P2-10 (ADR 0024), checked on every live flush (every 2 s while the feed is connected) as well as on the 5-minute quote tick; the Action center is evaluated on read xd-feed / xd-api End-of-day fetch & aggregation (with gap backfill) 15:50 on trading days of the calendar, never before the session has ended (doc 05 §6; built as the service eod (P1-13): the job dhan → aggregate → quality, trigger daily) xd-api today (xd-worker later) Data validation Runs after the EOD fetch and aggregation, as the last step of the same data job (doc 05 §10b; built in P1-14a: dhan → aggregate → quality, trigger daily; on demand from the Data quality screen; Data:Quality: ) xd-worker Account & portfolio sync 16:30 (designed); built per account, once a day at the time set under Accounts (ADR 0013) and shown on the Services screen as a read-only row with a link; since P2-16 a refresh that failed for a reason that may pass is retried quietly by the holdings refresh runner (1, 5 and 15 minutes, inside the day) and a renewed Dhan login makes the missed syncs run by themselves (doc"},{"d":3,"h":"3.1 Schedule configuration","a":"31-schedule-configuration","t":"Built (P1-13, spec, ADR 0018): the framework, the two first services and the API of the Services screen. - A service is a registered definition (id, name, runsAs, allowed schedule types, default schedule and execution settings, parameter schema with bounds, constraints MustFollow / MustNotOverlap / MustCover, a start-time advice) plus one row of the owner's choices in platform.service schedules (enabled, schedule, retries, timeout, catch-up, notify on failure, parameters, xmin as revision, who and when). Without a row a service runs on its first-start settings. The two services built so far are eod (End-of-day data run) and instrument-master (Instrument master refresh); the others of the table above register themselves when they are built, and their checks apply from then on. - One scheduler (ServiceScheduler, a hosted service in xd-api) looks at the rows at least once a minute and at the next start, so an edit counts at the next look without a restart. A service runs at most once at a time (a start while it runs is recorded as a skipped run). Every attempt is a row of platform.service runs (trigger scheduled catch up after manual); a failed attempt is retried up to retries times after 30 s, 2 min, 10 min, 30 min, 1 h (a sequence the previous process left in its back-off goes on after a restart); an attempt past the timeout is cancelled and recorded as failed (timed out; a job that cannot be cancelled and is still running is said so; a timeout is never retried); a run that succeeds starts the services set to run after it. At start-up the runs the previous process left running are closed as failed (interrupted) first, then each enabled service with catch-up on whose latest scheduled time passed without a run since is run once (catch up: the same day for a daily job, 24 hours for a weekly one, any age for the instrument master); a paused service never catches up, not even when resumed. The same rule applies when the machine slept through a time. Pause stops scheduled, catch-up and run-after starts; Run now (trigger manual) still works. - First-start settings. Data:Eod:Enabled / Data:Eod:TimeIst and Instruments:AutoRefresh:Enabled / TimeIst are read once , when a service has no row, and stored as its row; after that the Services screen is the only place to change them and the configuration is ignored (a restart keeps the edit). Until the row exists the screen shows the configured values. - Checks (ScheduleRules, pure, over the whole set; the same rules answer the dry run): errors block saving — a type the service does not allow, an unreadable time, no day chosen, a window whose start is not before its end, an interval outside 1–120 minutes or longer than its window, a run-after chain that is circular or points at an always-on or unknown service, retries outside 0–5, a timeout outside 1–240 minutes, an unknown parameter or one out of its bounds; warnings are shown and allowed — a start before or after the advised time (the end-of-day fetch before 15:35), a window that does not cover what the definition requires, a run during market hours that must not, a service that should follow another and does not, a chain of more than three, two services at the same minute. A finding is reported on the service it is about, so moving the end-of-day time after a dependent service's own time warns on the dependent. - API (/api/services, session + CSRF, no step-up, every change audited as ServiceScheduleChanged ServicePaused ServiceResumed ServiceRunRequested in the account events): list, one service, runs (newest first, the latest 200), timeline of a date, notices for the bell (service failed, derived on read), save (optimistic revision, 409 services.revision changed), preview (issues + next three runs), restore default, pause, resume, run now (202, 409 services.already running). services.invalid carries issues[]; services.read only for the per-account sync row. - Not in this slice: the xd-worker container (the scheduler runs in xd-api; moving i"},{"d":3,"h":"4. Human in the loop","a":"4-human-in-the-loop","t":""},{"d":3,"h":"4.1 Who decides what","a":"41-who-decides-what","t":"Category Examples Decision Automatic (no approval) Data fetch, aggregation, quality checks, grading, cell statistics, portfolio sync, valuations Services Proposed → you confirm Next-day trade tickets · AI instrument suggestions (track, add to / remove from basket) · hypothesis promotions · cell promotions to Active · config change proposals · (later) semi-auto orders You Only you Accounts & roles, credentials, baskets you create, risk limits, enabling execution You"},{"d":3,"h":"4.2 Next-day plan approval","a":"42-next-day-plan-approval","t":"- The nightly pipeline produces a proposed plan (primary tickets + reserve list) with reasons, P(win), expected R and the AI briefing. You review it in the evening or morning (phone works via the PWA). - Actions per ticket: Approve , Modify (reduce size, tighten stop, change account, choose reserve instead), Reject . Bulk \"approve all within limits\" is available. Every modification is re-validated against risk limits. - Review cutoff (default 09:05): unreviewed tickets lapse (safe default — unreviewed policy: lapse). - Only Approved → Armed tickets enter the live active set and alerts. - Every decision is recorded. Reports compare system proposal vs your decision — e.g. \"rejected tickets would have made +4.2R this month\" — so you can see whether your overrides add value."},{"d":3,"h":"4.3 AI suggestions inbox","a":"43-ai-suggestions-inbox","t":"The assistant and learning engine post suggestions to an inbox ; each has a reason, evidence and an expiry: Suggestion Example If accepted Track instrument \"Track PERSISTENT — strong RS, 3 passing setups in Best stocks rules\" Instrument tracked & backfilled Add to / remove from basket \"Add TATAPOWER to Best stocks (rule match, liquidity ok)\" Membership updated (dated) Next-day focus \"Prefer swing over intraday tomorrow: high-VIX regime\" Shown with the plan (no automatic effect) Hypothesis / promotion \"ORB 15m BUY: target 2.0R → 2.5R passed walk-forward + shadow\" Config version created for activation Rejected suggestions are not repeated for a configurable number of days."},{"d":3,"h":"5. Data model additions","a":"5-data-model-additions","t":"Table Purpose trading.accounts Broker accounts (no secrets) trading.account roles account id, role (data/trading/portfolio), is primary/standby, enabled, settings auth. ASP.NET Core Identity tables (user, password hash, lockout) ops.commands Desired-state changes and requested actions, status, actor, timestamps ops.audit log Every login, configuration change, approval and service action workflow.approvals Item type, item id, decision, modifications, actor, decided at, cutoff workflow.suggestions Source (AI / engine), type, payload, reason, expiry, status ops.service runs Service/job runs with schedule, start/end, outcome, catch-up flag"},{"d":4,"h":"1. Data sources (v1)","a":"1-data-sources-v1","t":"Source Role Cost Your CSV files (1-minute) Historical bootstrap and any bulk history you provide ₹0 Dhan Data API The only market-data API in v1: instrument master, end-of-day 1-minute/daily fetch for tracked instruments, live feed for the active set, quotes for holdings ₹499 + taxes / 30 days, on one account Dhan Trading API Funds, positions, holdings, trade history, ledger — every Dhan account Free NSE files (optional) Free official bhavcopy / corporate actions / holidays for reconciliation ₹0 Corporate actions (splits, bonus) Not from a feed: detected in the stored day bars and confirmed by you , typed in, or imported from CSV text (§4.2). Bars stay raw; adjusted reads apply them ₹0"},{"d":4,"h":"2. Three data tiers","a":"2-three-data-tiers","t":"- Tier A fills history once (and whenever you add instruments or files). - Tier B keeps every tracked instrument complete each evening — the learning engine and nightly plan use only Tier A/B data. - Tier C streams live data only for what you are actively trading or holding , keeping the system light."},{"d":4,"h":"3. CSV import (1-minute data)","a":"3-csv-import-1-minute-data","t":"Built so far (P1-17a, ADR 0016): one fixed profile instead of the configurable ones below — the folder Data:CsvFolder (mounted read-only at /data/csv) with files <SYMBOL 1m.csv[.gz] whose header columns time (epoch seconds, UTC, bar start), open, high, low, close, volume are found by name (others ignored). Each file is streamed (never held in memory) through the row rules, written with binary COPY into a staging table and merged in one transaction; a file already loaded and unchanged (name, size, modified time in market.csv files) is skipped unless forced. Rules: an inconsistent high/low (the opening bar when the open is the pre-open price) is repaired (high = max, low = min of open/high/low/close) and counted; unparseable rows, prices not above zero, negative volume, a minute twice (the first is kept) and a time that is not a whole minute are rejected and counted with the first ten reasons; zero volume and special sessions (Muhurat) are kept. CSV never overwrites Dhan; Dhan overwrites CSV. <SYMBOL ↦ NSE:<SYMBOL (shares and ETFs that are not retired); unknown or ambiguous symbols are listed and skipped. Profiles and the watched folder stay with P1-07/P1-08; the review step and the undo are built (below). A file is refused as a whole, before anything of it is merged (nothing stored, the file is not recorded as loaded, the reason is in the job's failedFiles) when - its clock is wrong : most bars lie outside the regular session (India time) but nearly all (≥ 99 %) fit a session after adding or subtracting 5 h 30 min (the message names the shift), or most lie outside every session, or the bars are stamped with the end of each minute (15:30 present, 09:15 absent) — the streaming counterpart of the window check of the Dhan fetch (§6); a few stray bars, the 60-bar Muhurat evening session and Saturday special sessions are legitimate and never trigger it (verified on the 214 real files: 41 M rows, none refused); - most of its times are not epoch seconds between 2000 and 2100 (milliseconds are named as such) or are later than one day from now ; a few such rows are only rejected and counted (timeOutOfRange, timeInFuture); - it exceeds the limits that bound the work per file : Data:Csv:MaxRowsPerFile (default 5 000 000 rows; the real files have ≈ 200 000), Data:Csv:MaxBytesPerFile (default 1 GiB read after decompression, so a gzip bomb stops), Data:Csv:MaxSpanDays (default 3 700 days ≈ 120 monthly chunks of the hypertable). Tracking after a load. After the files, the instruments that have bars from CSV files — loaded in this run or in an earlier one , so a tracking that failed is tried again by the next run even for unchanged files — are tracked, but only those that have never had a tracking row (active or not). An instrument the owner untracked is never tracked again behind their back, by a changed file or a forced load. Review step and undo (P1-07a, ADR 0017 §6). - GET /api/data/csv/files lists the folder from its directory entries and the log of loaded files only (no file is opened, so 214 files list at once): per file state — pending (never loaded, or its load was undone) · loaded (size and modified time are what they were when it was loaded) · changed (loaded, but size or modified time differ now) —, size, modified time, the symbol parsed from the name, the instrument it maps to or mapping unknown · ambiguous (several instruments, or several files, claim the symbol) · invalidName (a .csv/.csv.gz that is not <SYMBOL 1m.csv[.gz]; other extensions are not listed), and for loaded files rows, firstBar, lastBar (null for a file loaded before the range was recorded), loadedAt and overlapsWith (the names of the other loaded files of the same instrument whose first-to-last range intersects this one's — a file renamed after its load is loaded twice; a file without a recorded range counts as covering everything; null for pending files). Changed and pending files first, then loaded, each by name; q, state, page, pageSize (≤ 200); counts by state"},{"d":4,"h":"3.1 How files arrive","a":"31-how-files-arrive","t":"- Upload in the Data → Import screen (multiple files / zip), or drop into the watched folder data/inbox/ (mounted into xd-worker), or CLI xd data import <path --profile <name . - One file per instrument (symbol from file name) or multi-instrument files (symbol column) — both supported."},{"d":4,"h":"3.2 Import profiles (column mapping)","a":"32-import-profiles-column-mapping","t":"Different CSV sources use different layouts; a saved profile describes yours once: csv profiles: my 1min files: delimiter: \",\" header: true symbol: { from: filename, pattern: \"^(?<symbol [A-Z0-9&-]+) 1min\\\\.csv$\" } or { from: column, name: symbol } exchange: NSE columns: { timestamp: datetime, open: open, high: high, low: low, close: close, volume: volume, oi: null } timestamp: { format: \"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\", timezone: Asia/Kolkata, label: bar start } bar start bar end prices: { adjusted: false } raw prices; system applies corporate-action adjustments session filter: { from: \"09:15\", to: \"15:29\" } Example accepted file (TATASTEEL 1min.csv): datetime,open,high,low,close,volume 2026-10-05 09:15:00,152.10,152.60,151.95,152.40,412530 2026-10-05 09:16:00,152.40,152.55,152.20,152.30,198220"},{"d":4,"h":"3.3 Import pipeline","a":"33-import-pipeline","t":"1. Detect profile (or use the one specified); preview the first rows in the UI before committing. 2. Map symbol → master list; unknown symbols are listed for you to map or skip (never guessed). 3. Validate each row: timestamp parse, within session, OHLC consistency (low ≤ open/close ≤ high), no negative volume, no duplicate timestamps; bar-end labels shifted to bar-start. 4. Upsert into market.bars 1m with source = csv. Precedence on overlap (configurable): Dhan official CSV live; conflicts beyond tolerance are logged, not silently overwritten. 5. Refresh aggregates for the affected instrument/date range. 6. Report : rows imported/updated/rejected per file, coverage before/after, quality issues — stored as an import job you can review and undo. Built (P1-07a): the job's report per file, the preview before the load and the undo per file (see the notes above); undo by job id is not built. Instruments in the files are auto-added to the tracked list (configurable), so they are kept up to date by Tier B afterwards."},{"d":4,"h":"4. Aggregation to other timeframes","a":"4-aggregation-to-other-timeframes","t":"All timeframes are built from 1-minute bars — identical logic for CSV and Dhan data. Rule Detail OHLCV open = first, high = max, low = min, close = last, volume = sum, OI = last Alignment Intraday buckets anchored to the 09:15 session open: 15m = 09:15, 09:30…; 1h = 09:15–10:15 … 14:15–15:15, then 15:15–15:30 (partial, flagged) Timeframes 5m, 10m, 15m, 30m, 1h (and any custom minute size) · 1D · 1W · 1M Daily bar daily source: official (Dhan daily candles, default when available) or derived (from 1-minute) — official open uses the pre-open auction price, so the two can differ slightly Missing minutes Bucket built from available minutes; bars (the number of 1-minute bars in it) and a complete flag are stored (what complete means: see below) Special sessions Holidays skipped; special sessions (e.g. Muhurat, a Saturday session) are kept as they come, and the trading calendar (below) names them: a special session has its own window, a 1D bar of a Muhurat evening is complete when every minute between its first and last bar is there Implementation In the application, not continuous aggregates (ADR 0016): set-based SQL upserts (date bin with the 09:15 origin) run by the aggregate phase of the data job, for the ranges whose 1-minute bars changed since the last build (or everything on request); real-time aggregation combines materialised + newest rows (P2) Why not continuous aggregates. The runtime role xd app has plain DML only and may not refresh or own a continuous aggregate; a refresh policy would need a second privileged path, and a continuous aggregate cannot express the rules above (09:15 anchoring with a partial 15:15 hour, Monday weeks, a completeness flag, a day that is the India date, a special evening session) as plainly as a query we can test. So the application builds market.bars agg itself with idempotent upserts (a bucket that comes out the same is not written), per instrument and changed range, as xd app, and a test recomputes every bucket with an independent implementation over fixtures (a Muhurat evening, month and week boundaries, gaps) and compares them exactly. Bucket rules as built (all from the stored 1-minute bars; times are India time, UTC+05:30, no daylight saving): Timeframe Bucket start (ts) bars complete 5m, 10m, 15m, 30m 09:15 + k × N minutes of the same India date (09:15, 09:30 … for 15m) 1-minute bars in the bucket exactly the minutes of the bucket's window, clipped at the 15:30 close for buckets that start before it (the 10m bucket at 15:25 and the 30m bucket at 15:15 hold 5 and 15) 1h 09:15, 10:15 … 14:15, then 15:15 (the partial 15:15–15:30 hour holds 15 bars) as above as above 1D India midnight of the India date (a Muhurat evening belongs to its own date) 1-minute bars of the date all 375 minutes of 09:15–15:29 are there; for a date without a single regular-session minute (an evening session) every minute between its first and last bar is 1W India midnight of the Monday of the week sum of its days every day with bars is complete 1M India midnight of the first of the month sum of its days every day with bars is complete The streaming TimeframeBuilder of XDrishti.Indicators (P3-01, ADR 0031) reproduces these rules for 5m, 10m, 15m, 30m, 1h and 1D (not 1W or 1M) on a live stream of 1-minute bars and is tested against an independent grouping of the same fixtures; it emits a bucket when its last minute has arrived, or — flagged complete = false — when a later bucket or session proves the minute will not come. Where it can differ from the stored aggregates: (a) a regular date's 1D bar is closed by the 15:29 bar and any later bar of the same date (an evening bar) is ignored and counted, while the stored 1D merges every bar of the date; (b) a calendar entry that disagrees with the data (a session shorter than the bars) closes the day at the calendar's end; (c) the last bucket of a stream whose final minute never arrives is not emitted (there is no flush), and a special session without a calendar closes its "},{"d":4,"h":"4.1 Trading calendar (ADR 0017 §1, P1-11a)","a":"41-trading-calendar-adr-0017-1-p1-11a","t":"What a session is comes from one place, market.trading days (one row per date that is not an ordinary weekday session; nothing is seeded), read through the pure domain type TradingCalendar and, for any slice, the application port ITradingCalendar (the confirmed days are read once per request or job and kept as an in-memory snapshot; changing the calendar invalidates it). Column Meaning date (PK) the India date kind holiday (a weekday without a session) · special (a session of its own on any date: a Saturday session, the Muhurat evening) session from, session to India time, to exclusive; only for special (a holiday has none) source manual (the owner) · observed (derived from the stored bars) · nse (NSE's public holiday list) status confirmed (counts) · proposed (waits for the owner) · dismissed (declined; kept so it is not proposed or listed again) note, updated at free text up to 200 characters, last change proposal status, proposed kind, proposed from, proposed to, proposed note a proposal next to a confirmed row (P1-11a review): what the stored bars show when it contradicts a confirmed day, e.g. a session on an NSE-listed holiday. proposal status proposed (waits) · dismissed (declined; remembered so it is not proposed again) · null. The confirmed row keeps deciding what a session is; the proposal changes nothing until the owner confirms it xmin PostgreSQL's row version, mapped as the optimistic-concurrency token: a save based on a row someone changed meanwhile is refused, never overwriting or deleting the newer row TradingCalendar answers IsTradingDay(date) (a weekday that is not a confirmed holiday, or a confirmed special session), SessionOf(date) (09:15–15:30 India time, or the special window), ExpectedMinutes(date) (375, the window's length, 0 when closed), LastClosedSession(now) (today once its session is over — 15:29:59 is still before the close, 15:30:00 is after it, a Muhurat day closes at its own end —, otherwise the trading day before) and TradingDaysBetween(after, upTo) (trading days after the first date up to and including the second). Only confirmed days count; a proposal changes nothing until the owner confirms it. \"Behind\" is the last bar's date against the last closed session, counted in trading days — the Data screen's status ribbon, the overview's expectedSession/daysBehind/stale and every coverage row's status (current/behind/empty) and daysBehind. A long weekend with a holiday on the Friday is therefore not \"behind\". The overview also carries nextHoliday (the first confirmed holiday today or later) and calendarProposals (how many days wait for confirmation). Sources and trust (manual observed NSE). The owner adds a holiday or a special session (POST /api/data/calendar: confirmed at once, replacing whatever the date had), confirms a proposal (PUT /api/data/calendar/{date}/confirm) or removes a day (DELETE /api/data/calendar/{date}: the owner's own entry is deleted; a proposal or an NSE day becomes dismissed, so it comes back neither as a proposal nor from the list; removing a dismissed day deletes it; a date with a proposal next to a confirmed day dismisses only that proposal and leaves the confirmed day — remove the date again to take the day itself off ). Changes are audited (doc 04 §2: CalendarDayAdded, CalendarDayConfirmed, CalendarDayRemoved, CalendarRefreshedFromNse: the date, kind and window — never the note). Observed proposals are computed after every data job, from the daily aggregates (so the bars must be aggregated; while any instrument has changes that are not, nothing is concluded). A weekday on which fewer than 5 % of the instruments that have bars before and after it have any bar is a suspected holiday (a day with normal coverage, or one on which a handful of instruments lack their files, is nowhere near; a day on which only 3 of 214 instruments have bars is suspected, however it came about). A weekend date with bars from at least 5 % of the instruments, or a weekday whose bars lie in the ev"},{"d":4,"h":"4.2 Corporate actions and price adjustments (ADR 0017 §5, P1-10a)","a":"42-corporate-actions-and-price-adjustments-adr-0017-5-p1-10a","t":"Bars are stored raw . A split or bonus issue changes the share count and so the price level; market.corporate actions records them, and adjusted reads (GET /api/data/instruments/{id}/bars?...&adjusted=true, and the reusable application service IAdjustmentFactors for later research reads) multiply the older prices on read. Plans and anything about tradable levels use raw prices. Dividends are not adjusted (adjust.dividends: false; adjust.splits bonus: true in doc 16). Column Meaning id, instrument id the action and its instrument (unique per instrument id, ex date, kind) ex date the India date the price level changes: the first session at the new level kind split · bonus numerator, denominator whole numbers 1 to 100000. Split n:d = old shares : new shares — 1:5 turns one share into five (a 5:1 is a consolidation). Bonus a:b = bonus shares : shares held — 1:1 is one bonus share for each held, 1:2 one for every two factor the price multiplier for the bars before the ex-date, derived and stored (12 decimals): split n / d (1:5 → 0.2), bonus b / (a + b) (1:1 → 0.5, 1:2 → 2/3, 1:3 → 0.75). A split 1:1 changes nothing and is refused status suggested (found by the detection; changes nothing) · applied (counts) · ignored (declined; kept so it is not suggested again). Rows are never deleted: xd app has no DELETE source manual · detected · csv note, created at, decided at, decided by a note up to 200 characters, when it was created, when and by whom (the owner's e-mail) it was confirmed, edited or ignored (null while suggested) detected ex date the ex-date the detection first found the action on (null for actions nobody detected); it stays when the owner corrects the ex-date, so the detection does not suggest the old date again Adjustment (pure AdjustmentFactors): a bar whose India date is strictly before an action's ex-date is multiplied by its factor; the ex-date itself and later are not. Several actions multiply (order does not matter). Open, high, low and close are multiplied and rounded to 4 decimals only at the output ; volume is divided by the cumulative factor (more shares before a split) and rounded to whole shares. It works for every timeframe: each bar uses the factor of its own start date — a 1m or intraday bar its India date, a 1D bar its day. A 1W or 1M bar is built from the adjusted day bars when its bucket contains an ex-date after its first day (open of the first day, highest high, lowest low, close of the last day, volumes added, with the same bucket rules as the aggregates: weeks from Monday, calendar months); a bucket lying wholly before or after the ex-dates has one factor for all its days and is adjusted like a day bar, one that starts on the ex-date is not adjusted. Without an applied action the adjusted weeks and months equal the raw ones. (An earlier version applied the first day's factor to the whole bucket, which was wrong for the days from the ex-date on.) The response carries adjustment {applied, factor, actions[{exDate, kind, ratio, factor}]}: applied is the number of applied actions that changed the bars returned (ex-date after the oldest bar returned), factor the cumulative factor of the oldest bar, actions those actions oldest first. Without adjusted=true the prices are raw and adjustment is null. Detection (pure ActionDetector, over the day bars bars agg 1D; suggestions only, never applied). A day d is a candidate when 1. its open , or else its close , is within 2.5 % of one of the common price factors 1/2, 1/3, 1/4, 1/5, 1/10, 1/20, 2/3, 3/4 of the previous close; 2. its volume is at least 1.5 × the median volume of the 20 sessions before it (at least 10 must exist; the volume must be above 0); 3. the move is not reversed : neither the close of d nor the closes of the next 3 sessions that exist come back above previousClose × √factor (the middle between the old level and the new, in log terms). A candidate on the newest day is judged on the sessions there are. Reverse splits (a price rise) are not looked"},{"d":4,"h":"5. Real-time data — the active set only","a":"5-real-time-data-the-active-set-only","t":"To keep the system fast and within limits, live data is streamed only for the active set : Member of active set Why Method Open intraday / swing positions (all accounts) Live P&L, stop/target alerts WebSocket (quote mode) Today's armed plan tickets (until triggered/expired) Trigger detection & alerts WebSocket Context indices (NIFTY 50, NIFTY BANK, INDIA VIX) Market filter, live context WebSocket Pinned basket (optional) Your live watch list WebSocket (counts toward budget) Benchmark index (Nifty 50 by default, P2-07) Portfolio value history vs the same cash flows in the index Tracked like any instrument (IDX I day bars from the daily fetch); the Portfolio screen offers Track the benchmark Long-term holdings Valuation, day change Quote API polling (≤ 1,000 per request, every 5 min) — built (P2-06, ADR 0019): the service holdings-quotes - Holdings quotes (built, P2-06). POST https://api.dhan.co/v2/marketfeed/quote with the primary Data account's access-token and client-id headers and the body {\"NSE EQ\":[securityId, …]} (≤ 1,000 ids per request; more are split into several requests at least 1 s apart by a throttle of its own, 1 request/s, apart from the bar fetch's). The answer per security gives last price and ohlc.close (the previous session's close); a security that is missing or has a zero/negative/non-numeric price is left out for that security only. It is the one market-feed path on the endpoint allow-list (ltp, ohlc, any query, any other method or host stay refused). Failures are fail-closed with fixed safe messages: 401/403 (or DH-901/DH-902) → the token was refused, 429 (or DH-904) → rate limited, anything else or an unexpected answer → failed; nothing is retried inside the call (the next tick is the retry) and neither token, client id nor body is logged. The shapes in docs/research/dhan-samples/quote- .json are written from Dhan's documentation and unverified until the owner's first real run. Only the open holdings that map to an NSE instrument of the master are quoted; the rest keep their synced price. - Live feed (built, P2-10, ADR 0024). The container xd-feed connects to wss://api-feed.dhan.co (query version, token, clientId, authType — one exact entry on a WebSocket allow-list of its own, checked before anything is opened) with the primary Data account's token and client id, between 09:00 and 15:35 IST on trading days (the window of the service live-feed; it heartbeats all day). Active set in P2: the open holdings that map to an NSE instrument (quote mode), the instruments of active price alerts and the benchmark and context indices NIFTY 50 / NIFTY BANK / INDIA VIX (ticker mode), de-duplicated, recomputed every minute and at once when a holding or alert changes (NOTIFY active set changed), cut at Live:MaxWsInstruments (200) in the order alerts, indices, holdings by value. Subscriptions go out in messages of at most 100. Ticks are coalesced (latest per instrument) and flushed every 2 s in one statement into the open holdings (last price source = live; a quote or live price of the same India day survives a holdings sync; a live price never replaces a newer quote and the other way round; prices before 09:15 IST are indicative and never replace a same-day quote and trigger no alert ; previous close stays with the quote poll), into market.live quotes (unlogged, one row per instrument, emptied at each window start) for instruments that are not holdings, and into the price alerts (the P2-09 evaluator, triggered once). After a flush the feed sends NOTIFY live prices (at most once a second). Reconnects back off 1, 2, 4 … 30 s with jitter and resubscribe from the database; more than 10 reconnects in 5 minutes pause attempts for 5 minutes and end the run failed (\"flapping\"); a refused token waits until the token changes (checked once a minute). No packet for 30 s counts as dead when an index is subscribed (indices tick all the time). Observed in the first real session (2026-10-14): Dhan packs several packets back to back in "},{"d":4,"h":"6. End-of-day fetch (Tier B)","a":"6-end-of-day-fetch-tier-b","t":"Scheduled after close (default 15:50 IST) before the nightly pipeline: 1. Refresh Dhan token; refresh instrument master (new listings, lot sizes, expiries). 2. For every tracked instrument: fetch today's 1-minute candles and daily candle (throttled, chunked, resumable). 3. Detect gaps for recent days and backfill (§6.1); corporate actions (§4.2: the detection follows the aggregate phase and only suggests); calendar (§4.1: NSE's holiday list and the observed proposals follow every data job). 4. Quality checks (§10b, the last step of the same job); reconcile with NSE files if enabled. 5. Refresh aggregates; then the nightly pipeline starts (doc 03 §7). Adding new tracked instruments triggers a backfill job (CSV first, then Dhan for gaps). Built so far (P1-17a, ADR 0016 §7): the manual Dhan phase of the Update data button, without the schedule, gap detection or the daily candle. For every tracked, not retired share, ETF or index with a Dhan id (instrument broker ids) it calls Dhan's Data API once per window of at most 90 days, from two days before the instrument's last stored bar (the overlap proves the timestamp alignment: price differences beyond 0.05 % are counted as warnings) or Data:Dhan:InitialDays (365) back, up to now: Request POST https://api.dhan.co/v2/charts/intraday, headers access-token (the primary Data account's token from the vault) and Content-Type: application/json (no client-id: not documented for this endpoint, and the holdings read works without it) Body {\"securityId\":\"<SECURITY ID, text \",\"exchangeSegment\":\"NSE EQ\"\\ \"IDX I\",\"instrument\":\"EQUITY\"\\ \"INDEX\",\"interval\":1,\"oi\":false,\"fromDate\":\"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\",\"toDate\":\"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\"} — dates in India time , toDate = now; shares/ETFs are NSE EQ/EQUITY, indices IDX I/INDEX (a type and segment that do not belong together are not sent) Answer parallel arrays open, high, low, close (float), volume, timestamp (and open interest, ignored). The documentation does not state the time base: timestamp is read as Unix seconds , or milliseconds when it has 13 digits. Empty arrays are \"no bars\", arrays of different lengths are a failure Proof the bars of a window must lie within a day of the period asked for, and not be 5 h 30 min off: when most bars lie outside the regular session but all fit a session after adding or subtracting 5 h 30 min, or the bars include one stamped 15:30 and none 09:15 (stamped with the minute's end), that instrument fails with a message naming the suspicion — nothing is shifted or guessed. Bars outside sessions are rejected and counted by the pipeline; the minute still running is left for the next fetch Limits 15 s per request, answers up to 16 MB, no redirects, one request at a time through a shared throttle (Data:Dhan:RequestsPerSecond, default 4); the documentation states no rate limit Errors 401/403, or DH-901/DH-902 on a 4xx → the token or Data API subscription is refused: the phase ends (data.dhan not connected, reconnect under Accounts); 429, 5xx or DH-904 → one retry after 2 s , then the instrument fails; every other 4xx is never retried and fails only that instrument (the run goes on). DH-907 is not read as \"no data\" here (it also means wrong parameters): it is a visible failure of that instrument (the trade-history and ledger reads of §8.3 decide differently: there it is an empty result only after page 0) Leaks only the HTTP status and a well-formed Dhan error code (DH-905) are logged or shown; never the token, the URL, the request body or the text of Dhan's answer"},{"d":4,"h":"6.1 Scheduled run and gap backfill (ADR 0017 §2–§3, P1-12a)","a":"61-scheduled-run-and-gap-backfill-adr-0017-23-p1-12a","t":"Built (P1-12a, spec; moved onto the services framework by P1-13, ADR 0018): the schedule and the gap backfill, in xd-api. The scheduled run. The end-of-day run is the service eod of the Services screen (doc 04 §3.1): the scheduler starts the ordinary data job with the steps dhan, aggregate, quality (every step of the pipeline but csv, which is for history the owner brings by hand; the quality checks of P1-14a are §10b) and trigger daily, requested by system:data-eod, and the run ends as the job does (its progress is linked to the run record, data job id). The run time is set on the Services screen — by default 15:50 India time on trading days of the calendar (§4.1: no weekends or holidays, special sessions included) and never before that day's session has closed — a special evening session such as Muhurat moves the day's run to its end. Data:Eod:Enabled and Data:Eod:TimeIst are only the first-start settings : read once, when the service has no stored row, and ignored after it (a service paused there is switched off). Catch-up: when the application starts on a trading day after the run time and no run exists for the day, it runs at once (a paused service never does). At most one automatic run per day. When another data job is running, or the primary Data account cannot fetch (no account, not connected, expired) or the Dhan fetch is not available, no job starts: a skipped run is recorded (job status skipped, the reason as its error, no phase ran) and nothing is retried that day; the owner runs \"Update data\" by hand. A failure (a database error, a failed job) ends the run as failed; it is retried only when the service's retries setting says so (default none), and raises a notice when notify on failure is on. Jobs carry trigger (manual daily); the overview's eod block gives enabled (the service is not paused), timeIst (the time of the stored schedule), nextRunAt (the next scheduled start; null when paused), scheduleWords and lastRun (the latest automatic data job: id, start, end, status, trigger, reason) — the same schedule the Services screen edits. Gap detection and backfill (the Dhan phase of every job, scheduled or not). For each tracked instrument with bars, the sessions of the last Data:Gaps:LookbackSessions (default 30 , 1 to 250) trading days of the calendar that have closed (never today's open session) and that lie after the day of the instrument's first bar are counted. The bars of a day come from the daily aggregate's bars column — one cheap read of a month of days; only the days of the instrument's not-yet-aggregated range (the dirty range) are counted in the 1-minute table, by its primary key — and compared with the calendar's expected minutes : 375 for a regular session, the window's length for a special one, none for a holiday or weekend (never a gap). A session with fewer bars is a gap in market.bar gaps (instrument id, date, expected, found, attempts, last attempt at, status, detected at), status open filled unfillable. Situation What happens A gap older than the ordinary window (two days before the last bar) Recorded open and fetched in extra windows: gap sessions that follow each other in the calendar share a window, windows are cut at 90 days (the planner of §6 above); the bars go through the same merge, precedence and dirty-range path as every fetch, so the aggregates are rebuilt A partial session inside the ordinary window Fetched anyway; recorded as a gap only if the fetch does not complete it After the fetch: all expected bars there filled After the fetch: more bars than before, but not all stays open (found updated, attempts + 1); asked for again by the next run After the fetch: no more bars than before (a halt, a circuit-locked day) unfillable, attempts + 1; scheduled and ordinary runs leave it alone ; \"Re-fetch gaps\" (refetchGaps: true) asks again The instrument's fetch fails nothing is decided and no attempt is counted: it is retried by the next run A known gap complete by other means (a CSV file) fille"},{"d":4,"h":"7. Broker abstraction","a":"7-broker-abstraction","t":"Interface Methods v1 adapter IMarketDataProvider instruments, intraday/daily history, quotes Dhan (built so far: the 1-minute history behind IIntradayBarSource, §6) ILiveFeed subscribe/unsubscribe, tick stream Dhan WebSocket IAccountReader funds, positions, holdings, trades (history), ledger Dhan (built so far: holdings §8.2; trade history and ledger, P2-13, §8.3 as IBrokerTrades / IBrokerLedger; funds and positions not built) IBrokerConnector (P1-16c) start/complete the browser consent login Dhan (API key & secret consent flow) — §8.1 ITokenProvider get, refresh, expiry for the services Reads the vault and accounts.broker connections (P1-12) IOrderExecutor place/modify/cancel (later phase) — IFileImporter CSV profiles → bars CSV Dhan is the only broker (ADR 0009); another one means a new decision, one connector and one adapter project. Dhan API keys, secrets and tokens are kept in the encrypted broker vault (accounts.broker secrets, doc 04 §1). The instrument master maps each broker id (Dhan security id + segment) to a canonical symbol; mapping mismatches are quality issues, never guessed."},{"d":4,"h":"7.1 Instrument master sources (ADR 0010, P1-03)","a":"71-instrument-master-sources-adr-0010-p1-03","t":"The master list (doc 06 §2) is built from two public files ; no credentials, cookies or account data are sent. The API's HTTP clients call them with an allow-list (GET, HTTPS, exact host and path, no query, no redirects followed, no cookies, User-Agent: xDrishti/0.1 (personal use), no request logging beyond the source name and HTTP status), size caps and total timeouts: File URL Size / cap / timeout Used for Dhan detailed scrip master https://images.dhan.co/api-data/api-scrip-master-detailed.csv 207,560 rows, 35 MB · 100 MB · 120 s Instruments: canonical symbols, SECURITY ID, ISIN, series, lot and tick size, expiry, ASM/GSM, MTF leverage, freeze quantity. Serves ETag and Last-Modified; a conditional request returns 304 when unchanged NSE Nifty Total Market list https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/indices/ind niftytotalmarket list.csv 49 KB, 755 stocks · 5 MB · 30 s Sector (NSE \"Industry\", 22 values) by ISIN. NSE refuses clients without a User-Agent and ignores If-None-Match, so it is fetched in full on every changed master (it is small); a failure to get or read it never fails the refresh Dhan's header has 33 columns (the last one empty). The importer finds the 17 columns it reads by name and fails closed with a plain message when one is missing or repeated; extra or no-longer-present unused columns, and single unreadable or oversize rows, are tolerated with a warning (doc 06 §2.2). Both files can change format without notice; a refused file leaves the previous list untouched. Using them follows the sources' terms for personal use (doc 19)."},{"d":4,"h":"8. Accounts","a":"8-accounts","t":"Any number of accounts (all your own), each with roles — Data, Trading, Portfolio — as described in doc 04 §1. The market-data jobs and live feed in this document always use the primary Data account (with automatic failover to a standby Data account if configured). Trading accounts have mode (paper / manual live; automated modes later), risk profile, allowed styles/instruments and capital source. Nightly sync reads funds, positions, holdings, trade history and ledger → feeds the auto-journal (fills matched to plan tickets) and portfolio monitoring (doc 12). The instrument master needs no account: it comes from two public files (§7.1)."},{"d":4,"h":"8.1 Connecting: Dhan's consent flow","a":"81-connecting-dhan39s-consent-flow","t":"From Dhan's own documentation (verified 2026-10-11; ADR 0008, 0009, specs P1-16c, P1-16d). The connector calls only the endpoints below (allow-list in the HTTP client: HTTPS, exact host and path, method; no redirects followed), with a 15-second timeout, no retries (codes are single-use) and no request logging (URLs and headers carry tokens and codes; the tokenId in the consume URL counts as a secret). Dhan error codes are kept only when they have Dhan's form (DH-901); message texts are never used. Step Dhan (API key & secret) Start POST https://auth.dhan.co/app/generate-consent?client id={clientId}, headers app id, app secret → consentAppId (max 25 a day) Browser https://auth.dhan.co/login/consentApp-login?consentAppId=… → redirect ?tokenId=… (no state) Exchange POST https://auth.dhan.co/app/consumeApp-consent?tokenId=…, headers app id, app secret → accessToken, expiryTime, dhanClientId, dhanClientName, dhanClientUcc Holder check dhanClientId = the account's client id Expiry expiryTime (ISO, India time unless an offset is given); otherwise 24 h after issue Disconnect token deleted locally (Dhan has no logout for these tokens; no call) A 4xx answer is a refusal (wrong key/secret, used or expired tokenId), anything else (5xx, 3xx, timeout, network, unreadable body) is \"broker unavailable\"; both surface as 502 accounts.broker error with a safe message. Expiry times are stored in UTC."},{"d":4,"h":"8.2 Reading the holdings (ADR 0012, P2-01)","a":"82-reading-the-holdings-adr-0012-p2-01","t":"GET https://api.dhan.co/v2/holdings with the header access-token (the account's stored token, read from the vault) answers an array of {exchange, tradingSymbol, securityId, isin, totalQty, dpQty, t1Qty, mtf t1 qty, mtf qty, availableQty, collateralQty, avgCostPrice, lastTradedPrice} — no purchase date and no previous close. xDrishti uses exchange, tradingSymbol, securityId, isin, totalQty, avgCostPrice and lastTradedPrice. It is the holdings endpoint of the allow-list (GET, HTTPS, exact path, no query, no redirects, 30 s, answers up to 5 MB); the allow-list holds api.dhan.co paths for the chart data (§6), the quote (§5), and since P2-13 the trade history and the ledger (§8.3), never orders, positions or funds. A 401/403 means the saved token is expired or revoked (409 portfolio.not connected, \"sign in to Dhan again\"); other 4xx and 5xx/timeouts are 502 accounts.broker error. An empty array (HTTP 200 []) and Dhan's documented data error DH-907 (\"incorrect parameters or no data present\"; this request has no parameters, so here it can only mean no data) both read as an empty list; DH-1111 is not in Dhan's documentation and is only tolerated as an alias. An empty list is never trusted blindly: see the safeguard in doc 12 §3. Rows of one holding (same ISIN, or same security id and exchange) are added up (quantity summed, average cost weighted). Neither the token, the URL nor the raw answer is logged or returned."},{"d":4,"h":"8.3 Trade history and ledger (ADR 0025, P2-13)","a":"83-trade-history-and-ledger-adr-0025-p2-13","t":"Two more read-only endpoints are on the allow-list, both GET, HTTPS, host api.dhan.co, header access-token (the account's stored token), no redirects, 30 s timeout, answers up to 5 MB , calls kept at least Portfolio:BrokerHistory:CallSpacingMs (default 250 ms) apart by one process-wide throttle. The guard accepts exactly these shapes and still refuses every other path and method: Endpoint Shape accepted by the guard Answer (field names from Dhan's public documentation, not yet seen in a real answer of the owner's account) Trade history GET /v2/trades/{yyyy-MM-dd}/{yyyy-MM-dd}/{page}: two valid dates and a page number (digits, pages from 0, at most 10,000) in the path, no query an array (or an object with it under data) of exchangeTradeId (else tradeId), orderId, transactionType, exchangeSegment, productType, tradingSymbol (can be null; customSymbol is a company name and never a ticker), isin, securityId, tradedQuantity, tradedPrice, six rupee charge fields (sebiTax, stt, brokerageCharges, serviceTax, exchangeTransactionCharges, stampDuty) and exchangeTime (else createTime, updateTime; \"NA\" is missing), India time; at most 20,000 rows per page Ledger GET /v2/ledger?from-date=…&to-date=…: exactly those two query keys, each once, valid dates an array of rows — or, as in Dhan's published sample, one object (also under data), read as one row — of voucherdate, voucherdesc, narration, vouchernumber, exchange, debit, credit, runbal (numbers or text such as \"20,000.00\"; dates such as Jun 22, 2022); at most 50,000 rows Names match in any case and numbers may be text; the mapping is tolerant because it is not yet checked against a real answer of the owner's account (it was checked against Dhan's published sample, read 2026-10-14 from <https://dhanhq.co/docs/v2/statements/ : fixtures dhan-published-trade.json, dhan-published-ledger.json; ADR 0025 amendment g). A trade row is kept when it is NSE EQ and its product is not an intraday product — delivery (CNC, MTF), MARGIN and a row with no product are all kept: BSE, derivatives, currency, commodity and other segments are skipped per reason, and so are the intraday products (INTRADAY, INTRA, MIS, CO, BO), counted as intraday. A row without an id, side, whole positive quantity, positive price or readable date is dropped and counted. Security. tradingSymbol can be null in Dhan's rows: the ticker is then found through Dhan's securityId (market.instrument broker ids, dhan, NSE EQ), else through the isin; a row that no instrument matches but that has an ISIN is stored ISIN-only (empty ticker, like a tradebook CSV line with an ISIN and no symbol) and matched to its holding by ISIN; a row with none of ticker, resolvable id and ISIN is not stored and counted as skip reason no symbol. Charges and ISIN. charges is the sum of the six charge fields (rupees, 4 decimals; strings and \"NA\" tolerated; a missing or unreadable field is 0) and the ISIN is stored with the transaction; the charges are counted in the net returns since P2-14 (ADR 0026) as a separate line, and are not used by lots, cost or the gross XIRR. A broker trade stored by an earlier read without its ISIN, ticker or charges is completed when its day is read again (only those three, only when missing, never the date, side, quantity or price; counted as enriched, doc 12 §3). A ledger amount is one signed number, credit − debit; a row with an unreadable date or amount, or that moves nothing, is dropped and counted. The classifier knows the published sample's PAYBNK (funds out), FUNDS ADDED / PAYIN (funds in) and DPC Interest (charges). Dividends are not in this ledger as a rule: Dhan credits them to the owner's bank account (a dividend-class entry is rare, e.g. rented stocks), so the owner enters dividends (P2-14, ADR 0026); a dividend-class entry is only offered as a suggestion. Of the charges-class entries only those naming an account-level cost (DP, AMC, delayed-payment interest, penalty, platform fee, pledge / payout / withdrawal charges, cal"},{"d":4,"h":"9. Storage","a":"9-storage","t":"Table Type Notes market.instruments Table (P1-03) The master list: canonical symbol (unique), type, name, ISIN, series, lot/tick size, expiry, sector, flags, first/last seen, retired at; trigram indexes for search market.instrument broker ids Table (P1-03) Dhan SECURITY ID + exchange segment per instrument market.master refreshes Table (P1-03) One row per refresh run: trigger, status, phase, counts, validators, warnings, error market.bars 1m Hypertable (P1-17a), 1-month chunks, compressed after 7 days (segmented by instrument, ordered by time) PK (instrument id, ts) with ts = bar start (UTC); raw o/h/l/c (double), volume, source = csv / dhan. xd app: no DELETE. The one index is the primary key market.bars agg Table (P1-17a), not a hypertable One row per (instrument id, timeframe, ts) for 5m, 10m, 15m, 30m, 1h, 1D, 1W, 1M: o/h/l/c, volume, bars, complete; 09:15-anchored (§4). Built by the application. xd app: no DELETE market.bar coverage Table (P1-17a) Per instrument: first/last bar, 1-minute bars (all, csv, dhan), the changed range not aggregated yet (dirty from/dirty to), when and what the aggregates hold (aggregates JSON). Kept up to date by every write of bars market.csv files Table (P1-17a, P1-07a) The CSV files loaded: name, size, modified time, instrument, rows / inserted / updated / repaired / rejected, loaded at (written in the transaction of the file's bars), first bar/last bar (the range its undo works on; null for files loaded before they were recorded), undone at (set by the undo: the file is pending again; a new load clears it). xd app: no DELETE market.data jobs Table (P1-17a) One row per run of the data pipeline (CSV → Dhan → aggregates → quality checks): steps, status, the one instrument a fix action is about (instrument id, else null), the files a job was asked to load (csv files, JSON, null = all), the file whose load it undoes (undo csv file), and per phase its progress and counts (JSON); at most one row running (unique partial index + advisory lock); history is kept market.undo csv bars(p instrument uuid, p from timestamptz, p to timestamptz) → bigint Function (P1-07a) SECURITY DEFINER, owned by the migrating role (xd owner), SET search path = pg catalog, market, pg temp, every object schema-qualified. In one call: deletes the source = 'csv' rows of that instrument with p from ≤ ts ≤ p to from bars 1m (Dhan rows and other instruments never), the bars agg buckets (all timeframes, India-time days, weeks from Monday, months) that overlap the range, then brings bar coverage up to date with the range added to its changed range (dirty from/dirty to) — or deletes the coverage row when no bar is left. Returns the bars removed. EXECUTE revoked from PUBLIC, granted to xd app only; xd app cannot replace or alter it. Compressed chunks: see below market.quality runs, market.quality results Tables (P1-14a) One row per data quality run (trigger, window, counts by result, mean score, the data job it belongs to, the one instrument it is about for a fix action; at most one running) and one row per run and instrument (status, score, bars checked, missing minutes, the checks as jsonb); xd app: no DELETE (§10b) market.bars 1m live Hypertable, short retention Live bars for the active set market.bars 1d Table Official (Dhan daily candles) or derived (flagged) — the derived day bar is bars agg 1D until the official one exists market.context daily Table Indices, VIX, breadth, regime data.import profiles, data.import jobs, data.import issues Tables CSV import config, history, rejected rows market.corporate actions Table (P1-10a) Splits and bonus issues per instrument and ex-date: ratio, derived price factor, status (suggested · applied · ignored), source, note, decision time and by whom (§4.2). Unique (instrument, ex-date, kind). xd app: no DELETE (a declined action is ignored). Research reads apply the applied ones on read (adjusted=true, IAdjustmentFactors); plans use raw prices Size: 200 instruments × 8 years of 1-minute data ≈ "},{"d":4,"h":"10. Data quality","a":"10-data-quality","t":"Check Action Missing minutes (no halt) Backfill from Dhan; flag if unresolved Spike X × ATR with reversal Flag bad tick OHLC inconsistency / duplicate timestamps (CSV) Reject row, report Timestamp label mismatch (bar end vs start) Detected by session boundary check; profile fix suggested CSV vs Dhan conflict beyond tolerance Keep by precedence; log Unapplied corporate action Alert: the suggestion (§4.2) shows as pendingCorporateActions on the Data and Data quality screens and as the bell notice data corporate actions pending until confirmed, edited or ignored Rename / merger / delisting Master update; history linked Circuit-locked days Mark unfillable The nightly plan is not produced (fail closed) if index data fails or more than X% of tracked instruments fail; individual instruments that fail validation are skipped by the scan (per-instrument fail-closed)."},{"d":4,"h":"10b. Data quality window (validation workbench)","a":"10b-data-quality-window-validation-workbench","t":"The Data validation service runs after every end-of-day fetch (and on demand) over the last N sessions (default 5) of every tracked instrument: Check How Completeness 375 one-minute bars per session; lists missing minutes Bar sanity low ≤ open/close ≤ high, positive prices Duplicates No repeated timestamps Spikes Move k × median one-minute move and 1% Aggregates vs 1-minute Every stored 5m/15m/30m/1h/1D bucket is recomputed from the 1-minute bars with an independent reference implementation and compared exactly (OHLC and volume) — catches aggregates not refreshed after late data Derived vs official daily 1D built from 1-minute vs the broker's official daily candle (±0.05% OHLC, ±1% volume) Freshness Data present up to the last trading day The Data quality screen lists every tracked instrument with status (pass / warning / fail), coverage, bars checked, missing minutes, per-timeframe aggregate status, official-daily status and source (CSV / Dhan), plus a data quality score. Inspect shows each check, a side-by-side table of stored vs recomputed buckets (with \"differences only\"), the chart built from 1-minute data and the missing minutes. Re-fetch / Refresh aggregates repair an instrument and re-validate it automatically; every run is kept in a history. Built (P1-14a, spec, ADR 0017 §4): the backend of the window above, without the official-daily comparison (reported notAvailable until official daily candles are fetched). Where it runs. The quality run is the fourth step of the data job (csv, dhan, aggregate, quality, always in that order), recorded in market.quality runs / market.quality results. A job therefore shares everything a data job has: the single one-at-a-time rule (a quality run and a data job exclude each other , 409 data.job running), the worker, the progress (the job's quality block: done of total instruments, passed/warned/failed, runId) and the recovery at start-up (a run still running that began before the process is marked failed; a run whose data job ended is failed when the next one starts). Without steps \"Update data\" still runs csv, dhan, aggregate — the quality step is only run when the request lists it (trigger of the run: after job). The scheduled end-of-day run is dhan, aggregate, quality (daily). POST /api/data/quality/run {instrumentId?} is a job with the single step quality; the fix actions are jobs about one instrument (instrumentId on the job): Re-fetch = dhan (the instrument only, refetchGaps so unfillable days are asked for again) → aggregate → quality; Refresh aggregates = aggregate (the instrument's window marked changed and rebuilt) → quality. A job about one instrument is not counted as the last run of a phase on the Data screen and runs no calendar upkeep. The window. The last Data:Quality:Sessions (default 5, 1 to 60) trading sessions of the calendar that have closed (never today's open one), the same for every instrument. Instruments: tracked, not retired, with bars. For every instrument the 1-minute bars are read once, by the primary key, from the earlier of the Monday and the 1st of the month of the window's first session (the week and month buckets reach back that far) to now, and the stored aggregates from bars agg by its primary key. About 560,000 rows for 214 instruments; measured 1.7 s for the whole run on generated data (TimescaleDB in a container). Check Rule as built Completeness For every window session after the day of the instrument's first bar : the minutes the calendar expects (375, or the special session's length; none on a holiday) against the stored minutes of the session. Missing minutes are listed as ranges (≤ 500 ranges kept; the totals are exact). warning when the missing share of the window is above Data:Quality:MaxMissingPercent (default 1) or a session bar gaps marks unfillable still lacks minutes (unfillableMinutes); never fail Bar sanity Every window bar: prices finite and positive, low ≤ open, close ≤ high, volume ≥ 0. Any bad bar = fail (first 10 listed). Dupli"},{"d":4,"h":"11. Biases avoided","a":"11-biases-avoided","t":"Survivorship (historical index membership incl. delisted stocks), look-ahead (closed bars only; higher-TF from last closed bar; point-in-time regime), selection (basket rules are dated; point-in-time membership available). The closed-bar rule is built into XDrishti.Indicators (P3-01, ADR 0031) and checked from two sides: the frame never shows the bar still forming (a test builds a forming bar worth 200 against a closed bar worth 100 and asserts 100), and a prefix property (the outputs for the first k bars do not change when more bars are fed) guards against hidden state and any dependence on later input. The prefix property alone cannot detect a forming-bar leak in a push-only API, which is why the frame-semantics tests exist."},{"d":5,"h":"06 — Instruments & Baskets","a":"","t":"How you choose what the system works on and how you group and evaluate it."},{"d":5,"h":"1. Concepts","a":"1-concepts","t":"Concept Meaning Master list The instruments v1 works with from Dhan's instrument (scrip) master — NSE shares, ETFs, indices and futures (§2.1; options later) — refreshed every morning and on demand, mapped to canonical symbols (doc 05 §7) Tracked instrument An instrument you've switched on. Only tracked instruments get historical data, end-of-day updates, scans and analytics Basket A named group of tracked instruments; an instrument can be in many baskets Scope Which basket(s) a strategy scan, a Strategy Lab test, a report or a portfolio view applies to"},{"d":5,"h":"2. Master list & tracked instruments","a":"2-master-list-tracked-instruments","t":""},{"d":5,"h":"2.1 What the master list contains (ADR 0010, P1-03)","a":"21-what-the-master-list-contains-adr-0010-p1-03","t":"Dhan's detailed scrip master has about 207,000 rows (35 MB, republished daily around 08:20 IST); v1 keeps the ≈ 4,400 NSE rows it works with and skips the rest on import: Kept (type) Rule on Dhan's columns Real file, 2026-10-11 Share EXCH ID = NSE, SEGMENT = E, INSTRUMENT TYPE = ES (any series: EQ, BE, SM, ST, …) 3,204 ETF NSE, E, INSTRUMENT TYPE = ETF 405 Index NSE, SEGMENT = I, INSTRUMENT = INDEX (NIFTY, BANKNIFTY, INDIA VIX, …) 119 Future NSE, SEGMENT = D, INSTRUMENT ∈ {FUTSTK, FUTIDX} — stock futures (213 underlyings) and index futures 653 (635 stock, 18 index) Skipped: exchange test symbols ( NSETEST ), debt, bonds, mutual-fund units, REITs/InvITs, BSE, MCX, currency, commodity and options (options come with their phase, P14). Futures past their expiry date (India date) are not kept; one that was kept and has expired is retired. Canonical symbols (exchange-qualified, independent of any broker; every other module uses them): Type Canonical symbol Example Share, ETF NSE:{UNDERLYING SYMBOL} (the ticker) NSE:RELIANCE, NSE:SETFNIF50 Index NSE:{SYMBOL NAME} upper-case, spaces as NSE:NIFTY, NSE:NIFTY MIDCAP 150, NSE:INDIA VIX Future NSE:{UNDERLYING}-{YYYY}-{MM}-FUT (expiry month) NSE:RELIANCE-2026-10-FUT A collision (two rows with the same canonical symbol, or one Dhan id for two symbols) is a data-quality problem: the first row is kept, the others are skipped, and every one is listed in the refresh result — never silently merged. The real file has none. Flags and sector are derived, not guessed: - F&O eligible = a share with an active stock future (213 in the real file); futures, indices and ETFs are never flagged. - ASM/GSM = Dhan's ASM GSM FLAG (anything but N) and category. - Sector = the industry of the share's ISIN in NSE's Nifty Total Market list (ind niftytotalmarket list.csv: 755 stocks in 22 industries; 748 are shares of the master, 3 are REITs outside the scope). Only shares get a sector, so the sector facet and filter work on shares: shares without a match show \"Unclassified\" (a facet value; filtering by it returns exactly those shares). If the list cannot be fetched or read, for whatever reason, the refresh still succeeds with a warning and keeps the sectors it has."},{"d":5,"h":"2.2 Refresh","a":"22-refresh","t":"Trigger Behaviour Refresh list (Instruments screen, any signed-in session, no step-up — it only reads public files) POST /api/instruments/refresh → 202 and a background run; progress phases downloading → parsing → enriching → saving with the number of rows read; 409 instruments.refresh running while one runs Force refresh Same with force: true: skips the \"file unchanged\" check and imports again Daily automatic run A hosted service in xd-api at Instruments:AutoRefresh:TimeIst India time (default 08:45, after Dhan's 08:20 publication); Enabled switches it off. It starts the same command as the button; the run is recorded as daily . It waits in slices of at most one minute and re-reads the clock, so a sleeping or adjusted machine still runs on time. Catch-up: when the API starts and no refresh has run since the most recent scheduled time (it was down, or the list was never loaded), it refreshes at once — one conditional request when nothing changed Rules of a run: - One at a time across all instances (database advisory lock + a unique \"running\" row). When the API starts, a run still marked running from an earlier process is marked failed (interrupted) at once; a run that never ended is anyway treated as interrupted after 30 minutes. - Conditional download: the ETag / Last-Modified of the last good run are sent, and an unchanged file ends as unchanged without any work. The shortcut is not used when the list is empty, when the last good run could not apply the sector list, or when the last run worked under other importer rules (RefreshRules.Version, bumped whenever scope or parser rules change; recorded on every run). - Streamed and tolerant: the 35 MB file is streamed (never held as a string; a line over 64 KB is refused), columns are found by name. The run fails closed only on structural problems: unreadable header, a column the importer reads is missing, no in-scope rows, or too many unreadable rows (more than 50 or more than 1 % of the in-scope rows). Smaller problems are tolerated and listed in the run's warnings: extra or unused columns, and single in-scope rows that are malformed or do not fit the stored limits (series, ISIN, names, …) are skipped with a message naming the Dhan id (the first 10, then a count). The instrument of a skipped row is not retired because of it. - Tripwire: a file that would leave fewer than half of the currently active instruments is refused (the run fails with a plain message); a forced refresh applies it anyway. - All or nothing: the kept rows are copied into a temporary table (binary COPY) and merged in one transaction , so a failed run changes nothing. Instruments no longer in the file are retired (retired at, hidden by default, never deleted) and revived when they come back. - Sectors are optional: whatever goes wrong with the NSE list (timeout, refusal, bad data) only adds a warning; the stored sectors stay and the next refresh does not skip the unchanged master. - Recorded: every run — succeeded, unchanged or failed — is a row in market.master refreshes with counts (added / updated / retired / revived / unchanged / skipped by scope / collisions / sector matches / F&O underlyings), source validators, rules version, warnings and error. The daily run is the service instrument-master of the services framework (P1-13, doc 04 §3.1): its time and switch are set on the Services screen; Instruments:AutoRefresh: are the first-start settings only."},{"d":5,"h":"2.3 Searching, tracking and the instrument record","a":"23-searching-tracking-and-the-instrument-record","t":"- Search & add from the Instruments screen: server-side search (partial symbol, name, display name or ISIN) and filters (type, sector, F&O, ASM/GSM, series, retired), sortable and paginated (≤ 100 per page; GET /api/instruments, facets at /api/instruments/facets); later also exchange/segment, index membership, price and turnover. - Bulk add by pasting symbols or uploading a CSV list (both resolved by POST /api/instruments/tracked/resolve, below), or adding all members of an index/rule-based basket (with the baskets, P1-15). - Tracking (P1-05) is the owner's choice of what the system works on; it needs a session, no step-up. Rows live in market.tracked instruments (the module's schema is market; the catalog schema of the first sketch was not created): instrument id (key, FK to market.instruments, restrict), active, active since, deactivated at, data from, data to, last update, backfill requested at, note, tracked by; a row is kept for good. - POST /api/instruments/tracked {instrumentIds} tracks 1–500 instruments, DELETE /api/instruments/tracked {instrumentIds} untracks them. Both are idempotent and all-or-nothing (one transaction), duplicates count once, an unknown id is 400 instruments.invalid, and the answer counts what happened (added, reactivated, alreadyTracked / untracked, notTracked, totalTracked). - Retired instruments (no longer in Dhan's list) cannot be newly tracked or re-tracked: 400 instruments.retired naming them, nothing in the call is applied. One that retires after being tracked stays tracked: it keeps appearing in ?tracked=true (whatever includeRetired says) with tracked.retiredButTracked: true (the UI shows \"no longer in Dhan's list\"); it can still be untracked, and then cannot come back. - Untrack only sets active = false and deactivated at; data from, data to, last update stay. Tracking again reactivates the same row (active since restarts, deactivated at clears, the history fields are kept, so only the gap is filled). - Tracking sets backfill requested at; the backfill job itself belongs to P1-06 / P1-12 . Until history exists, dataFrom/dataTo/lastUpdate are null and the screens show \"—\" (\"history not collected yet\"). - Audit (accounts.account events, no account): InstrumentsTracked / InstrumentsUntracked with counts (\"Tracked 12 of 15 instruments (10 new, 2 tracked again, 3 already tracked)\"), written only when something changed; never symbols or ids. - POST /api/instruments/tracked/resolve {symbols} (up to 5,000 entries, blanks ignored) looks up pasted tickers (RELIANCE = NSE:RELIANCE), canonical symbols (NSE:RELIANCE-2026-10-FUT) and ISINs of shares/ETFs, trimmed and case-insensitive, repeats collapsed. The answer is {matched[], unmatched[], entries, duplicates}; a matched entry carries retired and tracked; an unmatched one has a reason (not found, ambiguous = an ISIN of several instruments with not exactly one still listed, invalid = over 100 characters or control characters). Nothing is guessed: no partial or name matching. - GET /api/instruments?tracked=true false filters on tracked-now; every instrument in lists and detail has a tracked block (active, since, deactivatedAt, dataFrom, dataTo, lastUpdate, backfillRequestedAt, retiredButTracked), and /api/instruments/facets has tracked (all tracked now, retired ones included) and trackedRetired, for the \"N tracked\" header. - On activation the system will backfill history (P1-06 / P1-12): first from your CSV files if present, then gaps from the Dhan historical API (1-minute up to 5 years, daily since listing), and builds all timeframes. - On deactivation collection stops; history is kept (re-activation only fills the gap). - Status shown per instrument: data coverage (from/to), last update, quality issues, baskets, open positions. (Baskets are shown in the detail drawer; see §3.4.) Field Example Canonical symbol NSE:TATASTEEL Broker ids Dhan SECURITY ID + exchange segment (NSE EQ, IDX I, NSE FNO) in market.instrument broker ids Type / segment share · etf"},{"d":5,"h":"3. Baskets","a":"3-baskets","t":""},{"d":5,"h":"3.1 Basket types","a":"31-basket-types","t":"Type How membership is defined Examples Manual You add/remove instruments MyChoice, MyLongTerm, Best stocks, Watchlist Rule-based (dynamic) A saved filter re-evaluated nightly \"F&O stocks with turnover ₹50 Cr\", \"Nifty 50 members\", \"Sector = IT\", \"Near 52-week high\" System Maintained automatically Portfolio (one per portfolio bucket, built — P1-15a, §3.4), Open positions, Today's plan, Paused-cell instruments Each basket carries a kind in the API: manual or portfolio today; rule-based and the other system baskets reuse the same field and the baskets.system protection."},{"d":5,"h":"3.2 Basket attributes","a":"32-basket-attributes","t":"Attribute Purpose Name, colour, description Display Purpose trading · long term · watch — drives defaults (e.g. long-term baskets excluded from intraday scans) Allowed styles / sides e.g. MyLongTerm: swing BUY only Strategies in scope Which strategies scan this basket (default: all enabled) Risk profile override (optional) e.g. smaller risk per trade for a speculative basket Target weights (optional, long-term) For allocation drift view in the portfolio Membership history Added / removed dates and notes — enables point-in-time evaluation"},{"d":5,"h":"3.3 How baskets are used","a":"33-how-baskets-are-used","t":"Use Example Scan scope Nightly plan scans \"Best stocks\" and \"MyChoice\" for intraday; \"MyLongTerm\" only for swing BUY Strategy Lab universe \"Test VWAP RSI TREND on MyChoice, 2022–today\" Reports lens Compare results of all baskets side by side Portfolio grouping Value, return and XIRR per basket (doc 12) Live watch Pin a basket to the live price panel (counts toward the live-feed budget, doc 05 §5)"},{"d":5,"h":"3.4 Manual baskets: what is built (P1-15) and portfolio system baskets (P1-15a)","a":"34-manual-baskets-what-is-built-p1-15-and-portfolio-system-baskets-p1-15a","t":"Manual baskets and the portfolio system baskets exist so far (rule-based baskets, the other system baskets, allowed styles/sides, strategies in scope, risk override and target weights come with their stories). Tables live in the market schema (not catalog, which was never created), next to the instruments they point to; the runtime role has plain DML on both and no DDL. Every route needs a session (changes also the CSRF token), none needs a step-up. Rules - Many-to-many, once per basket at a time: an instrument can be in any number of baskets, but at most once in a basket — ux basket members active is a unique index on (basket id, instrument id) where valid to IS NULL. Removing sets valid to (and removed by) and keeps the row; adding again makes a new row , so the history shows every stay. Rows are never deleted; baskets are archived, instruments retired (both foreign keys are restrict), and the runtime role xd app has no DELETE on market.baskets, market.basket members or market.tracked instruments. - Dated membership: valid from / valid to are timestamps. asOf=yyyy-MM-dd (an India date) returns the members at the end of that day : valid from < start of the next day (IST) and valid to empty or = that instant — i.e. a member added on the 13th is a member on the 13th; one removed on the 13th is not. - Names: 1–60 characters, unique among active baskets ignoring case (ux baskets active name lower on lower(name) WHERE archived at IS NULL); an archived basket frees its name. Colour is rrggbb (default 6366f1), purpose trading long term watch (default trading), description up to 500 characters, note on a membership up to 500. - Seed: the migration creates, empty and idempotently, MyChoice (trading, 6366f1), MyLongTerm (long term, 10b981) and Best stocks (trading, f59e0b) at positions 0–2 (the prototype's palette). The owner can rename or archive them. - Tracking: adding an instrument that is not tracked tracks it in the same transaction (the tracking service of §2.3, audited as \"… via basket 'Name'\"); a retired instrument that is not tracked is refused with instruments.retired and nothing in the call is applied. A tracked instrument the add relied on is part of the save (tracking rows carry PostgreSQL's xmin as a row version): if a concurrent untrack commits first, the save is refused and the add reads again and tracks it again, so a member is never added on the strength of a stale \"tracked\". Removing the last membership never untracks. Archiving never untracks. - Limits: 2,000 active members per basket (baskets.too many members), 500 instrument ids per add / remove / transfer call, a reorder must list every active basket / member exactly once (else 400 baskets.invalid). - Archived baskets are hidden from the list (includeArchived=true shows them), their members and history stay readable, and every change is refused with 409 baskets.archived. Archiving twice is fine. - Order: position (0 first) among the active baskets and among the active members; a reorder numbers them 0..n-1; new members are appended in the order given. Reads sort by position, then symbol / creation, then id, so the order is deterministic. - Concurrency: market.baskets.revision is an optimistic concurrency token that every change of a basket or of its members raises. A save based on a stale read fails; the use case then reads again and retries (up to 5 times, which also resolves two requests adding the same instrument or creating the same name). Only when all tries lose does the call end in 409 baskets.conflict (the UI reloads and may retry). A reorder whose list no longer matches because the basket changed between two tries is a 409 baskets.conflict rather than a 400. - Idempotent and atomic: add, remove, reorder and transfer can be repeated; what is already done is counted, not repeated, and writes neither rows nor audit entries. Each call is one transaction (members, tracking rows, revision and audit entries are written together). Portfolio system baskets (P1-1"},{"d":5,"h":"4. Evaluation lenses — looking at results from different angles","a":"4-evaluation-lenses-looking-at-results-from-different-angles","t":"Every analytic screen uses the same lens + filters model: pick the lens (how rows are grouped), then filter by date range, source, account, style, strategy, timeframe, side, exit, regime, basket. Lens Rows Typical question All trades One summary + equity curve \"How is the whole system doing this quarter?\" By basket One row per basket \"Is MyChoice better than Best stocks for intraday?\" By instrument One row per symbol \"Which stocks consistently work / fail?\" By individual trade One row per trade, drill to replay \"Why did this TATASTEEL trade lose?\" By strategy / cell Strategy × TF × side × exit \"Which setups work in which basket?\" By account One row per broker account \"How does each account perform?\" By sector / regime / month Grouped rows \"Where and when does it work?\" Lenses can be combined as a pivot (e.g. basket × strategy heatmap of expectancy, or basket × month returns). Each basket row also shows a buy-and-hold benchmark (equal-weight basket return and Nifty 50 over the same period) so trading results are judged against simply holding the basket. Overlapping baskets: an instrument in several baskets appears under each, so per-basket totals are not additive — the UI says so. For additive totals (e.g. portfolio value) a position has one portfolio bucket (doc 12 §5). Point-in-time vs current membership: reports default to current membership; switch to as-of membership to evaluate a basket exactly as it was composed on each trade date."},{"d":5,"h":"5. Data model","a":"5-data-model","t":"Table Key columns market.instruments id, canonical symbol, type, segment, sector, lot size, tick size, flags, listed on market.instrument broker ids instrument id, broker, broker id, segment market.tracked instruments instrument id, active, active since, deactivated at, data from, data to, last update, backfill requested at, note, tracked by market.baskets id, name, colour, purpose, description, position, created at, archived at, revision (concurrency token) — manual baskets (P1-15); type (rule/system), settings and rule (JSON) join with those basket types market.basket members id, basket id, instrument id, position, valid from, valid to, note, added by, removed by — one row per stay; target weight joins with the allocation story"},{"d":5,"h":"6. Screens","a":"6-screens","t":"- Instruments — master-list search with facets; tracked list with coverage/quality (basket chips: in the detail drawer, §3.4); bulk actions (track, untrack, add to basket, backfill). - Baskets — list with key stats; editor (manual add/remove, drag & drop, rule builder with live preview); basket detail (members, performance by lens, benchmark, open positions)."},{"d":6,"h":"1. Strategy anatomy","a":"1-strategy-anatomy","t":"Part Description Example (Opening Range Breakout) id Unique name ORB styles intraday / swing intraday timeframes Allowed entry timeframes 5m, 15m, 30m sides BUY / SELL both setup Conditions known before the trigger (evaluated tonight) High relative volume yesterday, NR7, trend filter trigger What must happen to enter (evaluated tomorrow) Candle closes above OR-high (BUY) valid window When the trigger may fire 09:30–11:30 initial stop Where the idea is wrong OR opposite side / trigger-candle extreme / k × ATR filters Context gates Higher-TF trend agrees; not results day invalidation Cancel if… Gap beyond trigger; stop level hit before trigger params ≤ 4 tunable values OR length, ATR multiple Built-in strategies are written in the same rules language users use in the Strategy Lab (doc 11), so there is one way to define a strategy."},{"d":6,"h":"2. Conditional trade plans","a":"2-conditional-trade-plans","t":"Intraday entries can't be fixed the night before, so each plan item is a conditional ticket — and the simulator tests exactly this ticket on history: BUY RELIANCE INTRADAY 15m ORB basket: Best stocks account: dhan main Trigger : 15m candle CLOSE above 2,948.0 between 09:30 and 11:30 Entry : at trigger-candle close (limit +0.1% max) Stop : 2,921.5 (OR low) → risk/share 26.5 Exit : 50% at 1R, stop → breakeven, trail rest ATR(15m)×2; square-off 15:15 Size : 37 shares (risk ₹980 = 0.49% of capital) P(win) : 0.57 (n=214, regime UP LOW VOL) E[R]: +0.38 after costs Cancel : open 2,960 (gap 0.5 ATR) or 2,921.5 touched before trigger Why : NR7 yesterday, rel-volume 1.8×, 1h & 1D uptrend, sector strongest Swing tickets from daily bars: signal known at close; trigger typically \"buy-stop above today's high tomorrow\" or \"enter at open if gap within limits\"."},{"d":6,"h":"3. Starter library","a":"3-starter-library","t":"Each mirrored for SELL where sensible. Most cells will fail qualification — expected and healthy. Intraday (5m–1h): ORB (opening-range breakout) · PDH PDL BREAK / PDH PDL REJECT (previous-day high/low) · VWAP PULLBACK · VWAP RECLAIM · EMA PULLBACK (9/21 EMA with higher-TF trend) · GAP GO / GAP FILL · INSIDE BAR BO · CPR (central pivot range) · MEAN REV BANDS. Swing (1h/1D, ≤ 5 sessions): BASE BREAKOUT · TREND PULLBACK (20 EMA / 50 DMA) · NR7 ID BO · RSI2 REVERSION (above/below 200 DMA) · BB SQUEEZE · HIGH 52W MOMENTUM · FAILED BREAKOUT · RS ROTATION (strongest stocks in strongest sectors)."},{"d":6,"h":"4. Multi-timeframe mapping","a":"4-multi-timeframe-mapping","t":"Entry TF Bias TFs Typical style 5m 15m, 1h Intraday 10m 30m, 1h Intraday 15m 1h, 1D Intraday 30m 1h, 1D Intraday / short swing 1h 1D Intraday / swing 1D 1W Swing Confluence (same symbol & side on several timeframes/strategies) is a feature and a selection bonus."},{"d":6,"h":"5. Feature catalogue (point-in-time)","a":"5-feature-catalogue-point-in-time","t":"Group Examples Trend EMA slopes (9/21/50/200), price vs EMAs, ADX, swing structure, supertrend Momentum RSI(2/14), ROC, MACD histogram, distance from N-day high/low Volatility ATR %, ATR percentile, Bollinger bandwidth, NR4/NR7, inside bars, gap in ATR Volume Relative volume (same time-of-day), delivery %, OI change Levels Distance to previous-day H/L/C, CPR width, VWAP distance, 52-week high/low, round numbers Relative strength Stock vs index, stock vs sector, sector vs index (5/20/60 days) Market context Index trend/ADX, VIX level/percentile/change, breadth Calendar Weekday, time of day, expiry day/week, pre/post holiday, days to results Plan geometry Stop distance in ATR, room to next obstacle in R, achievable R:R"},{"d":6,"h":"6. Market regime","a":"6-market-regime","t":"Daily label from index data: trend (UP / DOWN / RANGE via DMA slope + ADX) × volatility (LOW / NORMAL / HIGH via VIX and ATR percentiles) → up to 9 regimes. Used to condition statistics and as a feature."},{"d":6,"h":"7. Side specifics","a":"7-side-specifics","t":"- BUY and SELL are learned separately (different speed, stops, regimes). - Intraday SELL: cash (MIS) or futures; swing SELL: futures only. - Circuit-locked stocks excluded on the locked side."},{"d":7,"h":"1. R-multiples","a":"1-r-multiples","t":"- 1R = amount risked = entry − initial stop × qty. All results are in R, so trades are comparable. - Expectancy E[R] = P(win)·avg win R − P(loss)·avg loss R − cost R. - Break-even win rate before costs: 33.3% at 1:2, 25% at 1:3."},{"d":7,"h":"2. Exit policy library","a":"2-exit-policy-library","t":"Policy Description Key parameters FIXED RR Full exit at k×R or stop k ∈ {1.5, 2, 2.5, 3} PARTIAL BE Book x% at T1, stop → breakeven, rest at T2 x, T1, T2 PARTIAL TRAIL Book x% at T1, trail remainder x, T1, trail ATR TRAIL Chandelier/ATR trail after activation period, multiple, activate at STRUCTURE TRAIL Trail beyond prior swing / previous candle lookback EMA TRAIL Exit on close beyond EMA length PYRAMID Add at progress levels, trail combined position see §4 TIME STOP Exit if not ≥ +x R after N bars/sessions x, N SESSION EXIT Intraday square-off time MAX HOLD Swing exit at end of session N N ≤ 5 EVENT EXIT Exit before results days before SESSION EXIT (intraday) and MAX HOLD (swing) are always on."},{"d":7,"h":"3. How the exit is chosen","a":"3-how-the-exit-is-chosen","t":"Per cell (weekly): replay the same historical entries under every candidate policy; choose by out-of-sample risk-adjusted expectancy (mean R − λ·downside dev R), requiring minimum samples and stability across folds — prefer the middle of a plateau over a sharp peak. From MAE/MFE (trade \"nature\"): - Stop: if 90% of winners never went beyond 0.8R adverse, 1R is sound; if winners routinely dip 1.2R, the stop is too tight. - Target: if median MFE is 2.4R but only 30% reach 3R, prefer 2–2.5R or partial booking. - Trail activation: where MFE giveback becomes common. Per trade (tonight): scale by current ATR; snap to structure (swing points, previous-day levels, round numbers); obstacle check — cap target before a strong level or skip if achievable R:R < min rr; reduce target for late intraday triggers (learned)."},{"d":7,"h":"4. Pyramiding","a":"4-pyramiding","t":"Used only where the cell shows trend persistence (fat MFE tail), mostly 30m+ and swing. Parameter Default Initial position 50% of full size Add triggers +1R, +2R (on trigger-TF close) Add sizes 30%, 20% Max adds 2 Risk rule Before each add, move stops so total open risk ≤ initial 1R"},{"d":7,"h":"5. Time rules","a":"5-time-rules","t":"Rule Intraday Swing Latest entry e.g. 14:30 Signal valid N sessions (default 1) Forced exit Square-off (e.g. 15:15) Close of session ≤ 5 Time stop e.g. < +0.5R after 6 bars e.g. < +0.5R after 2 sessions Overnight gap — Gap through stop ⇒ filled at open (simulated realistically)"},{"d":7,"h":"6. Position sizing (per account)","a":"6-position-sizing-per-account","t":"risk amount = account capital × risk per trade pct qty = min(risk amount / entry − stop , max capital per trade / entry, liquidity cap) qty = round down to lot(qty) futures skip if qty < 1 lot, or cost R max cost R Optional confidence scaling (capped fractional Kelly, 0.5×–1.25×) — off until calibration is proven in paper trading. Learned multipliers on top (doc 09 §14): meta-labelling size × 0.5–1.5, equity-curve throttle × 0.5 while the system's equity is below its 20-trade average, reduced market-gate day × 0.75. The multipliers never raise risk above the profile's maximum per trade."},{"d":7,"h":"7. Portfolio limits (per account, from its risk profile)","a":"7-portfolio-limits-per-account-from-its-risk-profile","t":"Limit Default Max new entries per day 3 Max open positions 5 Max total open risk (heat) 3% Max per sector 2 Max pair correlation (60-day returns) 0.60 — above it the lower-ranked ticket goes to reserve Daily loss limit −2R or −1.5% → no new entries Weekly loss limit −5R → half size next week Max drawdown (goal) 8% → halt new entries, review Consecutive losses in a cell 4 → pause cell"},{"d":7,"h":"8. Costs & slippage","a":"8-costs-slippage","t":"Brokerage, exchange charges, STT/CTT, GST, stamp duty, SEBI fees — from your broker's current charge sheet in config (rates change with budgets). Slippage: ticks + fraction of bar range; extra for stop exits and gaps. Every simulation includes costs."},{"d":8,"h":"09 — Learning Engine","a":"","t":"\"Self-learning\" = continuously re-measuring which setups, timeframes, sides and exits work in which market conditions, and shifting recommendations accordingly — with guards against fooling itself."},{"d":8,"h":"1. Four layers","a":"1-four-layers","t":"Layer 1 — Cell statistics. For each cell (strategy, tf, side, style, exit policy, regime) and windows (all / 250 days / 60 days): n, win rate, avg win/loss R, expectancy, profit factor, max drawdown, MAE/MFE quantiles. Win rate uses a Beta prior shrunk toward the parent group when n is small; confidence = lower bound of the credible interval; exponential recency weighting. Layer 2 — Exit selection. See doc 08 §3. Layer 3 — Meta-model (meta-labeling). - Labels: triple barrier — target, stop, time limit (session end / max hold). - Model: gradient-boosted trees (ML.NET LightGBM, FastTree fallback) per style × side (strategy as feature), isotonic (PAV) calibration , per-prediction feature contributions + permutation importance; trained and scored in-process in .NET. - Must beat baselines (logistic regression; cell stats only) out-of-sample, else not used. - Top contributing features stored with each recommendation. Layer 4 — Monitoring & adaptation. - Regime-conditioned statistics. - Drift : rolling expectancy below the backtest band for K checks ⇒ auto-pause; paused cells keep being shadow-graded and re-qualify automatically. - Champion/challenger for models and parameters. - Learning changelog for every automatic change, with evidence."},{"d":8,"h":"2. Validation: purged walk-forward","a":"2-validation-purged-walk-forward","t":"train (3y) -gap- -- test (3m) -- train -gap- -- test -- Only test windows count. Gap ≥ max holding (5 sessions). Tuning only inside train windows."},{"d":8,"h":"3. Daily feedback loop","a":"3-daily-feedback-loop","t":"1. Grade yesterday's primary, reserve and rejected items against actual data. 2. Attach real fills (auto-journal) → measure live vs paper slippage; grade on executed prices (§9). 3. Append outcomes with decision-time feature snapshots. 4. Update cell stats; run drift and lesson monitors (§10). 5. Weekly: retrain, recalibrate, challenger test."},{"d":8,"h":"4. Outputs per candidate","a":"4-outputs-per-candidate","t":"Output Meaning p win Calibrated probability of target before stop/time limit exp R / exp R lower Expected R after costs / conservative bound (used for ranking) exit policy Chosen policy with fitted levels expected hold Typical bars/sessions to resolution confidence Sample size, calibration, regime match reasons Top features + cell statistics in plain text"},{"d":8,"h":"5. Hypothesis loop (one variable at a time)","a":"5-hypothesis-loop-one-variable-at-a-time","t":"Hypotheses can come from the LLM, a parameter grid, or you. Live strategies are never edited directly by an LLM. Record example: {\"id\":\"H-2026-10-18-01\",\"cell\":\"ORB 15m BUY INTRADAY \",\"variable\":\"exits.FIXED RR.rr\",\"from\":2.0,\"to\":2.5, \"rationale\":\"Median MFE 2.7R over 120 OOS trades\",\"status\":\"shadow\", \"backtest\":{\"oos trades\":212,\"exp R champion\":0.21,\"exp R challenger\":0.26}}"},{"d":8,"h":"6. Overfitting guards","a":"6-overfitting-guards","t":"1. Out-of-sample only for decisions. 2. Minimum 30 OOS trades per cell. 3. ≤ 4 parameters per strategy; coarse grids; plateaus. 4. Multiple-testing adjustment (deflated Sharpe, stricter thresholds as tests grow). 5. Costs always on. 6. Monte Carlo trade reshuffling for drawdown expectations. 7. Paper stage before live. 8. Random-entry baseline with identical exits."},{"d":8,"h":"7. Cell lifecycle","a":"7-cell-lifecycle","t":"Default gates: OOS trades ≥ 30 · expectancy ≥ +0.15R · profit factor ≥ 1.2 · positive in ≥ 60% of folds · max drawdown ≤ 10R · calibration error ≤ 5 pp."},{"d":8,"h":"8. Compute (M4 Pro, 48 GB)","a":"8-compute-m4-pro-48-gb","t":"Full walk-forward ( 20 strategies × 6 TFs × 2 sides × 8 exits × 200 symbols × 5 years): a few hours in parallel .NET workers, monthly/weekends. Nightly incremental: minutes. Model training: minutes."},{"d":8,"h":"9. Execution feedback: learning from what actually happened","a":"9-execution-feedback-learning-from-what-actually-happened","t":"The system learns from your real fills , not only from chart prices. Every graded trade carries both the plan prices and the executed prices, and the difference is decomposed every week: Feedback What the system changes automatically Labels use real fills Outcomes, R-multiples and MAE/MFE are computed from executed prices — the model learns what is achievable Slippage per liquidity bucket Re-estimated weekly from your fills; used in every backtest, experiment and E[R] Missed triggers (touched, not filled) Trigger buffer and order type (market vs limit-with-buffer) per instrument Partial fills / broker rejections Lower the cell's capacity score ; selection prefers tickets that can actually be filled Your overrides Graded against the system's proposal (Reports → Decisions); feeds the autonomy recommendation (§13) A setup that is profitable on plan prices but not after execution is treated as not profitable ."},{"d":8,"h":"10. Lessons: problem → diagnosis → fix → result","a":"10-lessons-problem-diagnosis-fix-result","t":"When a monitor fires, the system opens a lesson — a record of what went wrong and what was done about it. Monitors: drawdown beyond the Monte Carlo band, drift (CUSUM on expectancy), calibration error, execution gap, decision drag, tail losses, data-quality failures. Category Typical diagnosis Typical fix Regime Setup fails in one volatility/trend regime Regime gate for that cell Exits MFE after stop is high (stops inside noise); targets rarely hit Stop/target multiple (one variable) Execution Fills worse than plan on illiquid names or at the open Liquidity filter, order type, entry window Calibration Predicted win rate above actual Recalibration (PAV), stronger shrinkage prior Decisions Your rejections or modifications cost R Auto-approval rule (autonomy) Drift Edge decays over months Pause / retire; promote a shadow challenger Events Gaps through stops around results Event filter, gap-aware sizing Data Bad bars caused false signals Repair, fail-closed gate {\"id\":\"L1\",\"category\":\"Regime\",\"detected\":\"2025-11-10\",\"cell\":\"ORB 15m INTRADAY \", \"problem\":\"Win rate 47% → 29% over 38 trades with INDIA VIX 18 (−9.4R)\", \"diagnosis\":\"64% of losers hit the stop within 15 minutes (false breakouts in volatile opens)\", \"fix\":{\"variable\":\"regime gate.vix max\",\"from\":null,\"to\":18,\"how\":\"auto\",\"evidence\":\"7/8 folds, +0.38R\"}, \"result\":{\"metric\":\"exp R\",\"before\":-0.21,\"after\":0.17,\"trades after\":60},\"status\":\"resolved\"} Lessons are kept forever and shown on the Learning screen with a before/after chart, the walk-forward evidence and two actions: Revert and Re-run with a different value (opens §12 pre-filled)."},{"d":8,"h":"11. Making learning visible (Learning screen)","a":"11-making-learning-visible-learning-screen","t":"View Question it answers Journey Is the system improving? Weekly live expectancy vs a frozen copy of last year's model (the counterfactual) vs plan prices; red bands from problem to fix; pins for lessons; cumulative R uplift; model versions Learning health One 0–100 score from data quality, calibration, execution, stability and evidence (sample size) — and which part is weakest Lessons What went wrong, what was done, did it work (§10) Plan vs actual Where the edge is lost between plan and fills (§9) Setups (cells) Expectancy heatmap by setup × month; lifecycle funnel (candidate → shadow → probation → active → paused → retired) Tweak & re-run Change a learning knob and replay (§12) Autonomy How much the system decides alone; change log with revert (§13) The frozen-model line is what makes progress honest: improvement is measured against \"what would have happened if the system had stopped learning\", on the same days and instruments."},{"d":8,"h":"12. Tweak & re-run (experiments)","a":"12-tweak-re-run-experiments","t":"You can change any learning knob and replay the last 12 months walk-forward before anything goes live. Knob Current (demo) Effect History window 18 months How much history each cell learns from Recency half-life 120 days Older trades count less; shorter adapts faster but is noisier Min trades to activate a cell 30 Evidence needed before a setup can trade Shrinkage prior 35 trades Pulls small samples toward the strategy average Breakout VIX gate 18 (or off) Regime gate for breakout cells Intraday stop 1.2 × ATR Initial stop for 5m/15m setups Selection threshold P(win) ≥ 52% Minimum calibrated probability to enter the plan Slippage model 8 bps Cost assumed in backtests Rules that keep experiments honest: 1. Same trades, same days (common random numbers): candidate and current model differ only by your change. 2. 12 out-of-sample folds ; the verdict needs ≥ 8 folds better. 3. Judged after the expected live gap : a cheaper slippage model cannot \"win\". 4. Total R matters : higher expectancy on far fewer trades (total R/year down 10%) is not \"better\". 5. Multiple-testing guard : the required improvement rises with the number of experiments in 30 days (0.02R + 0.006R × √trials). 6. Next step after \"better\" is shadow (paper alongside live for 30 trades); Apply needs your password, is audited, and is monitored for 60 trades with automatic rollback if live results fall below the old version."},{"d":8,"h":"13. Autonomy: less intervention, same control","a":"13-autonomy-less-intervention-same-control","t":"Level Plan tickets Learning changes Always asks you Advisory You approve every ticket All wait for you — Supervised (default) Tickets with P(win) ≥ threshold (default 58%) and every pre-trade check passing are auto-approved by the pre-open service at the cutoff; you review only the rest Pausing cells, exit re-selection, recalibration, regime gates applied automatically within guardrails Risk increases, new strategies, live execution, accounts Autonomous within limits All tickets inside risk limits are armed; daily digest with veto until the cutoff Shadow-tested challengers promoted automatically Same as above Guardrails at every level: one variable per change, out-of-sample proof, a weekly cap on automatic changes (default 3; more wait for you), 60-trade monitoring with automatic rollback, every change in the change log with one-click Revert , and password re-entry to raise the autonomy level. Auto-approved tickets are marked on the plan and in the audit log; the Decisions report compares them with your manual decisions so the threshold can itself be tuned from evidence."},{"d":8,"h":"14. Smart rules: from learned signals to a safer plan","a":"14-smart-rules-from-learned-signals-to-a-safer-plan","t":"Five learned rules sit between the scanner's candidates and tomorrow's plan, plus two feedback features. All run in the nightly pipeline without you; their state is shown on Plan review and Learning → Smart rules , and switching one off needs your password. Rule How it is learned What it changes 1 Grade on fills (§9) Executed prices from the broker (auto-journal); simulated fills in paper mode Every R, label and statistic; \"R plan\" kept alongside for the execution gap 2 Frozen-model benchmark (§11) Last year's model replayed on the same days Progress line on Learning, Today and the weekly digest 3 Lessons + automatic rollback (§10, §12) Monitors; 60-trade monitoring after every change A change that does worse live is rolled back without you (example L9) 4 Equity-curve throttle The system's own taken trades: equity vs its N-trade average (default 20) Risk × cut (default 0.5) while below; back to × 1 when above. Demo history: max drawdown −9.3R → −6.0R for 10% less total R 5 Market gate (no-trade days) Outcome of past trades by INDIA VIX level, breadth (% of tracked stocks above 20-DMA) and high-impact events (RBI, Budget) Reduced : half the tickets, risk × 0.75 · No-trade : no tickets (best candidates kept on reserve for reference) 6 Meta-labelling Second ML.NET model on graded trades: features of the signal → \"was it worth taking?\" Score < threshold (default 50%) → reserve; otherwise size × 0.5–1.5 7 Learned entry windows Walk-forward expectancy by entry hour per setup (backtest, thousands of trades) The ticket's validity window becomes the best hour (e.g. ORB 09:15–10:00) 8 Correlation-aware plan 60-day return correlation between tickets, open trades and holdings across accounts Correlation limit (default 0.6) with a better ticket or open trade → reserve (\"same bet\"); pre-trade checklist warns 9 Automatic loss tags Rules on MAE/MFE, entry time, regime, slippage, event calendar Tags on every loss (gap through stop, gave back open profit, against regime, late entry, high slippage, news/results, normal variance); a growing tag opens a lesson 10 Weekly learning digest Built by the weekly retrain service One summary: progress vs frozen model, what changed automatically, what needs you, what is being watched, why trades lost, focus for next week Honesty rules for these features: each rule's evidence (trade counts, R by bucket) is shown next to it; a rule that would remove more than half of all trades, or whose \"skipped\" trades are not worse than the \"taken\" ones over the last 100 trades, is flagged for review instead of being trusted blindly."},{"d":9,"h":"10 — Trade Selection & Allocation","a":"","t":"From dozens of candidates to the best max new entries per day (e.g. 3), fitted to each account."},{"d":9,"h":"1. Pipeline","a":"1-pipeline","t":"Scan scope: candidates come only from instruments in the baskets listed in scan scope (doc 06), each with its allowed styles/sides (e.g. MyLongTerm → swing BUY only). Every candidate carries its basket(s) so results can later be evaluated by basket."},{"d":9,"h":"Smart rules (learned, doc 09 §14)","a":"smart-rules-learned-doc-09-14","t":"Rule Effect on the plan Market gate Normal · reduced (half the tickets, risk × 0.75) · no-trade (no tickets; candidates kept on reserve) Learned entry window Intraday ticket validity window = the setup's best entry hour from walk-forward results Meta-labelling Score < meta.min take → reserve; otherwise size × 0.5–1.5 Correlation 60-day correlation corr.max with a better ticket or an open trade → reserve (\"same bet\") Equity-curve throttle Risk × throttle.cut while the system's equity is below its N-trade average Filtered tickets stay on the reserve list with the reason, and are graded like every other candidate."},{"d":9,"h":"2. Eligibility filters","a":"2-eligibility-filters","t":"Filter Default Cell status ACTIVE required Min calibrated P(win) 0.45 (for 1:2 exits) Min conservative expectancy +0.10R Min achievable R:R before obstacle 1.5 Min OOS sample 30 Max costs 0.10R Liquidity / events / F&O ban / surveillance excluded"},{"d":9,"h":"3. Merge duplicates","a":"3-merge-duplicates","t":"Group by (symbol, side); keep the highest conservative-E[R] ticket; add a confluence bonus when independent strategies/timeframes agree. Opposite sides for the same symbol: keep the stronger, or drop both if close."},{"d":9,"h":"4. Ranking","a":"4-ranking","t":"rank score = exp R lower + w conf·confluence + w regime·regime fit − w corr·correlation with book − w event·event risk"},{"d":9,"h":"5. Portfolio fill (greedy, constraints checked after each addition)","a":"5-portfolio-fill-greedy-constraints-checked-after-each-addition","t":"Constraint Default New entries per day 3 Open positions (incl. carried swing) 5 Total open risk 3% Per sector 2 Pair correlation (60-day) ≤ 0.70 Intraday / swing mix 2 / 2 Net long / short risk 2% / 2% One position per symbol yes Per basket (optional) e.g. max 2 from MyChoice Already held long-term Skip SELL setups on instruments in your holdings (configurable) count entries on: trigger (default): the plan may list up to max plan items (e.g. 5) conditional tickets; once 3 trigger, the rest are cancelled. plan mode limits the plan itself to 3."},{"d":9,"h":"6. Per-account allocation","a":"6-per-account-allocation","t":"1. Account sync provides funds, open positions and today's trades. 2. For each account: filter by allowed styles/instruments, drop symbols already held, apply the account's risk profile, size from its capital and lot sizes. 3. Mode replicate: every eligible account gets the same top tickets, sized individually. Mode distribute: ranked tickets are spread across accounts, no symbol duplicated. Default: replicate (decision Q4, 2026-10-09). 4. Cross-account limits: max accounts per symbol, max total risk across all accounts."},{"d":9,"h":"6b. Pre-trade checklist","a":"6b-pre-trade-checklist","t":"Each proposed ticket carries automatic checks — data quality, conflict with your holdings (e.g. a SELL on a stock you hold long-term), sector exposure, correlation with open trades and approved tickets , results within 3 days — shown on the ticket in Plan review (doc 15 §2). A failed check needs an explicit confirmation to approve."},{"d":9,"h":"6c. Human review","a":"6c-human-review","t":"The output of this pipeline is a proposed plan. You approve, modify or reject each ticket before the review cutoff; only approved tickets are armed at the pre-open check (doc 04 §4.2). Allocation goes only to accounts with the Trading role."},{"d":9,"h":"7. Day-level guards","a":"7-day-level-guards","t":"Daily loss limit ⇒ cancel remaining tickets; the market gate (learned from VIX, breadth and the event calendar) reduces or cancels the whole plan; the equity-curve throttle halves risk while the system is cold. Optional pre-open check (default 09:10, after the review cutoff and NSE pre-open price discovery): gap beyond trigger or gap invalidate atr ⇒ invalidate and promote from reserve."},{"d":9,"h":"8. Proving the selector (and your overrides)","a":"8-proving-the-selector-and-your-overrides","t":"Rejected candidates are graded nightly. Reports compare primary vs reserve vs rejected and \"top-1/3/5/all\" curves — showing whether ranking adds value and what max new entries per day should be. Your decisions are graded too: approved vs rejected vs lapsed vs modified tickets, compared with what the system proposed."},{"d":10,"h":"11 — Strategy Lab","a":"","t":"Submit your own strategy rules at any time; the system validates, tests and reports a verdict — no code changes, no restart."},{"d":10,"h":"1. Ways to submit","a":"1-ways-to-submit","t":"Channel How Rules editor React page with Monaco YAML editor, live JSON-Schema validation and autocomplete Form builder Pick indicators, conditions, stops, exits from menus → generates the same YAML Plain English Describe the idea to the assistant → it drafts YAML → you confirm Upload / CLI Upload a strategy file in the Lab, or run xd strategy test file.yaml — the file is imported into the database C plugin (advanced) Implement IStrategy for logic the DSL can't express; compiled and run in xd-sandbox (no network, resource limits) All channels produce one validated strategy spec, run by the same engine as built-in strategies."},{"d":10,"h":"2. Rules language (DSL)","a":"2-rules-language-dsl","t":"Declarative, safe (parsed and compiled to expression trees, never eval), look-ahead-proof (only closed bars; higher timeframes = last closed bar). \"Intraday: after 9:45 buy when a 15-min candle closes above VWAP with RSI(14) above 55 and 1-hour EMA20 above EMA50. Stop below last swing low. Target 1:2, or trail after 1R. Exit by 15:15. Mirror for shorts. Test 2022 to today on my MyChoice and Best stocks baskets.\" strategy: id: VWAP RSI TREND version: 1 style: intraday timeframes: [15m] sides: [BUY, SELL] SELL auto-mirrored unless a sell: block is given universe: { baskets: [MyChoice, \"Best stocks\"] } or instruments: [...] or a rule-based basket params: { rsi len: 14, rsi min: 55, swing lookback: 5 } setup: all: - time = \"09:45\" - tf(\"1h\").ema(20) tf(\"1h\").ema(50) - rsi(rsi len) rsi min trigger: all: - crosses above(close, vwap) entry: { type: at close } at close stop above high limit pullback stop: { type: swing low, lookback: swing lookback, buffer atr: 0.1 } exits: - { policy: FIXED RR, rr: [2.0] } - { policy: PARTIAL TRAIL, book pct: 50, t1 rr: 1.0, trail: { type: ATR, mult: 2.0 } } time rules: { last entry: \"14:30\", square off: \"15:15\" } filters: [not event day, min turnover] test: period: { from: 2022-01-01, to: today } method: walk forward walk forward simple split full period (exploration only) compare with: [baseline random, ORB] robustness: { param sensitivity: true, monte carlo: 1000, by year: true, by regime: true } Building blocks Examples Price & bars open high low close volume, close[1], prev day.high, today.open, or high(15) Indicators sma ema rsi atr adx macd bbands vwap supertrend highest lowest rel volume — which exist now: see What exists now below Multi-timeframe tf(\"1h\").ema(50), tf(\"1D\").close Conditions crosses above/below, comparisons, between, rising/falling(x,n), all/any/not Patterns inside bar, nr(7), gap pct, engulfing, pivot high/low(n) (gap pct exists now; the others follow in P3-01b) Context regime(), index.trend, vix pct, sector rs(n), day of week, is expiry day Stops fixed pct, atr mult, swing low/high, bar low/high, or opposite, level Exits Any policy from doc 08"},{"d":10,"h":"What exists now (P3-01, ADR 0031)","a":"what-exists-now-p3-01-adr-0031","t":"The building blocks are implemented in XDrishti.Indicators as streaming code over closed bars; the DSL parser (P3-02) will read them through the IndicatorCatalog and the FeatureFrame. The 19 names that exist: Group Names Notes Rolling (continuous across sessions) sma(n), ema(n), rsi(n), atr(n), highest(n), lowest(n), rel volume(n) sma ema rsi highest lowest take a price source (close, open, high, low); rsi and atr are Wilder; rel volume divides by the mean of the previous n bars Session-anchored (reset per India trading date) vwap, or high(m), or low(m) intraday only (refused on 1D); vwap on the typical price (H+L+C)/3; the opening range is the first m minutes and must be a whole number of bars of the timeframe Session context prev day open, prev day high, prev day low, prev day close, prev day volume, today open, today high, today low, gap pct the previous session that had bars (weekends and holidays skipped by the data); the DSL spellings prev day.high, today.open map to these flat catalog names Not yet built — follow in P3-01b: adx, macd, bbands, supertrend; the patterns inside bar, nr(n), engulfing and pivot high/low(n); and the regime / market-context features (regime(), index.trend, vix pct, sector rs(n)). Weekly and monthly timeframes are not built either: a feature reads 1m, 5m, 10m, 15m, 30m, 1h or 1D. How the closed-bar rule is kept. A feature on a higher timeframe (tf(\"1h\").ema(50)) is computed over bars that a TimeframeBuilder emits only once their last minute has arrived, so at 10:30 it reflects the 09:15 hour (closed at 10:14) and never the 10:15 hour that is still forming. A bar with a missing minute is emitted late and flagged incomplete, and still used; the frame counts those so the simulator can report them. An indicator that is not ready is NaN, and a condition on it is false. The warm-up is counted in bars of the feature's own timeframe (ema(20) on 15m needs 20 fifteen-minute bars; prev day. and gap pct need 376 one-minute bars, 26 fifteen-minute bars or 2 daily bars)."},{"d":10,"h":"3. Pipeline","a":"3-pipeline","t":"Runs as Hangfire jobs in xd-worker; progress streamed to React via SignalR; indicator results cached. Full walk-forward: 10–60 min depending on universe, timeframes and exits."},{"d":10,"h":"4. Report","a":"4-report","t":"Section Content Verdict PASS / MARGINAL / FAIL with exact reasons (each gate ✓/✗) KPIs Trades, win rate, avg win/loss R, expectancy, profit factor, max drawdown, net ₹ after costs Best cells TF × side × exit with OOS expectancy and sample size By basket / instrument Same metrics per basket and per instrument — where the idea works and where it doesn't Charts Equity & drawdown, R histogram, MAE/MFE, by year, by regime, parameter heatmap, Monte Carlo fan Comparisons Random-entry baseline, existing strategies, index buy-and-hold Warnings Low samples, cost sensitivity, concentration, unstable parameters Trades Full list with chart replay Summary Plain English from the assistant, using report numbers only"},{"d":10,"h":"5. Guarding against \"test until it passes\"","a":"5-guarding-against-test-until-it-passes","t":"- Every variant recorded in a per-family test ledger ; thresholds tighten as variants grow. - Only walk-forward OOS can PASS; full period is exploration-only. - Promotion always passes through paper trading. - The assistant can run tests but cannot promote ."},{"d":10,"h":"6. Storage & reproducibility","a":"6-storage-reproducibility","t":"lab.strategies / lab.strategy versions (YAML + hash), lab.lab runs (data snapshot, config version, engine version), lab.lab results, lab.test ledger. The database is the only home of strategies (no repository folder); any version can be downloaded as a file and re-imported (ADR 0002)."},{"d":11,"h":"12 — Portfolio Monitoring","a":"","t":"Add your broker accounts and monitor every trade and holding : when it was added, current value, return %, XIRR, and totals — grouped by basket, account, strategy or sector."},{"d":11,"h":"1. Data sources","a":"1-data-sources","t":"Source What it provides When Dhan holdings API (GET /v2/holdings) Current holdings (delivery): quantity, average cost, last traded price, ISIN — no purchase date (P2-01) On the first import (saving the account's Portfolio settings), \"Sync now\" and, per account, the daily automatic refresh (§3); positions API later Dhan trade history API (GET /v2/trades/{from-date}/{to-date}/{page}) Every trade with date/time, price, quantity and side, read by path dates and page in windows of at most 30 days; charges = the sum of the six charge fields of the row and the ISIN are stored (the charges are used by the net returns, P2-14; not by lots, cost or the gross XIRR); the ticker is found through Dhan's securityId or the ISIN when tradingSymbol is missing; only NSE EQ rows that are not intraday products are kept — built in P2-13 (ADR 0025, §3 \"Dhan sync (P2-13)\") One-time backfill from the first-import date, then daily at 16:30 IST on trading days (service portfolio-broker-history) and \"Sync trades & ledger\"; since P2-16 a failure that may pass is retried quietly, a renewed login catches up by itself and, when the trades do not explain a holding, one earlier year is read per day (ADR 0027, §3 \"Self-healing and attention items (P2-16)\") Dhan ledger API (GET /v2/ledger?from-date=…&to-date=…) Cash credits/debits (funds added/withdrawn, charges; a dividend entry is rare — Dhan credits dividends to the bank account, so the owner enters them, P2-14), each entry classified (funds in, funds out, dividend, charges, other) — built in P2-13 ; shown in the Cash & dividends panel. In the net returns (P2-14) only entries of class charges that name an account-level cost count (DP charges, AMC, delayed-payment / DPC interest, penalty, platform fee, pledge / payout / withdrawal charges, call & trade: LedgerCosts.IsAccountLevel); trade-related phrases (brokerage, STT, stamp duty, SEBI, transaction charges, settlements, bills) are never summed, because the trade rows already carry the trades' charges; a class dividend entry is only a suggestion the owner accepts or dismisses Same run as the trades Dividends entered by the owner Dividends received, per holding or unallocated (an account without a holding) — built in P2-14 (ADR 0026, §3 \"Dividends, charges and net returns (P2-14)\"); Dhan credits dividends to the bank account, and no API for its Money → Dividends list was found; the owner's dividend payout file (CSV) is recognised by its headers, its scrip names are matched to holdings and it is imported with Undo — built in P2-15 (ADR 0028, §3 \"Dividend import (P2-15)\") On demand CSV import Older trades (before API history / other brokers), contract-note or tradebook exports — built in P2-03 (ADR 0023): a generic CSV with auto-detected headers, a preview, an import with Undo, and a template (§3 \"Trades and the rebuild of lots\") On demand Dhan's downloaded reports The global transaction report (bill-level trades with side and all charges), the DP-charges sheets (depository costs) and the demat holding statement (ISINs and the depository's quantities), as CSV, legacy .xls or .xlsx; recognised by their title and headers, previewed and imported with Undo; the profit-and-loss statements are recognised and not imported — built in P2-17 (ADR 0029, §3 \"Dhan report imports (P2-17)\"); Dhan's own trades take precedence over the report's bill rows On demand (Import files) Manual entry Corrections, off-platform transactions — built in P2-03 : one trade at a time, voided (never edited) when wrong On demand Corporate actions Splits, bonuses, dividends Nightly Prices Live quotes for open trades & holdings; EOD close for valuation (doc 05 §5) Market hours / EOD History depth (decision Q5, amended by ADR 0012): Dhan's holdings carry no purchase date, so the owner sets the acquisition date of each holding when the first import happens, in the account's Portfolio settings (§3 \"Where acquisition dates come from\"). Lots are refined from a tradebook CSV or from trades typed in by hand"},{"d":11,"h":"2. Domain model","a":"2-domain-model","t":"- Transaction — immutable record (source: broker / CSV / manual; charges attached: counted in the net returns, not in the cost or the lots, P2-14). Built in P2-03 for CSV and manual trades (portfolio.transactions, §9); the broker source (source = broker) is built in P2-13 with the Dhan trade-history sync (§3). - Lot — each buy creates a lot; sells consume lots FIFO (matches Indian practice) → realised P&L per lot. - Position — open quantity, average cost as the broker reports it (charges are a separate line, not part of the cost: ADR 0026), first-added date, linked plan ticket (if any). - Valuation — market value at a time; a daily snapshot after close builds the portfolio value history. - Cash flow — dated money in/out used for XIRR."},{"d":11,"h":"3. Metrics","a":"3-metrics","t":""},{"d":11,"h":"Per position / trade","a":"per-position-trade","t":"Metric Definition Added on Date/time of first buy (and of each add) Days held Calendar days since first buy (or until exit) Quantity, avg cost Open quantity; average cost as the broker reports it — charges are not in it (a separate line, ADR 0026) LTP, market value Live or last close price × quantity Unrealised P&L (₹, %) Market value − remaining cost Realised P&L (₹) From FIFO-closed lots at the real (or last-seen) sale price, before charges (charges are a separate line) Total return % ( built in P2-14 ) (Realised + unrealised + dividends) / invested , where invested is the cost of what is still held (open lots), as in the gross P&L %. Realised profit stays in the numerator, so for a partly sold position the figure can look high (the denominator does not grow with what was sold); it is not a return on all the money ever put in. Null when nothing is invested or the scope has no usable price. With no dividends and nothing realised it equals the gross P&L % Day change (₹, %) vs previous close ( built , P2-06): ₹ = qty × (last − previous close), % = (last − previous close) / previous close; unavailable (never zero) when there is no previous close. Known limitation: previous close comes only from quotes and a holdings sync never touches it, so on a day without a quote (the poll off, failed or outside its window) a sync moves the price while the previous close stays from an earlier session, and the day change then spans sessions; the row exposes priceAt and priceSource so the screen can say so XIRR Annualised return from dated cash flows (§4); gross (price only) Dividends ( P2-14 ) Σ dividends the owner entered or imported from a file (P2-15) and did not void, in the scope; at account and total scope also the unallocated ones. \"None entered yet\" is not zero Charges ( P2-14 ) Trade charges (Σ charges of the scope's non-voided transactions: Dhan, CSV and manual) + at account and total scope the ledger's account-level costs (ledgerCharges). Charges are a separate line, never in cost, lots or the gross figures Net total return % ( P2-14 ) (Realised + unrealised + dividends − charges) / invested; with no dividends and no charges there is no net row at all Net XIRR ( P2-14 ) XIRR of the lot flows plus dividends (money in) and charges (money out) on their dates (§4) Coverage dates ( P2-14 ) dividendsFrom, tradeChargesFrom, ledgerChargesFrom, chargesFrom and coverageFrom say from which date each source is known, so a total is never read as \"all there was\" (§3 \"Dividends, charges and net returns\") CAGR For holdings 1 year Weight % Share of portfolio (or bucket) value System trade info Strategy, cell, entry plan, R-multiple, stop/target distance (if from a plan ticket)"},{"d":11,"h":"Where acquisition dates come from","a":"where-acquisition-dates-come-from","t":"XIRR, % return and absolute return need the date and size of every cash flow . Dhan's GET /v2/holdings gives quantity, average cost price , last traded price and ISIN, but no purchase date (docs read 2026-10-11), so the dates are set by the owner (ADR 0012, P2-01). This replaces the earlier idea (P1-16e) of deriving them from Dhan's trade and ledger APIs; ADR 0025 (P2-13) reinstates the trade API — as stored trades from which lots are rebuilt where that is safe, not as a replacement for the owner's dates. The first import is part of saving the account's Portfolio settings (ADR 0013, P2-01a); there is no separate import endpoint or dialog. The settings carry a first import date (firstImportDate, default 1 Jan 2025 , between 1990-01-01 and today India). When the account is connected, has the Portfolio role and has no holdings in the system, Save (PUT /api/accounts/{id}/portfolio) stores the settings and then imports in the same request, without any per-holding list on the settings screen : Dhan is fetched again inside one transaction, one holding with one lot (Dhan's quantity and average cost) is created for every holding Dhan lists, in the account's bucket (its own default bucket or the custom bucket chosen in the settings), dated with the first import date and flagged uses default date (counted on the Portfolio screen until its date is edited there). firstImport.rows = [{key, acquiredOn?}] is an optional API detail: a row's date overrides the default for its key. The run is recorded with the trigger import. The answer carries the result: import.status imported (counts imported, defaultDated, alreadyImported, unknown), skipped (the account already has holdings: nothing is imported, Dhan is not called; redated/redateSkipped say how many lots followed a changed first import date), deferred (not connected, or role off: nothing is called) or failed (the settings are still saved ; import.errorCode portfolio.import failed, import.cause the reason code such as portfolio.not connected or portfolio.sync running; retry with Save again or Sync now). Invalid dates or rows are 400 accounts.invalid (fields firstImportDate, firstImport.rows[i].key, firstImport.rows[i].acquiredOn) and nothing is saved. An account that is not connected keeps the stored first import date, and its first sync — manual or daily — performs the import: every holding Dhan lists gets one lot dated with the first import date (flagged as default, editable per lot) and the run is recorded as import. After that nothing imports again: saving the settings again imports nothing, and everything else arrives through the refresh below. The first import date stays editable (ADR 0015, P2-01b): saving a changed date on an account that has holdings re-dates, in the same unit of work, every lot with source = import and uses default date = true to the new date (the lots stay flagged; a closed lot whose closed on is before the new date cannot take it and is skipped). Lots the owner dated by hand, lots found by refreshes and manual lots are never touched; returns and XIRR are computed on read, so they follow. No Dhan call is made (import.status stays skipped); the answer's import carries redated and redateSkipped (lot counts, 0 when the date did not change or the account has no holdings). The same date again changes nothing. The change is audited once (FirstImportDateChanged: old and new date and the counts, no per-lot data). Without holdings only the date is stored. Lots. A holding has lots (acquired on, qty, unit cost); a closed lot also has closed on, close price and estimated. A partial sale splits a lot into a closed piece and an open remainder with the same date and cost, so every lot has one close price. The open quantities always add up to Dhan's quantity, and the holding's quantity and average cost are always Dhan's after a sync. Any lot's date can be edited later (PUT /api/holdings/{id}/lots/{lotId}); figures are computed on read, so they follow at once. Sync rules (\"Sync now\""},{"d":11,"h":"Trades and the rebuild of lots (P2-03, ADR 0023)","a":"trades-and-the-rebuild-of-lots-p2-03-adr-0023","t":"A broker tradebook (CSV) , trades typed in by hand or (P2-13) trades read from Dhan are stored as transactions . For CSV and manual trades a holding's lots are rebuilt from the real buy and sell dates on an explicit and previewed step; the Dhan sync rebuilds automatically where that is safe (below), so the previewed step is no longer the only path. Either way XIRR, returns, the value history and the benchmark use true dates and real sale prices (a sale closes a lot at its real price, estimated = false). The broker stays authoritative for the quantity and the total cost of a holding: trades can only explain a holding, never change what Dhan says is held. Transactions. portfolio.transactions: account, instrument (when mapped), ISIN, symbol, exchange, side buy/sell, India trade date and optional trade time, qty (whole shares, bigint), price (numeric(18,6) since P2-17, so a bill-level price keeps its value to the paisa), charges (one total: counted in the net returns since P2-14, as a separate line — not in the cost, the lots or the gross figures), source csv/manual/broker/bill (broker since P2-13, with a nullable run id; bill since P2-17: a bill-level row of an imported Dhan report, which the broker's own trades replace — a voided row may carry superseded by, §3 \"Dhan report imports (P2-17)\"), batch id, seq (the line of the file, 0 for a manual trade), external id, who/when and voided at/by. A transaction is immutable except for being voided — the runtime role has no DELETE. external id is the trade's identity and is unique per account among the trades that are not voided (a partial unique index), so a voided trade (or an undone file) can be imported again: the trade id of the file together with the trade date (t:yyyyMMdd: + id — exchanges number trades per day, so the same id on two days is two trades) when it has such a column, else a hash of (account, date, time, symbol or ISIN, side, quantity, price, order id, how many identical lines came before in the file) (h: + 32 hex), so two genuine identical trades are both kept and the same file read again adds nothing; a manual trade has a random identity (m:) and is never deduplicated. Import batches (portfolio.trade batches): file name, SHA-256 (the file itself is never stored), counts (parsed, imported, duplicates, skipped, bad), status imported/undone, who/when; a file that adds nothing new makes no batch. Neither table is ever deleted. The file (generic, auto-detected, UTF-8 with or without BOM, comma separated, at most 10 MB and 20,000 data rows). Headers match without regard to case or punctuation. Required: a date, a symbol or an ISIN, a side, a quantity, a price (a missing one refuses the file with 400 portfolio.invalid on field file, naming what is missing and the accepted names). Accepted names, among others — date: date, trade date, transaction date, order execution time; symbol: symbol, trading symbol, scrip, instrument, security, stock name; isin; side: trade type, side, buy/sell, transaction type; quantity: quantity, qty, traded quantity; price: price, trade price, rate, avg price; optional: exchange, trade id/trade no (the identity), order id (only part of the line hash), time, segment/instrument type (to skip non-equity rows), charges. Unknown columns are ignored. Dates: yyyy-MM-dd, dd-MM-yyyy, dd/MM/yyyy, dd-MMM-yyyy — day first, never MM/dd (13/25/2024 is a bad row) — optionally followed by an India time; a date in the future is a bad row. Numbers take . for decimals and , only to group thousands (1,234.50, 12,34,567). Side: buy/sell/b/s/purchase/sale. Symbols are upper-cased and trimmed; NSE: and -EQ are stripped. Quantity must be a whole number above 0, price above 0. Charges: a total charges/charges column is used as it is, otherwise the component columns (brokerage, STT, stamp duty, GST, taxes, …) are summed, never both. Rows for derivatives, commodity or currency segments (by the segment/instrument-type/exchange column, or by the shape of the symbol: expiry, s"},{"d":11,"h":"Per group (basket / bucket / account / strategy / sector / total)","a":"per-group-basket-bucket-account-strategy-sector-total","t":"Invested · current value · realised · unrealised · total return % (with dividends, P2-14) · XIRR · dividends · charges · net total return % · net XIRR (P2-14, the income object below) · day change (sum of the holdings' ₹; % = Σ₹ / Σ qty × previous close of the included holdings, not an average of percentages; holdings without a previous close are left out and counted as dayChangeExcluded) · number of open positions · win rate & expectancy of closed trades · benchmark XIRR (same cash flows invested in Nifty 50) and alpha (XIRR − benchmark XIRR) · max drawdown of the group's value history. Built in P2-07 for the total, one account and one bucket: see Value history, benchmark and drawdown in §4. Built in P2-08 (ADR 0021): invested, value, unrealised P&L (₹, %), realised, XIRR, day change, counts and weight for groups by account, bucket, sector or basket (below); the group benchmark XIRR / alpha / max drawdown, win rate and expectancy, and grouping by strategy are not built yet. Holding groups (P2-08, ADR 0021) GET /api/holdings/groups?by=account bucket sector basket&accountId&bucketId&status&q&sort&stackBy partitions the holdings of the scope in memory and applies the same pure calculators as the summary to each group (Application/Portfolio/Groups: HoldingPartitions, GroupCalculator, over ReturnsCalculator and DayChangeCalculator) — so a group, the summary and a holding agree. Session only, read-only, no table, no job. - Scope. The filters of the holdings list (q, status: open by default) apply before grouping; without accountId only the accounts that count in totals (includeInTotals) are read, exactly like the summary — accounts with includeInTotals = false are in no group, not in the total and not in the children of a group, while the flat holdings list (no groupBy) still shows them. An unknown accountId gives 200 with no groups (as the list does; the summary answers 404), so for the additive groupings (account, bucket, sector; additive: true) Σ group invested / value / P&L equals the summary's for the same filters (status=all for the summary, which includes closed holdings). The weight of a group is its value over the scope's total value of priced holdings (weightPct, percent number). - Per group {key, name, holdings, unpriced, invested, value, pnl, pnlPct, realised, xirr, xirrState, dayChange, dayChangePct, dayChangeExcluded, weightPct, parts, income} and a total row for the whole scope (the same fields without key, name and parts). income (P2-14) is the object of §3 \"Dividends, charges and net returns\", null when nothing is recorded for the group. XIRR is the XIRR of the union of the group's cash flows , never an average; xirrState is ok insufficient n m no data, or no price. Unpriced open holdings are left out of invested, value, P&L and XIRR exactly as in the summary and counted in unpriced; a group made only of them — and the total when the whole scope is unpriced — has value, pnl, pnlPct, xirr, weightPct null, invested 0 and xirrState: \"no price\" (its worth is unknown, not zero; this is what a holding row shows, whereas the summary of such a scope shows value 0 and no data / insufficient). The day change is DayChangeCalculator over the group (holdings without a previous close are counted in dayChangeExcluded, never taken as zero). - Keys. The key is the account id, bucket id, basket id or the sector name; none is reserved for Unclassified (a holding not in the master, or whose instrument has no sector — the master's sector, doc 06) and No basket ; it cannot clash with a real key (a master sector spelled none, in any case, is deliberately treated as Unclassified). Keys are compared ignoring case; when the master spells one sector two ways (Banks / banks) they are one group shown under the Ordinal-smallest spelling, whatever order the holdings were read in. - Baskets are a lens. A holding is in every live basket that holds its instrument today (archived baskets are left out; the portfolio's system baskets are a view of the"},{"d":11,"h":"4. Returns and XIRR","a":"4-returns-and-xirr","t":"Computed on read from the lots by pure functions (XDrishti.Application/Portfolio/Returns), valued on today (India) at the holding's last price: Figure Definition Invested Σ open lot qty × unit cost Value Σ open lot qty × last price Unrealised P&L ₹ / % value − invested; P&L / invested × 100 (null when nothing is invested) Realised P&L Σ closed lot qty × (close price − unit cost); realisedEstimated when any sale price is a last-seen price Day change \"—\" until a previous-close source exists (P2-07) Group (total / account / bucket) Sums of the above; the group XIRR comes from the union of all member cash flows , never an average Total return % (P2-14) (realised + unrealised + dividends) ÷ invested × 100; invested is the cost of the open lots (§3), realised profit is in the numerator Net total return % (P2-14) the same with the charges subtracted in the numerator XIRR cash flows: each lot −qty × unit cost on its acquired on; each closed lot also +qty × close price on its closed on; plus +current value on the valuation date. XIRR is the rate r where Σ CFᵢ / (1 + r)^((dᵢ − d₀) / 365) = 0 - Newton–Raphson from a good first guess (growth of everything paid in into everything received over the money-weighted period); if it does not converge, bisection on [−0.9999, 100] (when the ends have the same sign, on the sign change nearest the guess); tolerance 1e-9. It follows the definition of a spreadsheet's XIRR (days / 365 from the first date) and agrees to better than 1e-6 with reference values from an independent 60-digit decimal bisection written in Python — not with Excel itself — on the committed sample sets (XirrTests). - States: ok; no price (the holding has no usable price — a last price of 0 — so priced is false and value, P&L, P&L % and XIRR are null: its worth is unknown, it is not a total loss; such holdings are left out of group and summary invested / value / P&L / XIRR and counted in summary.unpricedCount; realised results of their closed lots still count); insufficient (shown \"—\": no outflow or no inflow, less than 30 days between the first and the last flow, or no solution in the range); n m (shown \"n/m\": the rate is beyond ±1,000 % a year, also when it is above the solver's range); no data (no flows). - Computed for any set of cash flows: one lot, a holding, a bucket, an account, the total. Percentages in the API are percent numbers (16.1 = 16.1 %). - Example: buy ₹1,00,000 on 1 Jan; add ₹50,000 on 1 Apr; value ₹1,72,000 on 31 Dec → XIRR ≈ 16.1% (absolute return 14.7%). - Net XIRR (P2-14, ADR 0026). The XIRR of the same lot cash flows as above plus each dividend as money in on its pay date and each charge as money out on its date (a refund, a negative charge, as money in), with the terminal value — today's value of what is held — as for the gross XIRR (the valuation date, at the holding's last price). Same states and rules as the gross XIRR (insufficient, n m, no data, no price; less than 30 days or no flow in one direction is insufficient). Only the flows of priced holdings are used: a holding without a usable price leaves out its lot flows, its dividends and its trade charges alike (the gross XIRR already leaves it out; they still count in the dividends and charges sums), while the account-level items (unallocated dividends, trade charges of no holding, ledger costs) of a scope that includes them are always part of it; a scope whose holdings are all unpriced is no price. A holding's net XIRR uses its own dividends and trade charges. A scope that mixes priced and unpriced holdings (R4, P2-18): the dividends and charges of an unpriced holding are in the dividends and charges sums and in the total-return and net-total-return percentages, but not in the net XIRR : its lots have no value to put on the other side of the flows, so its income would lift the rate of the holdings that do have one (IncomeCalculatorTests pins it). A dividend dated before the first lot or a charge dated after the last flow is just another flow on its "},{"d":11,"h":"Value history, benchmark and drawdown (P2-07, ADR 0020)","a":"value-history-benchmark-and-drawdown-p2-07-adr-0020","t":"Computed on read, with no snapshot table and no job, by pure functions in Application/Portfolio/Returns (PortfolioHistoryCalculator, NavSeries, DrawdownCalculator, BenchmarkReturns, AdjustedCloses, HistoryResolutions) from the lots and the stored 1D aggregates. - Held on a day. A lot is held on d when acquired on ≤ d and (closed on is null or d). Value(d) = Σ qty × adjusted close(d); Invested(d) = Σ qty × unit cost of the same lots. A lot bought and sold before a trading day comes never counts. A day without a close carries the last earlier close forward. The dates are the calendar's trading days from the earliest lot to the last stored close, plus today's live point (prices of today from a quote or the last sync, from the session's open, only when such prices exist; it replaces a stored close of the same day). - Adjusted prices. Stored closes are raw; the applied corporate actions of each instrument (one read for all instruments) multiply the closes before an ex-date by their factor (ADR 0017 §5), so a lot's value is continuous across a split or bonus and an action the owner confirms changes the next read. - Coverage. An open holding without any stored day bar (not tracked, not in the master, bars not fetched) is left out and counted (coverage.unpriced), the series is partial; it is never valued at zero. A lot acquired before its instrument's first stored close counts from that close (coverage.lateStart), not at an invented price. Closed holdings without bars make the series partial without being counted. - Benchmark (default master-list Nifty 50, NSE:NIFTY; stored in platform.app settings). The same cash flows go into the index: a purchase (at its cost) buys units at that day's close, a sale (at its proceeds) sells units, at most the units held (the benchmark never goes short); BenchmarkValue = units × close, the index close carried forward. If the index has no close on or before the first flow the benchmark is unavailable (reason given), never zero. Benchmark XIRR = XIRR of the flows of the priced holdings (the same set as the portfolio XIRR) with the benchmark's value at the valuation date (last close on or before it) as the closing inflow; alpha = XIRR − benchmark XIRR in percentage points. States ok unavailable insufficient n m (summary field benchmarkState); alpha only when both rates are ok. The index has no live quote: on a live day the benchmark lines end at the index's last close (benchmark.asOf; the live point has no benchmark value, the benchmark NAV and drawdown use the days the index has), the summary reports benchmarkAsOf, and alphaBasis is last close or live vs last close (the portfolio at prices of a later trading day than that close). Chart and XIRR put a cash flow into the index at the close of the first trading day on or after its date; a lot bought and sold before a trading day comes leaves no flows. - NAV (unit method) and max drawdown. The first NAV is 100. NAV(d) = value of what was held the day before, at d's prices ÷ units issued so far (the previous NAV when no units exist). The day's net flow (purchases − sales, valued at d's close, so it does not move the NAV) issues flow ÷ NAV(d) units; all units are cancelled when nothing is held and the NAV carries on from where it was. Max drawdown = max over d of (peak − NAV(d)) / peak × 100 with the peak and trough dates (pct is a fall of pct %; flat or rising gives 0); the benchmark's NAV and drawdown use the same method. A deposit during a drawdown therefore neither hides nor deepens it. The drawdown is measured within the requested range (the peak must lie inside it; rangeFrom / rangeTo are its first and last day), on daily points whatever the resolution. - API. GET /api/portfolio/history?accountId&bucketId&from&to&resolution → points {date, value, invested, benchmarkValue, nav, benchmarkNav}, coverage, partial, benchmark, maxDrawdown, benchmarkMaxDrawdown, firstDate, lastPriceAt, resolution; week / month keep the last trading day of the period; at most 2"},{"d":11,"h":"5. Grouping: baskets vs portfolio buckets","a":"5-grouping-baskets-vs-portfolio-buckets","t":"Concept Overlap Use Instrument baskets (doc 06) An instrument can be in many baskets Analysis lens — totals not additive Portfolio bucket Each position belongs to exactly one bucket Additive view — bucket totals sum to the portfolio - One default bucket per Portfolio account, no presets. Every account that has the Portfolio role gets a bucket named \"{account name} · Portfolio\" (e.g. Dhan · Main · Portfolio ), created automatically when the role is switched on (or the account is created with it), linked to the account (default for account id, unique), renamed when the account is renamed, archived with the account (renamed … · Portfolio (archived) so the name is free again), and kept — but hidden from pickers — while the role is off. A name collision gets a numeric suffix ( … · Portfolio 2 ). It is where the account's imported holdings go unless another bucket is chosen. There are no predefined buckets (the earlier Trading / MyLongTerm / MyChoice presets were removed in P1-16e). - Custom buckets: the buckets API stays (GET/POST /api/portfolio-buckets, defaultBucket on the settings), but Accounts no longer offers any bucket choice — no default-bucket select, no Buckets list, no Add bucket form, in the Portfolio tab or the add-account wizard; saves leave defaultBucket out, so imports go to the account's own default bucket and a custom bucket set earlier is kept (ADR 0015). - A position's bucket defaults to the account's chosen bucket and can be overridden per trade — e.g. the same stock bought for long-term (custom bucket Long term ) and for a swing trade (the account's own bucket) is tracked as two positions with separate lots, returns and XIRR. - System-plan trades go to the Trading account's own default bucket unless a custom bucket is configured for plan trades. Views: tree — Total → Account → Bucket → Position → Lots/Transactions; pivot — rows by bucket, basket, account, strategy or sector with the metrics in §3. Built in P2-08 (ADR 0021): the pivot for account, bucket, sector and basket (GET /api/holdings/groups, §3) and the holdings of a group as its children (groupBy / groupKey on GET /api/holdings); accounts, buckets and sectors are additive (a holding is in exactly one), baskets are the lens (additive: false, a holding in every live basket that holds it, plus No basket ). Strategy follows when plans and positions exist."},{"d":11,"h":"6. Live updates","a":"6-live-updates","t":"What Update method Frequency Open intraday & swing positions, armed tickets Dhan WebSocket (active set) Real-time Long-term holdings Dhan quote API (up to 1,000 instruments/request) Every holdings refresh minutes (default 5) Portfolio snapshot EOD official close Daily after close Built (P2-06, ADR 0019): the quote poll of the long-term holdings is the service holdings-quotes (the first interval service: every 5 min from 09:15 to 15:30 IST on trading days, retries 0, timeout 2 min, catch-up off, a tick that falls while the previous run goes on is dropped with a log line and no run record). It writes last price, previous close, last price at and last price source = quote into every open holding of a quoted instrument in one statement; a holdings sync keeps a quote of the same India day. The Portfolio screen re-reads once a minute while the market is open (with the live feed and the hub of P2-10a it re-reads every 5 minutes and on reconnect, and once a minute while the hub is not connected). Built (P2-10, ADR 0024): the live feed (xd-feed, doc 05 §5) writes the last live price of every open holding into the same price columns every 2 seconds in market hours (last price source = live, price time = tick time), so value, P&L, XIRR, day change, the Action center and the price alerts need no change; the 5-minute quote poll stays and still owns previous close. GET /api/holdings rows report priceSource = live. Source precedence: a holdings sync keeps a quote or live price of the same India day; a live price replaces a price only when it is newer (and not a pre-open tick over a same-day quote); a quote does not overwrite a newer live price but still writes the previous close. The live table market.live quotes holds the last price of the indices and alert-only instruments. Day change on a live price counts only when the previous close was written (by a quote, previous close at) on the same India day as the price time; otherwise the holding is left out of day change and counted in dayChangeExcluded, so a live price is never set against a two-session-old close (first minutes of the day, or a failed quote poll). Ticks carry whole-second times: a tick with the same time and another price replaces the older one. The screen part is P2-10a (below). The React Portfolio screen receives updates via SignalR (/hubs/events, P2-10a); values recalculate incrementally. Income on a live price (P2-14, ADR 0026). Dividends, charges and the coverage dates do not change with a price, so the browser's pure twins (incomeAt in liveFigures.ts, the twin of IncomeCalculator) move only the two return percentages with a pushed price: totalReturnPct = (realised + live profit + dividends) ÷ invested and netTotalReturnPct = the same less the charges, rounded once like the server. The net XIRR stays as the last read gave it (like the gross XIRR, benchmark and counts) and is exact again at the 5-minute read. The golden fixtures (from the real calculators, now schema 3: they carry income on rows, tiles, groups and totals, including ledgerCharges, the unallocated group and dividends, trade charges and ledger costs) and the 150-seed property test compare the twins with the server by exact equality (toBe). Built (P2-10a, backend; ADR 0024 §7): the hub pushes, to a screen that subscribed with its scope, the changed prices of exactly the instruments of that scope - the open holdings of the accounts and bucket the summary is made of, plus the holdings the screen shows - as pricesChanged (instrument id, last price, price time, source live / quote / sync), within about a second of the feed's flush (a burst is coalesced to one message a second), and the feed's state as feedStatus (GET /api/live/status is its REST twin for when the hub is not connected). The price time lets the screen apply the same rules as the server: the day change of a live price counts only against a previous close written the same India day, and a holding whose source is not live is shown as \"not live\". Quote "},{"d":11,"h":"7. Alerts","a":"7-alerts","t":"Built in P2-09 (ADR 0022): price alerts on NSE shares and ETFs (/api/portfolio/alerts; one-shot, checked on the last price of every quotes tick, so up to 5 minutes late — doc 15 §4) and the bell notices price alert triggered and actions need decision (GET /api/portfolio/notices). The other alerts below need data that does not exist yet (plans, events). Price reaches a plan's stop/target level · swing position at max holding (5 sessions) · results date within N days for a holding · position weight max position weight pct · bucket drawdown threshold · large day move on a holding · data or token issue for an account."},{"d":11,"h":"8. Link to the trading system","a":"8-link-to-the-trading-system","t":"- Positions created from plan tickets keep plan item id → strategy, cell, R-multiple and grading. - Trades not matching any plan are tagged discretionary ; their results are reported separately (and in the discipline report). - Long-term holdings are not managed by the system's exits unless you assign a strategy/exit to them."},{"d":11,"h":"9. Data model (portfolio schema)","a":"9-data-model-portfolio-schema","t":"Built in P2-01 / P2-05 (migration AddPortfolioHoldings): portfolio.holdings (account, bucket, optional instrument id, exchange, security id, ISIN, symbol, name, qty, avg cost, last price and time, status open/closed, first seen / closed / synced), portfolio.holding lots (holding, acquired on, qty, unit cost, source import/added/manual, uses default date, closed on, close price, estimated), portfolio.holding syncs (account, trigger manual/auto/import, status running/succeeded/failed/skipped, counts, error, requested by; one running row per account) and the refresh columns of accounts.portfolio settings (refresh mode off/daily, refresh daily time ist, and first import date since migration PortfolioDailyRefreshAndFirstImportDate, ADR 0013). Built in P2-06 (migration AddHoldingQuotes): portfolio.holdings.previous close (numeric(18,4), null until a quote gave one; a sync never touches it) and last price source (sync quote, default sync, CHECK), next to last price / last price at — only the latest quote is stored, no price history. Built in P2-07 (migration AddPortfolioBenchmarkSetting): platform.app settings (key, value, updated by, updated at; never deleted by the runtime role) holds the benchmark instrument (portfolio.benchmark instrument, an index id); valuations daily is not built, the history is computed on read. Built in P2-09 (migration AddActionCenterAndPriceAlerts): portfolio.action states (rule, key, state snoozed/done/cleared, snoozed until, updated by, updated at; primary key (rule, key)) holds the owner's decisions about Action-center items, portfolio.price alerts (id, instrument id, direction at or below/at or above, level numeric(18,4), note, status active/triggered/cancelled, created at/by, triggered at, trigger price; index on (status, instrument id)) the one-shot price alerts; neither is ever deleted by the runtime role (a decision is cleared and an alert triggered or cancelled by an update); the thresholds of the rules are one more platform.app settings row (portfolio.action thresholds, JSON). Built in P2-03 (migration AddTradeTransactions, ADR 0023): portfolio.transactions (id, account id, optional instrument id, ISIN, symbol, exchange, side buy/sell, trade date, trade time, qty bigint 0, price numeric(18,4) 0, charges ≥ 0, source csv/manual, batch id, seq, external id, created by/at, voided at/by; a partial unique index on (account id, external id) where voided at IS NULL; indexes on account × symbol / ISIN and on the batch) and portfolio.trade batches (id, account id, file name, SHA-256, parsed / imported / duplicates / skipped / bad counts, status imported/undone, created by/at, undone at); holding lots.source gains trade (check constraint widened). The runtime role may read, insert and update the two new tables and can never delete from them (holding lots keeps its DELETE: a rebuild replaces the lots of a holding). The target model's transactions row below is realised by the first table; its lots and lot closures stay holding lots (a closed piece is a closed lot). Built in P2-13 (migration AddDhanTradeAndLedgerSync, ADR 0025): portfolio.transactions.source gains broker (check constraint widened) and a nullable run id (foreign key to the run, partial index); portfolio.broker sync runs (id, account id, kind trades/ledger, trigger manual/scheduled/first, from date, to date, status running/succeeded/failed/skipped, counts jsonb, started by/at, finished at, error; a unique partial index allows one running row per account across both kinds), portfolio.broker sync state (account id, kind, watermark date, updated at; primary key (account id, kind)) and portfolio.ledger entries (id, account id, entry date, voucher no, description, narration ≤ 500, exchange, amount numeric(18,2) signed, class funds in/funds out/dividend/charges/other, running balance, external id unique per account , run id, created at). The runtime role may read, insert and update the three new tables and has no DELETE on them (nor on transactions). "},{"d":11,"h":"10. Portfolio screen (React)","a":"10-portfolio-screen-react","t":"- Account tiles (one per Portfolio account + \"All accounts\"): value, unrealised P&L (₹, %), day change, XIRR, open positions, realised P&L, top holding and its weight, number of open action items — click to focus the whole page on that account. - Action center: positions that need a decision, per account — below cost, large gain, overweight, sector concentration, max holding exceeded, results due, long-term soon, lagging Nifty, not in a basket — with Open / Set alert / Add to basket / Snooze / Done (doc 15 §3). P2-09 (API built; card in the frontend part of the story): seven of the nine rules, evaluated on read by GET /api/portfolio/actions (status open/snoozed/done/all, the account and bucket scope, counts per rule and severity, \"not evaluated\" holdings), decisions by POST /api/portfolio/actions/{id}/snooze done reopen, thresholds by GET/PUT /api/portfolio/action-thresholds; the per-account tiles above are not part of that slice. - Header tiles: total value · invested · total P&L (₹, %) · XIRR · day change · benchmark XIRR / alpha / max drawdown (API built in P2-07). Live (P2-10a): value, P&L, day change and the weights of rows, group rows and tiles move with pricesChanged of the hub (§6); the prices line says Live , Pre-open , Delayed , Quotes or Closed from the hub's feedStatus and the times of the prices, and the summary notes how many holdings are not live. The numbers are re-read exactly every 5 minutes and after a reconnect. Income tiles (P2-14, built): under the gross tiles a second row shows Dividends (with \"from <date \" and, when there are any, the unallocated part), Costs (trade charges and the ledger's account-level costs, \"from <date \"), Net total return (with the figure before costs) and Net XIRR , each saying from which date it is complete (§3 \"Dividends, charges and net returns\"). While nothing is recorded (income null) there are no zeros: the tiles are replaced by an invitation — Add a dividend and Read the ledger — and the note under the tiles says the figures are price returns. Alpha, the benchmark and the value history stay price returns and say so. - Group by: Bucket · Basket · Account · Strategy · Sector · None — with tree-grid (expand to lots and transactions) and column chooser. P2-14 (built): a sortable Dividends column in the group table and the holdings grid (hidden below md; \"—\" when nothing was entered, not zero), and, for the dividends and costs that belong to no holding, an Unallocated row (key unallocated, last, not expandable) so the column adds up to the total; at the default open-only status the groups leave out the dividends and trade charges of closed holdings, which the summary tiles include. P2-08 (API built; screen in the frontend part of the story): None · Account · Bucket · Sector · Basket, group rows that expand to their holdings (GET /api/holdings/groups, groupBy / groupKey on the holdings list, §3), a total row, and the note \"Baskets overlap — totals do not add up to the portfolio\" on the basket view; strategy, lots as tree levels and the column chooser come later. - Charts: allocation by group, sector exposure stacked by account, portfolio value vs the same cash flows in Nifty 50 (API built in P2-07), XIRR by group. P2-08: the data of the allocation donut (the groups' value / weightPct) and of the sector-by-account stacked bar (stackBy → parts) is served by GET /api/holdings/groups. - Price alerts: create from the Action center or a position; evaluated by the services; list in an Alerts panel. P2-09 (API built): GET/POST /api/portfolio/alerts, POST /api/portfolio/alerts/{id}/cancel; checked by the quotes service every 5 minutes in market hours. - Position drawer: the holding's income facts (dividends and charges with their dates, total return with dividends, net total return, net XIRR) and a Dividends section ( P2-14, built : the dividends entered for the holding, newest first, Add dividend , Void after asking; a voided dividend stays in the list and stops counting); transaction ti"},{"d":12,"h":"1. Screens","a":"1-screens","t":"Screen Purpose Key components Login / lock screen One centred panel over a dotted background: the hero (brand, headline, what the app does) on the left, form on the right; below 860 px the form card comes first and the hero shrinks to its brand row. P2-12: the React page shows no live status before sign-in (no fabricated or leaked service data); the prototype's status line stays a prototype-only demo. Show/hide password, Caps Lock warning, attempts left, live lockout countdown. Idle or manual lock keeps the user and asks only for the password (\"Welcome back · Unlock\"), and survives a page reload; password re-entry for sensitive actions Form, status strip Today Plan status, live open positions & P&L, armed tickets, alerts, briefing, services health KPI tiles, ticket cards, live price panel (SignalR) Plan review Approve / modify / reject proposed tickets before cutoff; reserve list; limits check Ticket cards with chart, bulk approve, countdown to cutoff Suggestions AI / engine suggestions inbox (track instrument, basket changes, promotions) Cards with reason & evidence, accept / reject Portfolio All holdings/trades across accounts: added date, value, return %, XIRR; group by bucket / basket / account / strategy / sector (doc 12). Built in P2-01 / P2-05 (holdings, dates, returns — ADR 0012); grouping by strategy, the treemap and live prices follow in later P2 stories; grouping by account / bucket / sector / basket is served by GET /api/holdings/groups since P2-08 (ADR 0021, doc 12 §3; the screen follows in the frontend part of the story) ; the value history, benchmark XIRR / alpha and max drawdown API is built in P2-07 (ADR 0020, doc 12 §4); the Action center (seven rules, decisions, thresholds), price alerts and the bell notices are served by /api/portfolio/actions, /api/portfolio/action-thresholds, /api/portfolio/alerts and /api/portfolio/notices since P2-09 (ADR 0022, doc 15 §3-§4; the cards, the Alerts panel and the Settings card follow in the frontend part of the story) ; trade import (a broker tradebook CSV or trades by hand, stored as transactions) and the rebuild of a holding's lots from the real buy and sell dates are served by /api/portfolio/trades/ , /api/portfolio/trade-batches and /api/holdings/{id}/trades, /api/holdings/{id}/rebuild-lots since P2-03 (ADR 0023, doc 12 §3; the Trades drawer and the holding's Trades section follow in the frontend part of the story) ; the trades and the ledger of a Dhan account are read from Dhan since P2-13 (POST /api/accounts/{id}/sync/broker-history, GET …/status, GET /api/accounts/{id}/ledger; ADR 0025, doc 12 §3 \"Dhan sync (P2-13)\"): the sync lines and the Sync trades & ledger button in the sync bar, the Cash & dividends drawer and the Dhan-marked trades of the holding's Trades section are built with it; dividends and charges are part of the net returns since P2-14 (ADR 0026; POST /api/portfolio/dividends, GET /api/holdings/{id}/dividends, GET /api/accounts/{id}/dividends, the income object of the summary, rows and groups; the income tiles, the Add dividend form, the Dividends column and the holding drawer's Dividends section are built with it; a dividend payout file is imported with Import dividends since P2-15 (ADR 0028; POST /api/portfolio/dividends/preview and /import, GET /api/portfolio/dividend-batches, POST /api/portfolio/dividend-batches/{id}/undo)); the syncs heal themselves and what needs the owner arrives as an actionable item in the bell and as a pop-up since P2-16 (ADR 0027; GET /api/portfolio/notices), and Dhan's downloaded reports are imported on Import files since P2-17 (ADR 0029; POST /api/portfolio/imports/preview and /import, GET /api/portfolio/cost-batches, POST /api/portfolio/cost-batches/{id}/undo) — see Portfolio screen (P2-01 / P2-05) and Notifications and pop-ups (P2-16) below Header tiles, grid, position drawer (treemap and benchmark chart later) Instruments Master-list search, tracked instruments, coverage & quality, bulk actions (doc 06). Built in P1-03 "},{"d":12,"h":"1b. Clickable prototype","a":"1b-clickable-prototype","t":"A working front-end prototype of these screens lives in prototype/index.html (open it directly in a browser — offline, demo data, no backend; services and the live feed are simulated in the browser). It demonstrates the login (demo credentials prefilled); Accounts (list/detail tabs, role matrix, risk profiles, wizard, data failover); plan review with approve / modify / reject, the pre-trade checklist and auto-approval at the cutoff ; suggestions inbox; portfolio with per-account tiles, Action center, price alerts, XIRR and animated grouping; instruments (one scrollable grid with sticky header and symbol column) & baskets; CSV import with aggregation; the Data quality window; the Learning screen (journey, lessons with loss tags, plan vs actual with fills ledger, setups, smart rules, tweak & re-run, autonomy, weekly digest); live tickets filled with simulated slippage and graded on fills; Reports (overview with auto-insights, basket comparison, pivot and 10 more reports); Strategy Lab; Services with schedule configuration, dependency checks, timeline and catch-up; settings with password re-entry; the audit log — responsive from phones to 4K monitors, light and dark. Visual language (carried into the React app): Inter font and a standard, calm palette (Tailwind slate/gray neutrals with one indigo accent; green/red/amber only for meaning), a light sidebar with a soft colour-tinted icon per screen, tiles with tinted icons and count-up numbers, collapsible cards, animated dialogs/drawers/toasts (respecting reduce motion ), light and dark themes, and scrollable data grids with sticky headers instead of pagination. In MUI this is one theme object (palette, shape, typography) shared by every screen. Navigation for speed: a command palette ( ⌘K / Ctrl K or / ) searches pages, Learning tabs, actions (run nightly, digest, lock, theme), baskets and every instrument (opens its chart drawer); g + letter jumps to a page (g t Today, g r Plan review, g p Portfolio, g l Learning …); ? lists all shortcuts; the browser tab title shows the current screen."},{"d":12,"h":"1c. Reports experience","a":"1c-reports-experience","t":"- Landing = Overview: KPI tiles (trades, win rate, expectancy, total R & ₹, profit factor, max drawdown), auto-insights in plain sentences (doc 15 §6), equity & drawdown, monthly results, strategy × timeframe heatmap, expectancy by basket, R distribution, win rate by weekday. - Left-hand catalogue: Overview · Basket comparison · Strategies · Exit policies · Side & regime · Calendar & time · MAE/MFE · Calibration · Instruments · Accounts · Decisions · Pivot builder · Trade list. - Global filters: date range with presets, multi-select baskets , side, style, account. - Basket comparison: choose 2+ baskets → side-by-side KPIs (trades, win rate, expectancy, total R, profit factor, max drawdown, net ₹, best strategy, buy-and-hold return), overlaid cumulative-R curves, expectancy by strategy per basket, basket × side and basket × style heatmaps. - Pivot builder: any two dimensions (strategy, timeframe, basket, side, style, exit, regime, account, instrument, sector, weekday, month, decision) × metric (expectancy, total R, win rate, trades, profit factor)."},{"d":12,"h":"1d. Responsive design (all monitors)","a":"1d-responsive-design-all-monitors","t":"Width Layout < 480 px (phones) Single column, 2-up KPI tiles, larger touch targets, off-canvas navigation 481–860 px (small tablets) Single column, off-canvas navigation, wrapping filters 861–1200 px (tablets / small laptops) Icon-only sidebar (expandable), 1–2 column grids 1201–1679 px (laptops / desktops) Full sidebar (collapsible), 2–3 column grids 1680–2559 px (large / QHD) No max-width cap, 2-column plan tickets, taller charts, larger base font ≥ 2560 px (4K / ultrawide) Up to 4 columns of tickets/cards, larger charts and font Rules: no horizontal page scroll at any width (wide tables scroll inside their card), fluid grids (auto-fit/minmax), charts resize with their containers, sidebar state remembered per device."},{"d":12,"h":"1e. Visual system (P1-19)","a":"1e-visual-system-p1-19","t":"Calm, data-first, trustworthy: cool-neutral base, one deliberate accent. Tokens live in frontend/src/theme/tokens.ts and the prototype's proto.css; the MUI theme and the prototype read the same values. All text pairs meet WCAG AA (4.5:1), UI parts 3:1 — enforced by theme/contrast.test.ts. Token Light Dark Role background f4f6fa 0b1220 app canvas (60 %) surface ffffff 131c2e cards, dialogs, tables surfaceAlt f1f4f9 182238 table header, alternate rows, hovered links, segmented tray surfaceStrong e8edf4 202c46 neutral chips, progress tracks, hovered controls border / borderStrong dfe3ea / c9d0dc 26324a / 34415c hairlines (cards, table rows, dividers) / scrollbars, hovered buttons inputBorder 7b8798 5f6f8a input outlines, unchecked checkbox/radio, switch track (3:1+ on surface, surfaceAlt and background) text 0f172a e6eaf2 primary text textSecondary 475569 9aa7bd captions, helper text textTertiary 5b6b82 7d8aa3 hints, sidebar section labels, search button accent 4338ca 9aa5ff primary actions, links, selection, focus accentContrast ffffff 0b1220 text on accent fills accentSoft eceefe 222a52 selected nav/rows, info banners, primary chips good / bad / warn / info 146c36 / b91c1c / a35200 / 0e7490 4ade80 / f87171 / fbbf24 / 67e8f9 gains / losses / warnings / information goodSoft / badSoft / warnSoft / infoSoft e9f8ef / fdecec / fff5e6 / e6f6f9 10281d / 2f1a1d / 2d2513 / 15303a tonal chips and banners (the colour as text on its tint is 4.5:1, tested) Type role Size / weight Use h1 / h2 / h3 24/750, 16.5/700, 14.5/650 (tracking -0.02 to -0.005em) page, dialog / section, card titles body1 / body2 14/1.5, 13/1.5 text, dense data caption / overline 12/1.5, 11/700 uppercase +0.06em helper text, section labels button 14/550 (12.5 small, 15 large) actions numeric tabular-nums + cv11, zero tables, stat tiles, countdowns, header counts mono JetBrains Mono Variable ids, URLs, code Fonts: Inter Variable and JetBrains Mono Variable, self-hosted (latin + latin-ext), font-display: swap, metric-matched fallbacks. The root font size is 14 px (html at 87.5 %, htmlFontSize: 14): every rem in the theme is px / 14 (rem(px) in tokens.ts). Rules: - 60-30-10 : 60 % neutral backgrounds, 30 % surfaces and text, at most 10 % accent. The accent is for primary actions, links, selection and focus - never for gain/loss (use good/bad plus a sign or arrow; status is never colour alone). - Tabular figures for every number column; right-align numbers. - Focus ring : 2 px accent outline, 2 px offset, on every interactive element. - Elevation : soft cool shadows in light, border-led in dark; motion : 120 ms (hover/press) and 200 ms (panels), honouring prefers-reduced-motion. - Table rows 36 px, header on surfaceAlt (small uppercase caps), alternate rows tinted, cell padding 9 / 12; selected nav item on accentSoft with a 3 px accent marker flush to the sidebar edge."},{"d":12,"h":"Prototype parity (P2-12)","a":"prototype-parity-p2-12","t":"The prototype's spacing, type and colour were audited against the app (spec P2-12, Audit result ) and brought into the theme (tokens.ts, componentsControls.ts, componentsSurfaces.ts, globalStyles.ts) and the shell (AppShell, TopBar, Sidebar, NavRow, PageHeader, StatTile), never per screen. The two large causes of the heavier look were the 16 px root (the scale is written for 14) and MUI 9 ignoring color=\"text.secondary\" on Typography (now mapped in the theme). Values: page padding 14 / 24 / 28·36 / 32·48 by width; top bar 60 px; controls 36 px (small 30, large 44) with radius 10 (8); icon buttons 36 square; cards 12 px radius with the soft shadow, body 14 / 16, header 13 / 16; tonal pill chips (22 px); banners tinted with a 22–25 % hairline; segmented control tray; dialog bands on the surface with hairlines. Deliberately not copied: the prototype's 1560 px page cap (screens are full width), the 861–1200 px icon rail, hover lifts and native checkbox / switch sizes. The sign-in page is the prototype's two-panel layout without the status line."},{"d":12,"h":"Icons (P1-20)","a":"icons-p1-20","t":"Every icon sits in a chip : a rounded square tinted from its own colour pair, so colour carries meaning at a glance while the page stays calm (the accent still means \"action\"). Tokens: iconHues, iconChip, navHue in tokens.ts; the prototype reads the same pairs from ui.js (NAVSTYLE, C). Size Box / icon / radius Where sm 28 / 17 / 8 px sidebar items, page-header icon, card and section headers, header accounts menu rows, toasts md 34 / 17 / 10 px stat tile (top right) lg 40 / 22 / 12 px feature cards (lesson cards) - Icon colour: the hue's strong value in light mode, its light value in dark mode. Chip background: that colour at 12 % (light) / 16 % (dark) over the surface. - Chips are decorative: the label always sits next to them; a chip has assistive text only when it stands alone. - Selected sidebar item keeps the accentSoft row and accent marker; its chip keeps its own hue. Planned (coming soon) items are coloured too. - Tile hues follow keywords: money/value indigo, cash/funds amber, gains/ok green, loss/risk rose, time/day sky, data/learning teal, returns violet, automation pink, history slate. - Header chips reuse the sm size (28/17 px), and tile and header tints are unified at 12 % (light) / 16 % (dark): a deliberate deviation from the prototype's 26 px / 18 % header and 11 % / 15 % tile values. - Toasts (restyled in P2-16, ADR 0027): a rounded card with a coloured accent bar on its left edge and the severity's soft tint — success green, info indigo, warning amber, error rose, from the theme tokens in light and dark mode — an icon chip, an optional bold title, the message in secondary text, up to two text buttons and a close button (a deliberate deviation from the prototype's dark toast); the behaviour is under Notifications and pop-ups (P2-16) . Hue Light value Strong value Hue Light value Strong value today f59e0b df6714 reports c4b5fd 7c3aed review 10b981 059669 lab f472b6 db2777 suggestions d946ef 8b5cf6 learning 2dd4bf 6366f1 portfolio 38bdf8 3b82f6 accounts 60a5fa 2563eb instruments 818cf8 4f46e5 services 38bdf8 6366f1 baskets fb923c f43f5e settings a1a1aa 52525b data 22d3ee 0d9488 audit fbbf24 b45309 quality a3e635 159f48 system status 2dd4bf 0f766e indigo / green 818cf8 / 34d399 4f46e5 / 059669 amber / rose fbbf24 / fb7185 d07206 / e11d48 sky / violet 38bdf8 / c084fc 0284c7 / 7c3aed teal / pink / slate 2dd4bf / f472b6 / 94a3b8 0d9488 / db2777 / 475569 \\ Contrast-adjusted (icon on its own chip over the surface must be at least 3:1 in both modes; enforced by theme/contrast.test.ts). Darkened minimally, hue kept: today f97316 to df6714 (2.48 to 3.01), quality 16a34a to 159f48 (2.89 to 3.00), amber d97706 to d07206 (2.81 to 3.01). The adjusted values are copied back into the prototype (ui.js). The prototype's navigation fallback ( 64748b) is replaced by the tile slate ( 475569) in the app. Dark-mode light values are all at least 4:1 on their chip (asserted in the test); no exceptions below 3:1."},{"d":12,"h":"Layout and shared list controls (P1-03a)","a":"layout-and-shared-list-controls-p1-03a","t":"- Shell: the content area is full width on every page (padding only, no max-width); text-heavy blocks keep a readable line length through their own inner widths (page descriptions 90ch, the roadmap placeholder card). From md up the shell is the viewport height and main is the one scroll container, so a screen can fill it (height: 100%) and scroll a region of its own; below md the document scrolls. - Grid (shared/ui/grid, imported by path so MUI Table stays out of the initial bundle) is the one list control: columns (id, header, accessor/render, width/minWidth/grow, align, numeric tabular figures, sortable, hideBelow breakpoint), rows + getRowId, controlled sort/onSortChange (aria-sort), optional controlled selection (selectedIds/onSelectionChange: checkbox column, select-all (\"Select all loaded rows\" in infinite mode), shift-click ranges), onRowClick + activeRowId, infinite ({ total, hasMore, loadingMore, onLoadMore, error?, onRetryMore? }: infinite scroll — a sentinel row at the end of the rows is observed with an IntersectionObserver rooted at the scroll container (400 px look-ahead) and calls onLoadMore once per page, never while loading, after a failure or while the rows shown are a stale list; every page re-arms it, so a first page that does not fill the viewport keeps loading; a small spinner row while loading; after a failure an inline \"Could not load more rows — Retry\" row; a fixed slim footer \"Showing 100 of 3,204\" with aria-live=\"polite\"; aria-rowcount is the total; End / PageDown / Arrow Down on the last loaded row asks for more; selection by id survives new pages) and resetKey (the list identity: a changed resetKey or sort scrolls to the top and resets the tab stop), loading (skeleton rows; rows already shown stay, dimmed), error + onRetry, emptyState, stickyHeader, stickyFirstColumn, density. It fills its parent; the body is the scroll container (header and footer stay), and it scrolls sideways when the columns do not fit. Keyboard: rows are one tab stop (row checkboxes are not), Up/Down/Home/End/PageUp/PageDown move, Enter opens, Space toggles selection; only events from the row itself are handled (never with Ctrl/Meta/Alt), so drag handles, inline editors and buttons in cells keep their keys; a new list (sort, resetKey) scrolls to the top and resets the tab stop. The reusable useInfiniteRows hook (shared/lib, over TanStack useInfiniteQuery: fetchPage(page, { pageSize, signal }), pages of 100, next page from page × pageSize < total, rows de-duplicated by id, previous rows kept and marked isPlaceholderData while a new filter or sort loads, loadMoreError + retryMore) feeds it. Semantics grid/row/columnheader/gridcell with aria-rowindex (position in the whole list), aria-colindex/aria-colcount, aria-multiselectable. Reuse points: a column with kind: 'handle' (drag handles, row menus; excluded from row click and keyboard) and getRowProps(row, index) (sx, ref, data- ); rows are memoised and the roving tabindex is kept in the DOM so keys and selection re-render only the rows that changed. Used by Instruments, Portfolio holdings and Data quality in infinite mode (Data follows in P1-17b); P1-05 (selection, bulk actions), P1-15 (basket members) and later lists reuse it. - Typeahead (shared/ui/Typeahead, MUI Autocomplete, imported by path): options with label, optional right-aligned count and hint; case-insensitive substring filter with the match highlighted; clearable (also by emptying the text); no-options and loading text; getOptionDisabled; a value that is not among the options stays selected; combobox/listbox semantics. Used for Sector; also for baskets, accounts and series later. - SearchField (shared/ui/SearchField, imported by path): the debounced search box of the list screens (commits 300 ms after the last keystroke, follows the value when it changes from outside, clear button); used by Instruments and Portfolio."},{"d":12,"h":"Portfolio screen (P2-01 / P2-05)","a":"portfolio-screen-p2-01-p2-05","t":"Route /portfolio (lazy chunk; nothing of it is in the initial bundle). Same viewport-height layout as Instruments from md up (header, filters, tiles and sync bar stay; the holdings list scrolls inside its card, loads further pages of 100 as it is scrolled and keeps the \"Showing N of total\" footer fixed; the page scrolls normally below md). All view state is in the URL: account, bucket, status (open default, closed, all), q, sort and holding (the open drawer); the summary tiles follow the account and bucket only, so loading more rows never changes them. - Scope: Account type-ahead (All accounts, or an account with the Portfolio role), Bucket type-ahead, a debounced search (symbol, name, ISIN) and an Open / Closed / All toggle. With \"All accounts\" and more than one account the list gets an Account column. Import, Sync now and Auto refresh act on one account: the chosen one, or the only one there is; with several accounts and \"All accounts\" they ask to pick one first. An account that is not connected shows \"Connect the account first\" with a link to its Connection tab, and its Import and Sync buttons are off (what is already stored stays visible). - Summary tiles (totals of the chosen account and bucket, from GET /api/holdings/summary): Invested · Current value (as of the valuation date) · Unrealised P&L ₹ with % · XIRR · Realised (est.) · Holdings (open, closed, not in master list). Gains and losses always carry a sign and an arrow (▲ +₹18,400, ▼ −₹12,000), never colour alone. An XIRR that cannot be shown is \"—\" with its reason ( insufficient : needs 30+ days and money in and out; n/m : beyond ±1,000 %; no data : no price) as the tile hint and a tooltip. An unpriced holding (no usable price) shows a \"No price yet\" chip and dashes for value, P&L, % and XIRR (never −100 %), and the totals leave it out with the note \"{n} holdings without a price are left out of value, P&L and XIRR.\"; an XIRR beyond ±1,000 % shows \"n/m\"; every reason is reachable by keyboard. Under the tiles: a chip \"12 lots use the default date\" (it lists the oldest purchase dates first, where the default dates are) and a footnote that these gross figures are price returns (while no dividend or charge is recorded) or that the net figures count dividends and costs only from the dates on their tiles (once some are); corporate actions are not included. - Income tiles (P2-14, built): a second row under the gross tiles from income of the summary: Dividends (the sum entered, \"from <date \" = the first dividend entered, \"none entered yet\" otherwise, and the unallocated part when there is one), Costs (trade charges plus the ledger's account-level costs, \"from <date \" = chargesFrom, \"none read yet\" otherwise), Net total return (with the return before costs and the date the net figures are complete from, coverageFrom = the later start of the sources that exist) and Net XIRR (with the reason when it cannot be shown, as for the gross XIRR). The gross tiles do not change; alpha, the benchmark and the value history say \"price return, before dividends and charges\". While nothing is recorded (income null) the row is replaced by an invitation — Dividends and costs are not in the figures yet , with Add a dividend (opens the Cash & dividends panel on the Add dividend form) and Read the ledger (takes the owner to the Sync trades & ledger control); zeros would claim there were none. With a bucket chosen the tiles count only the bucket's holdings' dividends and trade charges. - Sync bar: the last run in words (\"Synced 5 min ago — 1 new, 1 closed\"; a skipped run shows the reason the API recorded, e.g. \"The account is not connected to Dhan.\"), the auto-refresh setting and next run (\"Auto refresh: every 15 min in market hours (Mon–Fri 09:15–15:30 IST) · next run 09:30, 12 Oct IST\"), Sync now (progress \"Syncing…\", \"already running\" and \"connect the account first\" shown in words, and the safeguard: when Dhan's answer would close all or most holdings the API answers 409 portfolio.confirmation r"},{"d":12,"h":"Top bar (P1-21)","a":"top-bar-p1-21","t":"Left to right, as in the prototype: menu toggle (below md) · page title (from sm) · Search or jump to… (⌘K / Ctrl+K) · spacer · accounts control · notification bell · colour-mode toggle · user button (soft-indigo avatar with the owner's initials, name, chevron). Element Behaviour Real now or later Search or jump to Palette dialog: Pages (every navigation item; planned ones marked with their phase), Accounts , Actions (toggle dark mode, go to Accounts / Settings); substring match, arrow keys, Enter, Esc; combobox + listbox semantics. Icon only below lg. Real. Instruments and baskets join with P1-11 / P1-12. Accounts control Summary of all accounts with Connect / Reconnect (P1-16d). Doubles as the status element until the pills below exist. Real Notification bell Unread badge (max \"9+\", resolved items not counted) and a right-hand drawer: severity chip, title, text, relative time, up to two action buttons (or an Open link), \"Mark all read\", a collapsed Resolved group, empty state \"You're all caught up\" (P2-16: Notifications and pop-ups below). Items are derived from real state only: account not connected, token expired, token expires within 2 h (all three now come from the server as reconnect dhan), API key expires within 30 days / expired (still derived in the browser). An item's id includes the situation (e.g. the expiry), so a changed situation is new and unread. Read state is a per-browser convenience (localStorage). Real for accounts; data notices (the last automatic data update failed, sessions with missing bars) are derived by the backend, GET /api/data/notices (doc 05 §6.1; the bell reads them with the accounts' notices); service failures are derived by the backend too, GET /api/services/notices (doc 04 §3.1; the bell shows them next to the data notices); alerts and plan tickets feed it later (P2 / P7) User button Avatar (28 px, accentSoft background, accent 13 px bold initials), name (from lg), chevron; menu with name, email, Sign out. Real Market open + NIFTY pill, services-health pill Shown in the prototype. Later : need market data (P1-11 / P1-12 / P2-07) and the service framework (P1-13); not faked."},{"d":12,"h":"Notifications and pop-ups (P2-16, ADR 0027)","a":"notifications-and-pop-ups-p2-16-adr-0027","t":"What the app can mend by itself it mends quietly (doc 12 §3 \"Self-healing and attention items (P2-16)\"); what only the owner can do arrives once, as an item in the bell and as a pop-up , each with a button that goes straight to the fix, and goes away by itself when the cause is gone. - The bell and its drawer. The bell reads GET /api/portfolio/notices every 60 seconds beside the data, service and account notices and shows an unread badge (max \"9+\") that does not count resolved items . The right-hand drawer (400 px, full width on phones) has two groups: Unresolved — the open items, most urgent first (error, warning, information), newest first within a level — and a collapsed Resolved (n) group of what was resolved in the last 24 hours (greyed, labelled \"Resolved\", with no buttons; the group header shows only when there is something resolved). Each item has a severity chip (icon and a hidden word, never colour alone), a bold title, the text, the relative time and at most two buttons (the first filled, the second outlined; \"Retry now\", \"Reconnect Dhan\", \"Review\", \"Open trades\", \"Open\"); a notice without buttons keeps its Open link. \"Mark all read\" and the empty state \"You're all caught up\" are as before. A button marks the item read first; a route button navigates (and closes the drawer), a command button calls the existing endpoint it names (POST /api/accounts/{id}/sync/broker-history …) with the session and CSRF token, keeps the drawer open and waits on that button, then reads the notices and the screens again and says the result in a toast (\"Retry now: started. This notice goes away by itself once it works.\" or the API's own words when it refused, e.g. a run is already going). The browser follows only a route that is a path inside the app (one slash, no host) and only a command that is a POST to one of the whole-path patterns of the server's one list of commands - /api/accounts/{guid}/sync/broker-history, /api/accounts/{guid}/holdings/sync and /api/services/{id}/run - anything else is dropped, never followed. The list is not copied by hand: shared/api/commands.ts builds its allow-list from shared/api/attentionCommands.generated.ts, which npm run gen:api writes from the x-xdrishti-attention-commands extension of the API contract (doc 12 §3 \"Self-healing and attention items\"; P2-18, ADR 0030). A holdings Retry now cannot confirm a sync that would close all or most holdings: the refusal is shown with a pointer to Sync now on the account's Portfolio tab. Read state stays per browser (localStorage); it is keyed on the id plus since for the seven server attention kinds only (reconnect dhan, sync failing, holdings need review, trades unexplained, dividends pending, securities unmatched, and since P2-18 rows dropped), so a condition that clears and returns is unread again; every other notice (price alerts, 'holdings need a decision', the API-key expiry) is keyed on its plain id, which already changes with the situation or the day. The server sends Since = null for the 'holdings need a decision' notice (its At still moves on each poll: cosmetic). Items of the account notices that the browser still derives itself are the API-key expiry (key expiring, key expired); the Dhan login (missing, expired, about to expire) is the server's reconnect dhan. - Where the buttons lead (deep links). /accounts?account={id}&tab=connection (Reconnect Dhan, Connect to Dhan) and …&tab=portfolio (a failing sync's Open ), /services (a failing price poll's Open ), /portfolio?holding={id}&section=trades (a holding open on its Trades section) with &rebuild=preview (the rebuild preview open too; both parameters are taken out of the URL once the section has scrolled into view, so the link can be used again), /portfolio?cash=suggestions&account={id} (the Cash & dividends panel with the keyboard on the suggested dividends), /portfolio?focus=sync (P2-18, a rows dropped item's Open : the keyboard goes to Sync trades & ledger , or - when that button is off because a re"},{"d":12,"h":"2. Report catalogue","a":"2-report-catalogue","t":"Report Answers Visuals R1 Next-day plan What to do tomorrow and why Ticket cards, mini-charts, reserve list R2 Daily scorecard How did yesterday's plan do R per ticket, trigger hit/missed R3 Performance overview Am I making money Equity (R, ₹), underwater drawdown, KPI tiles R4 Strategy × timeframe matrix What works Expectancy heatmap, split by side R5 Exit-policy comparison 1:2 vs 1:3 vs trail vs pyramid per cell Grouped bars on identical entries R6 Side analysis Longs vs shorts Side-by-side KPIs and equity R7 Regime analysis What works in which market Cell × regime heatmap, regime timeline R8 MAE/MFE Stops too tight? targets too greedy? Scatter, histograms with target markers R9 R distribution Payoff shape Histogram R10 Calendar & time-of-day When it works Calendar heatmap, weekday/hour/expiry bars R11 Symbol & sector Where it works Treemap / ranked bars R12 Calibration Are probabilities honest Reliability diagram R13 Selector effectiveness Does top-3 beat the rest Primary/reserve/rejected, top-k curves R14 Backtest vs paper vs live Does reality match research Overlaid equity, slippage distribution R15 Monte Carlo risk Expected drawdowns Fan chart, percentiles R16 Learning changelog What changed and why Timeline R17 Account comparison How each account performs Per-account KPIs and equity R18 Data quality Can tonight's data be trusted Check status, flagged symbols R19 Discipline Am I following the plan Taken vs recommended, cost of deviations in R R20 Basket comparison Which basket works best, for which style/strategy Side-by-side KPIs, basket × strategy heatmap, vs basket buy-and-hold & Nifty R21 Instrument scorecard Which instruments consistently work / fail Ranked table, sparkline equity per instrument R22 Portfolio overview What is my portfolio worth and how is it doing Value, invested, P&L, XIRR, alpha; allocation by bucket/basket/sector R23 Portfolio returns Returns and XIRR by group over time XIRR by group bars, value vs benchmark, drawdown R24 Realised P&L What did closed trades make FIFO realised P&L by period / bucket / instrument, charges breakdown R25 Data coverage Do I have complete data for every tracked instrument and timeframe Coverage heatmap, import & EOD history R26 Decisions review Do my approvals/rejections add value vs the system's proposal Approved vs rejected vs lapsed outcomes in R R27 Basket comparison Which basket works best and why Multi-select baskets: KPIs, overlaid equity, strategy × basket, buy & hold R28 Pivot Any dimension × any dimension Heatmap with metric selector R29 Data quality Is every timeframe correct Validation status, stored vs recomputed R30 Learning journey Is the system actually getting better Weekly expectancy: live vs frozen-model counterfactual vs plan prices; problem→fix bands; cumulative uplift R31 Lessons What went wrong and how it was fixed Lesson cards (problem → diagnosis → fix → result), before/after metric, walk-forward evidence R32 Execution gap Where the edge is lost between plan and fills Waterfall (plan → slippage → missed → overrides → charges → actual), by strategy and month R33 Experiments Which settings were tried and what they did Experiment history with Δ expectancy, folds better, verdict, status R34 Loss tags Why trades lose Tag counts and R lost by tag, linked lessons R35 Smart-rule evidence Is each rule still earning its place Throttle: equity with/without; meta: R taken vs skipped; gate: R by VIX/breadth bucket; windows: R by hour; correlation matrix Standard KPIs: trades · win rate · avg win/loss R · expectancy · profit factor · total R · net ₹ after costs · max drawdown (R, %) · Sharpe/Sortino · avg holding · exposure · longest loss streak. Drill-down: heatmap cell → cell detail (equity, exit comparison, MAE/MFE, regime split) → trade list → trade replay (entry, stop, target, trail path, exit reason, feature snapshot)."},{"d":12,"h":"3. Exports & delivery","a":"3-exports-delivery","t":"Plan: page + PDF/HTML. Portfolio: CSV/Excel of positions, transactions and realised P&L. Any report: CSV/Excel. Weekly review pack (PDF): R3, R4, R5, R12, R13, R16 + assistant summary. Notifications appear in-app (SignalR); optional external channel only if you enable one."},{"d":12,"h":"4. Implementation notes","a":"4-implementation-notes","t":"- Report queries live in the .NET Reporting module; heavy aggregates via SQL materialised views refreshed nightly; responses are chart-ready series. - Lightweight Charts for anything price-based; ECharts for analytics. - The SPA is a PWA so it can be \"installed\" on your phone (LAN access)."},{"d":13,"h":"1. Roles","a":"1-roles","t":"Job Who Why Signals, stops, targets, sizes Deterministic .NET engine Exact, testable P(win), expected R Gradient-boosted trees in ML.NET (trained & scored in .NET) Measurable accuracy Exit choice, ranking, selection Deterministic rules on learned statistics Auditable Daily briefing, weekly review LLM Language summarisation Questions about your data LLM + read-only tools Numbers come from tools Draft strategy rules for the Strategy Lab LLM (you confirm; tests decide) Faster research Propose hypotheses (one variable) LLM (engine validates) Research help Suggest instruments / basket changes / next-day focus LLM + engine evidence → suggestions inbox; you accept or reject Human in the loop Extract data from filings/announcements (later) LLM (validated) Unstructured text Predict prices / make trade decisions Never Unverifiable"},{"d":13,"h":"2. Setup","a":"2-setup","t":"Component Choice Model server xd-llm: llama.cpp server (image quay.io/ramalama/ramalama, pinned) on the libkrun Podman machine with GPU (Vulkan), OpenAI-compatible API; started on demand (ADR 0005) Main model Qwen3-30B-A3B Q4 K M (MoE, ≈ 18.6 GB; measured 41 tok/s at 8K context in the container); alternate gpt-oss-20b (12 GB) — final pick by the eval set (§5). Context 16K default, 32K supported; keep prompts ≤ 8K tokens Small model none — the MoE main model is faster than a dense 8B; a dense 14B was rejected (first token 27 s) Embeddings nomic-embed-text or bge-m3 (for search over notes/docs) Fallback Host-native llama.cpp (Metal) — measured 1.1–1.4× faster generation and 1.4–1.8× faster prompt reading than the container, loads in 5–10 s; endpoint change only. The container meets the speed thresholds, so it stays the default Model choice is config; select by our eval set, not leaderboards."},{"d":13,"h":"3. Integration (.NET)","a":"3-integration-net","t":"- xd-api assistant endpoint uses Microsoft.Extensions.AI with tool calling; streams to React. - Tools are served by xd-mcp (ModelContextProtocol C SDK) over a read-only DB role : list tables, describe table, run sql(read only, row limit), get report(type, filters), get plan(date, account), get cell(key), get portfolio(group by, as of), get basket(name), make chart(spec), lab validate(yaml), lab run(yaml). - Guardrails: numbers only from tool results; no write tools except lab run; no promotion; no config changes (only proposed diffs you apply in the Config screen); prompts and tool calls logged in ai. ; broker secrets never in context. If the LLM is down, the nightly plan is unaffected."},{"d":13,"h":"4. Features","a":"4-features","t":"1. Daily briefing (≤ 300 words): regime, the tickets with one-line rationale, risks, system changes. 2. Ask your data : \"Expectancy of 15m SELL setups in high-vol regimes in 2025?\" → query + numbers + chart. Also portfolio and baskets: \"XIRR of MyLongTerm vs Nifty this year?\", \"Which basket worked best for 15m setups?\" 3. Strategy drafting : English idea → DSL YAML → validation → you confirm → Lab run → summary. 4. Hypothesis proposals for the learning loop (doc 09 §5). 5. Event flags (later): classify announcements for the event-risk filter."},{"d":13,"h":"5. Eval set (re-run on every model/prompt change)","a":"5-eval-set-re-run-on-every-modelprompt-change","t":"Task Test Pass Data questions 30 questions with known answers ≥ 90% exact numbers Briefing 10 historical plans No numbers absent from input; all tickets covered Strategy drafting 10 ideas Valid DSL; matches intent on review Event flags 200 labelled announcements Precision on \"material\" ≥ 80%"},{"d":13,"h":"6. Hermes Agent (Nous Research) — decision","a":"6-hermes-agent-nous-research-decision","t":"What it is: open-source (MIT) always-on agent: persistent memory, self-written skills, built-in cron, MCP client, messaging gateways (Telegram, WhatsApp…), works with local OpenAI-compatible models. Decision: not part of the core. Build the built-in assistant first. Evaluate Hermes in Phase 9 as an optional front-end (phone access, scheduled summaries) connected only to xd-llm and xd-mcp. Why cautious: a third-party review (Sept 2026) cites 44 published CVEs including high-severity remote-code-execution and supply-chain issues, and an audit found unrestricted shell and credential-file access in default config; self-written skills/memory can persist errors or injected instructions; it needs ≥ 32–64K context. If adopted, mandatory hardening: own image from a pinned release (no curl bash); shell, browser, web-search, file-system and media tools disabled; only xd-mcp registered; no secrets mounted; egress only to xd-llm/xd-mcp; skills git-tracked and memory reviewed weekly; messaging off by default; monthly CVE check. PoC pass criteria: hardened container works with the local model · ≥ 90% on the data-question eval · 8/10 strategy drafts valid · cron summary delivered 5 days running · prompt-injection probe (malicious journal note) causes no unintended tool calls · runs alongside the core stack without memory pressure · clear benefit over the built-in assistant."},{"d":14,"h":"15 — Smart Insights & Automation","a":"","t":"How xDrishti turns data into decisions you can act on , for both trading and portfolio management — without taking decisions away from you. Everything here is deterministic rules and statistics evaluated by the services; the LLM only explains (doc 14)."},{"d":14,"h":"1. Where the system is \"smart\"","a":"1-where-the-system-is-smart","t":"Area Smart behaviour Where you see it Next-day plan Learned setups, calibrated probabilities, top-N selection (docs 09–10) Plan review Pre-trade checklist Every proposed ticket is checked against your holdings, sector limits, results dates and data quality Plan review (per ticket) Portfolio Action center Rules flag positions that need a decision, per account Portfolio Price alerts Your alerts are watched by the services, not your browser Portfolio, notifications Data quality gate Instruments failing validation are skipped by the nightly scan and flagged on tickets Data quality, Plan review Report auto-insights Best/worst strategy, basket gap, side bias, best exit, regime, weekday, calibration — in plain sentences Reports → Overview Schedule intelligence Dependency and timing checks; run-after chains; catch-up after sleep; automatic data failover Services, Accounts Decision feedback Your approvals/rejections are graded against the system's proposal Reports → Decisions Auto-approval (autonomy) Tickets above your probability threshold with every check passing are approved at the cutoff Plan review, Learning → Autonomy Lessons & execution feedback Problems detected by monitors become lessons with a fix and a measured result; grading uses real fills Learning Tweak & re-run Change a learning knob and replay 12 months walk-forward with honest comparison rules Learning → Tweak & re-run Smart rules Market gate (no-trade days), equity-curve throttle, meta-labelling, learned entry windows, correlation-aware plan (doc 09 §14) Plan review banner & tickets, Learning → Smart rules Graded on fills Live and paper trades graded at executed prices; R at plan prices kept for the execution gap Today, Reports → Trade list, Learning → Plan vs actual Automatic loss tags Every loss tagged (gap, gave back profit, against regime, late entry, slippage, news) and linked to a lesson Learning → Lessons, Reports → Trade list Weekly learning digest One summary instead of daily approvals Today, Learning, notification"},{"d":14,"h":"2. Pre-trade checklist (per proposed ticket)","a":"2-pre-trade-checklist-per-proposed-ticket","t":"Check Rule Result Data quality Instrument's latest validation status ✗ fail blocks one-click approval (you can still approve with a confirmation, and it is recorded) Holdings conflict SELL ticket on an instrument you hold long in any account; or an open trade already exists ⚠ Sector exposure Open trades + already-approved tickets in the same sector ≥ profile limit ⚠ Event risk Quarterly results within 3 days ⚠ (later) Correlation 60-day correlation with open trades 0.7 ⚠ Checks are shown on the ticket card; \"approved despite\" reasons are stored and appear in the Decisions report."},{"d":14,"h":"3. Portfolio Action center","a":"3-portfolio-action-center","t":"Built in P2-09 (ADR 0022): evaluated on read, not by a service. GET /api/portfolio/actions computes the items from the current holdings, lots, prices, sectors, baskets and benchmark with pure functions (one rule = one function over a read model), exactly like the summary and the groups, so the card is always consistent with the numbers beside it and there is no job to run, lag or fail. Only the owner's decisions are stored (portfolio.action states). Thresholds are configurable (Settings → Action center thresholds; stored as JSON in platform.app settings, fixed ranges, Restore defaults , audited). Seven rules are built; Max holding exceeded (needs trading buckets and plans, P7) and Results due (needs events data, P15) are not, and the per-account tiles of doc 12 §10 are not part of this slice (Group by Account covers the figures). Rule Default trigger Suggested action Below cost Unrealised ≤ −10% (high if ≤ −20%) Hold with a stop alert, average only if the thesis holds, or exit Large gain Unrealised ≥ +25% Book part of the gain or set a trailing-stop alert Overweight Weight max(15%, 1.5 × equal-weight share) of the account (≥ 3 positions) Trim or rebalance Sector concentration One sector 35% of the account Diversify Max holding exceeded (not built yet) Trading-bucket position older than the 5-session limit Exit per plan rules Results due (not built yet) Results within 7 days Decide before the event Long-term soon Profitable holding becomes 12 months within 30 days Check tax impact before selling early (informational only) Lagging Nifty Held 180 days and XIRR more than 10 pp below the same cash flows in Nifty 50 Review the thesis / replace Not in a basket Holding not assigned to any basket Assign for basket analytics Each item offers Open , Set alert , Add to basket , Snooze 7 days or Done ; snooze, done and reopen are audited. Per-account tiles on the Portfolio page (value, P&L, XIRR, day change, top holding, number of open action items) come later; for now the Action center takes the Portfolio screen's account and bucket scope. How the built rules behave (P2-09). Severity, text and numbers are recomputed on every read; an item has a stable, path-safe id ({rule}:{holdingId}, or sector concentration:{accountId}:{12 hex of SHA-1 of the lower-cased sector}). Holdings without a usable price never fire a price-based rule (they are counted as \"no price\"). Overweight counts only priced positions and needs at least min positions (3) of them in the account; Sector concentration is one item per account and sector, \"Unclassified\" never fires, and the item lists the share and the largest holding; Long-term soon uses calendar dates (India): a lot is 12 months old on the same date a year later (29 February → 28 February) and the text only asks to check the tax impact; Lagging Nifty compares the holding's XIRR with the XIRR of the same cash flows put into the benchmark index, and a holding whose benchmark has no bars from its first flow (or whose XIRR is not meaningful) is not evaluated and counted as unavailable, never as fine; Not in a basket ignores holdings that are not in the instrument master. Order. Items are sorted by severity, then by the rule (below cost, lagging Nifty, overweight, sector concentration, large gain, long-term soon, not in a basket), then by the size of the trigger within the rule — the sizes of different rules are in different units (percent, rupees, days) and are never compared. Long-term soon lists a lot that cost nothing (bonus or transferred-in shares) as profitable, with no gain percentage. Decisions. Snooze hides an item until the India date 7 days later; Done hides it for as long as the condition holds and is then set to cleared (an UPDATE — the runtime role has no DELETE) so a later recurrence fires again. The reconciliation runs after every quotes tick and every holdings sync, after every change of the thresholds, and opportunistically inside the Action-center read (the one write a read makes — idempotent, a"},{"d":14,"h":"4. Price alerts","a":"4-price-alerts","t":"- Conditions: price at or below / at or above a level (quick presets ±5/10/20%), optional note. - Evaluated by services: alert symbols join the polled set (or the live set if already streamed); triggered alerts create a notification and audit entry. Alerts survive logout and browser close. - Later: indicator alerts (e.g. close below 50 DMA), trailing alerts, alert on basket-level drawdown. Built in P2-09 (ADR 0022). An alert is a master-list NSE share or ETF (not retired, quotable by Dhan; indices and futures are out of scope), a direction, a level (above 0, at most 10,000,000, 4 decimals), an optional note (200 characters of plain text) and a status active → triggered cancelled; never deleted, at most 200 active . It is one-shot . The holdings-quotes service quotes the union of the open holdings and the active alerts' instruments in the same request (still at most 1,000 per call) and checks every active alert against the last price of that tick : at or below ⇒ last ≤ level, at or above ⇒ last ≥ level. A spike between two ticks is not seen, so the latency is up to one tick (5 minutes, market hours) — doc 15's \"every minute\" returns with the live feed (xd-feed). An alert that is hit becomes triggered with the trigger price and time in the same transaction as its audit event , by a conditional update on status = 'active' (so it triggers once even with overlapping runs); an instrument with no quote in the answer stays active; a failed run triggers nothing; the run message adds \"N alerts triggered\". Creation refuses a condition that already holds at the last known price (the holding's last price, else the last stored daily close if it is at most 5 trading days old; unknown: allowed) with the price in the message. The bell shows price alert triggered (warning, for 7 days after triggering) and actions need decision (information, at most once a day) from GET /api/portfolio/notices; the bell polls it every minute, so the count of open items is kept for at most 60 seconds in the portfolio cache (dropped at once by the writes that drop the cached histories, and by decisions and threshold changes), and a failure to count never hides the alert notices. Since P2-10 (ADR 0024) the alerts are also checked on every live flush (every 2 s while xd-feed is connected, with the live price and its time; pre-open ticks trigger nothing), so the latency above applies only when the feed is off; the conditional update keeps a trigger single when both checks run at once, and the audit actor is system:live-feed for the feed. Since P2-16 (ADR 0027) the same endpoint also carries the attention items (reconnect Dhan, a sync that keeps failing, holdings to review, trades that do not explain a holding, possible ledger dividends, unmatched securities and, since P2-18, rows a Dhan read dropped), each with a title, at most two actions and a resolved flag, and the alert and decision notices get a title and an Open action too (doc 12 §3 \"Self-healing and attention items (P2-16)\")."},{"d":14,"h":"5. Data quality gate","a":"5-data-quality-gate","t":"After every end-of-day fetch the Data validation service re-checks the last N sessions of every tracked instrument (doc 05 §10b). Results drive: - a data quality score (pass = 1, warning = ½) shown on the Data quality page and Today, - per-instrument fail-closed : failing instruments are skipped by the nightly scan and flagged on any ticket, - one-click repairs (re-fetch the day, refresh aggregates) followed by automatic re-validation."},{"d":14,"h":"6. Report auto-insights","a":"6-report-auto-insights","t":"Computed from graded trades for the current filters (minimum 15 trades per group): best and weakest strategy (pause candidate), best vs worst basket gap, long vs short bias, best exit policy, most/least favourable regime, best/worst weekday, calibration quality (predicted vs actual win rate). The local LLM can turn these into the weekly review text; numbers always come from the report engine."},{"d":14,"h":"7. Schedule intelligence","a":"7-schedule-intelligence","t":"- Rules checked on every schedule change: pre-open check after the review cutoff and before 09:15 (and ideally after NSE pre-open price discovery 09:08); EOD fetch after 15:35; nightly pipeline after EOD fetch and portfolio sync; live window covering the session; no backups in market hours; no circular \"run after\" chains. Errors block saving; warnings are shown. - Run-after chains (e.g. data validation after EOD fetch; AI suggestions after the nightly pipeline). - Catch-up of missed runs after the machine wakes; automatic data failover to the standby Data account."},{"d":14,"h":"8. Further smart features (roadmap candidates)","a":"8-further-smart-features-roadmap-candidates","t":"Idea Value Rebalancing to basket target weights Suggested buy/sell list to bring long-term baskets back to targets Regime-aware sizing Scale risk per trade down in high-volatility regimes (learned) Correlation clusters Avoid hidden concentration across accounts What-if on approvals Show portfolio heat / sector exposure if you approve a ticket Post-trade review prompts Journal questions after each closed trade; discipline score Tax-lot aware selling Prefer lots that minimise short-term gains (informational) Earnings & corporate-action calendar Feed event filters and alerts Dividend tracking in XIRR Dividends from ledger as cash flows Volatility-scaled position sizing Size so each trade risks the same rupees after slippage, capped by liquidity (capacity)"},{"d":15,"h":"1. Two kinds of configuration","a":"1-two-kinds-of-configuration","t":"Kind Where Edited via Examples Trading config Stored versioned in config.config versions (seed: built-in defaults shipped with the backend) React Config screen (Monaco + JSON Schema, diff, activate) Scan scopes, data, strategies, exits, risk, accounts, learning, selection, portfolio Catalog data Database tables, edited in dedicated screens Instruments, Baskets, Import screens Tracked instruments, baskets & members, CSV import profiles (YAML seed optional) Infrastructure settings .NET appsettings.json + environment + Podman secrets Files in deploy/ Connection strings, endpoints, ports, log levels Rules: JSON Schema validation before activation; every run records the active config hash; secrets are referenced by name only (Keychain → Podman secrets)."},{"d":15,"h":"2. Trading config (v1 defaults, illustrative)","a":"2-trading-config-v1-defaults-illustrative","t":"version: 1 market: exchange: NSE timezone: Asia/Kolkata session: { open: \"09:15\", close: \"15:30\" } goals: target expectancy R: 0.20 max drawdown pct: 8 max drawdown action: halt and review Accounts and their roles are managed in the Accounts screen (stored in DB, password re-entry); shown here as seed. Dhan only (ADR 0008, 0009). API key and secret are entered in the app (write-only) and kept encrypted in accounts.broker secrets — never in this file, the config versions or the repo. accounts: - id: dhan main broker: dhan Dhan is the only broker client id: \"1000000001\" required; shown masked api key issued on: 2026-10-11 keys are valid 12 months → warning 30 days before secrets: [api key, api secret] set / not set only; values live in the vault roles: data: { enabled: true, primary: true } trading: { enabled: true, mode: paper, profile: standard, styles: [intraday, swing], instruments: { intraday: [EQ MIS], swing buy: [EQ CNC], swing sell: [FUT] }, capital: { source: broker funds, cap: 1000000 } } portfolio: { enabled: true, include in totals: true } default bucket \"Dhan · Main · Portfolio\" is created automatically - id: dhan longterm broker: dhan client id: \"1000000002\" roles: portfolio: { enabled: true, default bucket: Long term } optional: a custom bucket for imports; omitted = the account's own bucket - id: dhan swing broker: dhan client id: \"1000000003\" roles: data: { enabled: true, standby: true } failover if primary token/subscription fails trading: { enabled: true, mode: manual live, profile: conservative, styles: [swing], capital: { source: fixed, amount: 300000 } } portfolio: { enabled: true } data provider: { reconcile with: nse files } market data always from the primary Data account access: idle timeout min: 30 absolute timeout h: 12 lockout: { attempts: 5, minutes: 15 } step up for: [account roles, broker secrets, config activation, execution, data deletion] approvals: plan review cutoff: \"09:05\" unreviewed policy: lapse lapse arm top n allow modifications: [reduce size, tighten stop, change account, swap with reserve] suggestions: { expiry days: 3, snooze after reject days: 14 } risk profiles: shared; edited on Settings → Risk profiles; accounts may override values standard: risk per trade pct: 0.5 max capital per trade pct: 25 max new entries per day: 3 max open positions: 5 max total risk pct: 3.0 max per sector: 2 max pair correlation: 0.70 max intraday: 2 max swing: 2 daily loss: { R: 2, pct: 1.5 } weekly loss: { R: 5, action: halve size } conservative: inherits: standard risk per trade pct: 0.25 max new entries per day: 2 max open positions: 3 daily loss: { R: 2, pct: 1.0 } allocation: mode: replicate replicate distribute max accounts per symbol: 2 max total risk pct all accounts: 3.0 scan scope: which baskets the nightly plan scans (baskets are managed in the UI) - { basket: \"Best stocks\", styles: [intraday, swing] } - { basket: \"MyChoice\", styles: [intraday] } - { basket: \"MyLongTerm\", styles: [swing], sides: [BUY] } context indices: [NIFTY 50, NIFTY BANK, INDIA VIX] filters: { min price: 100, min avg turnover cr: 50, exclude: { fno ban: true, asm gsm: true, results within days: 2 } } catalog: auto track imported instruments: true master refresh: daily basket membership mode default: current current as of (for reports) data: base resolution: 1m csv import: inbox path: data/inbox watch inbox: true default profile: my 1min files precedence: [dhan eod, csv, live] which source wins on overlap conflict tolerance pct: 0.1 eod fetch: time: \"15:50\" lookback gap days: 5 backfill years on track: 5 Dhan intraday history limit 5 years throttle requests per sec: 5 aggregation: timeframes: [5m, 10m, 15m, 30m, 1h, 1D, 1W, 1M] session anchor: \"09:15\" daily source: official official derived keep partial last bar: true min completeness pct: 80 adjust: { splits bonus: true, dividends: false } bars stay raw; applied splits/bonus adjust on read (adjusted=true), dividends never (05 §4.2) quality: { max missing bar pct: 2.0, spi"},{"d":15,"h":"3. Infrastructure settings (appsettings.json excerpt)","a":"3-infrastructure-settings-appsettingsjson-excerpt","t":"{ \"ConnectionStrings\": { \"Db\": \"Host=xd-db;Database=xdrishti;Username=xd app\" }, \"Llm\": { \"Endpoint\": \"http://xd-llm:8080/v1\" }, \"Models\": { \"Path\": \"/models\" }, // ML.NET model files \"Seq\": { \"ServerUrl\": \"http://xd-seq:5341\" }, \"Secrets\": { \"Path\": \"/run/secrets\" } } Passwords and the broker vault key (BrokerVault Key, API only) are read from /run/secrets/ (Podman secrets) via the KeyPerFile provider. Broker API keys, secrets and tokens are not configuration: they are entered in the app and stored encrypted in the database (ADR 0008). Brokers PublicBaseUrl (default http://localhost:8080, set from XD HTTP PORT in compose) is the base of the broker redirect URLs /api/brokers/<broker /callback."},{"d":15,"h":"Instrument master (Instruments: , ADR 0010, P1-03)","a":"instrument-master-instruments-adr-0010-p1-03","t":"Option (appsettings / environment) Meaning Default Check Instruments:AutoRefresh:Enabled (Instruments AutoRefresh Enabled; compose: XD INSTRUMENTS AUTO REFRESH) First-start setting (ADR 0018): whether the master list is refreshed by itself once a day. Read once, when the service instrument-master has no stored row; after that the Services screen is the only place to change it true boolean Instruments:AutoRefresh:TimeIst (Instruments AutoRefresh TimeIst; compose: XD INSTRUMENTS REFRESH TIME IST) First-start setting (read once, like the switch above): time of the daily run as HH:mm (24-hour), India time (UTC+05:30, no daylight saving); Dhan publishes the file around 08:20 08:45 required, validated at start (ValidateOnStart): a wrong value stops the API with a message The two source URLs are not configuration: they are fixed in the code and on the allow-list (doc 05 §7.1, doc 17 §3). The manual \"Refresh list\" button works whether the daily run is on or off."},{"d":15,"h":"End-of-day run (Data:Eod: , ADR 0017 §2, ADR 0018)","a":"end-of-day-run-dataeod-adr-0017-2-adr-0018","t":"Option (appsettings / environment) Meaning Default Check Data:Eod:Enabled (Data Eod Enabled) First-start setting : whether the service eod is on. Read once, when it has no stored row; after that the Services screen is the only place to change it true boolean Data:Eod:TimeIst (Data Eod TimeIst) First-start setting (read once, like the switch above): time of the run as HH:mm (24-hour), India time ; a run never starts before that day's session has ended 15:50 required, validated at start The other Data: options (CSV folder and limits, Dhan fetch, gap look-back, data quality thresholds) are ordinary configuration and unchanged."},{"d":15,"h":"Holdings refresh (Portfolio: , ADR 0012, P2-01)","a":"holdings-refresh-portfolio-adr-0012-p2-01","t":"Option (appsettings / environment) Meaning Default Check Portfolio:AutoRefresh:Enabled (Portfolio AutoRefresh Enabled; compose: XD PORTFOLIO AUTO REFRESH) The API looks once a minute at the accounts whose own refresh setting is on and runs the due ones; false switches every schedule off (\"Sync now\" keeps working) true boolean Each account chooses its own schedule in the app (Accounts → Portfolio, and on the Portfolio screen): Off (default), Every 5 / 15 / 30 / 60 minutes during market hours (Monday–Friday 09:15–15:30 India time) or Daily at HH:MM IST ; it is stored in accounts.portfolio settings, not in this file (doc 04 §1, doc 12 §3). The Dhan holdings endpoint (GET https://api.dhan.co/v2/holdings) is fixed in the code and on the allow-list (doc 17 §3)."},{"d":15,"h":"Trades and ledger from Dhan (Portfolio:BrokerHistory: , ADR 0025, P2-13)","a":"trades-and-ledger-from-dhan-portfoliobrokerhistory-adr-0025-p2-13","t":"Option (appsettings / environment) Meaning Default Check Portfolio:BrokerHistory:Enabled (Portfolio BrokerHistory Enabled; compose: XD PORTFOLIO BROKER HISTORY) First-start setting (ADR 0018): whether the service portfolio-broker-history (\"Portfolio trades & ledger\", daily 16:30 IST on trading days) starts enabled; read once, when the service has no stored row, and ignored after it (then the Services screen decides). false starts it paused; \"Sync trades & ledger\" per account keeps working true boolean Portfolio:BrokerHistory:CallSpacingMs (Portfolio BrokerHistory CallSpacingMs; no compose variable) The least time between two calls to Dhan's trade history and ledger, kept by one throttle for the whole process; 0 switches the spacing off (tests) 250 integer 0 to 60,000 The window length (30 days), the page cap (200), the 30 s timeout and the 5 MB answer limit are fixed in the code, not configuration (tuned after the first real read). The two Dhan endpoints are on the allow-list (doc 05 §8.3, doc 17 §3)."},{"d":15,"h":"Self-healing syncs (Portfolio:Heal: , ADR 0027, P2-16)","a":"self-healing-syncs-portfolioheal-adr-0027-p2-16","t":"Option (appsettings / environment) Meaning Default Check Portfolio:Heal:RetryBackoffMinutes (Portfolio Heal RetryBackoffMinutes; compose: XD PORTFOLIO HEAL BACKOFF MINUTES) The waits before each quiet retry of a sync that failed for a reason that may pass (Dhan did not answer, its rate limit, an interrupted run), in minutes, comma-separated; their number is the number of retries of one failure, all inside the same India day. With the default the first retry is due 1 minute after the first failure, the second 5 minutes after the second, the third 15 minutes after the third. Empty switches the quiet retries off (the nightly and scheduled runs still happen) 1,5,15 up to ten waits of 1 to 999 minutes (^\\d{1,3}(,\\d{1,3}){0,9}$, blanks allowed) Portfolio:Heal:PersistDays (Portfolio Heal PersistDays; compose: XD PORTFOLIO HEAL PERSIST DAYS) The consecutive India days whose last attempt failed after which a failing sync (holdings, trades, ledger or the price poll) becomes an item in the bell; a reason only the owner can fix (the Dhan login) is told at once. The same number makes a failing price poll a failed run of the service 2 integer 1 to 30 Portfolio:Heal:DeepenMaxYears (Portfolio Heal DeepenMaxYears; compose: XD PORTFOLIO HEAL DEEPEN YEARS) How many years back from today the nightly deepening of the trade history reads at most, one year per day , while the trades do not explain a holding; 0 switches it off 8 integer 0 to 30 Portfolio:Heal:AttentionRetentionDays (Portfolio Heal AttentionRetentionDays; compose: XD PORTFOLIO HEAL ATTENTION DAYS) How many days a resolved item of the bell (portfolio.attention items) is kept before the healer tick deletes it (ADR 0030, P2-18). Unresolved items are never deleted; a resolved item is not shown after 24 hours anyway (that is fixed in the code). The runtime role has DELETE on that one table only (migration AttentionRetentionGrant) 30 integer 1 to 3650 These are ordinary deployment settings, read at start (not first-start settings): change them in the environment and restart xd-api. The quiet retries and the catch-up after a reconnect are done by the holdings refresh runner, so Portfolio:AutoRefresh:Enabled = false also switches the healing off (the scheduled runs, \"Sync now\" and the \"Retry now\" button keep working) - and with it the once-a-minute reconcile of the attention items and the retention above, because both are done by the same tick: the items are then refreshed only at the end of a holdings, trades or ledger attempt and after a Dhan sign-in, and no resolved item is deleted. The 1-minute look of the healer, the 24 hours a resolved item is kept in the bell and the 2 hours before a login runs out at which it is called \"expiring\" are fixed in the code. Details: doc 12 §3 \"Self-healing and attention items (P2-16)\"."},{"d":15,"h":"Live feed (Live: , ADR 0024, P2-10)","a":"live-feed-live-adr-0024-p2-10","t":"appsettings.json of xd-feed (and Live:ManualRunMinutes of xd-api too); bounds are checked at start-up. The window itself (09:00–15:35, trading days) is the schedule of the service live-feed on the Services screen, not a setting here. Live: MaxWsInstruments: 200 1–5000: the cap of the active set (alerts, then indices, then holdings by value) FlushSeconds: 2 1–30: how often the latest ticks are written SilenceSeconds: 30 5–300: no packet for this long in market hours = dead connection (when an index is subscribed) BackoffInitialSeconds: 1 1–60: reconnect back-off 1, 2, 4 … BackoffMaxSeconds, jittered BackoffMaxSeconds: 30 1–300 SubscribeSpacingMilliseconds: 250 0–5000: gap between subscription messages (≤ 100 instruments each) ManualRunMinutes: 30 1–240: how long Run now keeps the connection open outside the window"},{"d":15,"h":"4. Most-used settings","a":"4-most-used-settings","t":"Setting Meaning Default risk profiles. .max new entries per day Max new trades/day per account 3 risk profiles. .max open positions Max open trades per account 5 risk profiles. .risk per trade pct Size of 1R 0.5% styles.swing.max holding sessions Max holding 5 allocation.mode Replicate or distribute across accounts replicate scan scope Which baskets are scanned, with which styles/sides Best stocks, MyChoice, MyLongTerm data.csv import.default profile Column mapping for your CSV files my 1min files live.max ws instruments Cap on live-streamed instruments (active set) 200 portfolio.min days for xirr Below this, show absolute return instead of XIRR (v1: fixed at 30 in Xirr.MinDays, not yet configurable) 30 selection.min p win / min exp R lower Trade quality bar 0.45 / +0.10R learning.min trades Evidence needed per cell 30 goals.max drawdown pct Halt-and-review threshold 8%"},{"d":16,"h":"17 — Deployment & Operations (Podman, local-only)","a":"","t":"Everything runs in Podman containers on your Mac. No cloud service is required; internet is used only for broker/exchange APIs."},{"d":16,"h":"1. Podman machine","a":"1-podman-machine","t":"- Containers run in a Linux VM (Podman machine). Use the libkrun provider on Apple Silicon — it gives containers GPU acceleration (Vulkan), which xd-llm needs. - Size: 10 CPUs, 24 GB RAM, 120 GB disk (decision Q8; confirmed by the xd-llm benchmark in P0-15, 2026-10-10: with the 30B model loaded and the stack running the VM peaked at 5.3 GB because model weights live in GPU memory). Revisit the disk before tick-data storage (P13). - Static IP (decision Q6): not needed until automated orders (P12) — see §6. - The host Mac (48 GB) is already swapping heavily (28–42 GB of macOS swap in use in the sampled P0-15 runs); a resident 30B model adds ≈ 20 GB of GPU memory and loaded in 22–105 s (cause not established). xd-llm is therefore started on demand (ADR 0005). - Disk budget: VM disk 120 GB (originally planned 250 GB) holds the images, databases and, if used, the 18.6 GB model volume; revisit before tick data (P13). - Source code: local git only (decision Q7) — no remote yet. Keep a copy outside the Mac's internal disk (Time Machine or the external SSD) until a remote is chosen; xd-backup protects data, not code. - The Mac must be awake for nightly jobs (and during market hours for later live services): no sleep on power, wired network, small UPS recommended."},{"d":16,"h":"2. Containers","a":"2-containers","t":"Container Image basis Profile Notes xd-proxy Caddy (+ React build) core Only published HTTP port (127.0.0.1:8080 until login/TLS, then LAN); routes /api, /health, /hubs (the SignalR hub, WebSocket upgrade; later /hangfire); serves the docs read-only at /prototype/ (clickable prototype, delivery plan at /prototype/delivery/) and /docs/ (design-docs site) — both only for a signed-in owner (forward auth to /api/auth/verify, otherwise redirect to /login); compression, security headers xd-migrate API image, migrate core One-shot: applies migrations, provisions the owner account when none exists, then exits xd-owner-reset API image, owner-reset tools On-demand one-shot (xd-up.sh --owner-reset): replaces the owner's password from the secrets, revokes all sessions xd-api .NET 10 ASP.NET Core core REST, SignalR, auth, assistant endpoint; the only holder of the broker vault key (BrokerVault Key, refuses to start without it); Brokers PublicBaseUrl = http://localhost:${XD HTTP PORT} (broker redirect URLs); background workers for the instrument master (manual and daily refresh, Instruments AutoRefresh , doc 06 §2.2); networks back + egress (broker sign-in, master files) xd-worker .NET 10 + Hangfire core CSV import (watches data/inbox), EOD fetch, aggregation, account & portfolio sync, nightly pipeline, backtests, Lab runs, ML.NET training; scale replicas xd-feed .NET 10 core Market hours: Dhan WebSocket for the active set ( built , P2-10, ADR 0024): live prices written into the holdings and market.live quotes, price alerts on every flush, heartbeat in platform.service heartbeats; the vault key and the app DB password as secret files, networks back + egress, 256 MB, health check = heartbeat < 60 s old. The quote poll of holdings stays in xd-api (service holdings-quotes); live bars and depth recording come later xd-sandbox .NET 10 worker core User C plugins; no network , read-only data, CPU/memory/time limits xd-db PostgreSQL + TimescaleDB (official image) core Named volume; read-only role for tools xd-seq Seq ops Logs xd-backup restic + pg dump ops Nightly encrypted backup to external SSD xd-llm llama.cpp server (pinned RamaLama image) ai GPU via libkrun (/dev/dri); model from the host folder or the volume xd-models (decide in P9-01); no published port; on demand xd-mcp .NET 10 ai Read-only MCP tools xd-hermes Own image, pinned Hermes Agent ai (optional) Only after PoC; hardened (doc 14) xd-exec .NET 10 live (later) Orders; isolated network; only holder of order-capable tokens Defined in deploy/compose.yaml with profiles core, ops, ai, live (podman compose --profile core --profile ops up -d). Podman Desktop shows them as one application."},{"d":16,"h":"3. Networks","a":"3-networks","t":"Network Members front proxy (the only published port), seq (ops) back (internal) api, worker, sandbox , db, llm, mcp, seq, feed, proxy (to reach the api) egress (not internal, nothing published) api only (P1-16c, P1-03) exec (internal) exec, db (later) Egress allowed later worker, feed, exec (broker/exchange hosts only) xd-sandbox has no egress and no access except its job inputs/outputs. xd-api joins egress for Dhan's sign-in (ADR 0008, 0009), for the read of the account's holdings (ADR 0012), for the 1-minute chart data of the Data API (ADR 0016), for the trade history and the ledger of the account (ADR 0025) and for the instrument master files and NSE's trading-holiday list (ADR 0010, ADR 0017): outbound only, no published ports, no other member. The network itself does not filter hosts; the API's HTTP clients do (HTTPS, exact host and path, method, no redirects, no cookies or credentials, size caps and timeouts): Client Reaches only Method Limits Broker (Dhan sign-in) auth.dhan.co: /app/generate-consent, /app/consumeApp-consent (doc 05 §8.1) POST 15 s, 256 KB; no order, position or funds endpoint is callable Broker (Dhan holdings) api.dhan.co: /v2/holdings only, no query (doc 05 §8.2) GET 30 s, 5 MB; the stored access token goes in the access-token header and is never logged; the other api.dhan.co paths that are callable are the rows below, never an order, position or funds path Broker (Dhan trade history) api.dhan.co: /v2/trades/{yyyy-MM-dd}/{yyyy-MM-dd}/{page} — two valid dates and a page number in the path, no query (doc 05 §8.3; ADR 0025) GET 30 s, 5 MB; calls at least Portfolio BrokerHistory CallSpacingMs (250) apart; the account's access token goes in the access-token header and is never logged, nor are the URL or the rows Broker (Dhan ledger) api.dhan.co: /v2/ledger with the query of exactly from-date and to-date, each once, valid dates (doc 05 §8.3; ADR 0025) GET same limits and spacing as the trade history Broker (Dhan chart data) api.dhan.co: /v2/charts/intraday only, no query (doc 05 §6; ADR 0016) POST 15 s, 16 MB; one request at a time, at most Data Dhan RequestsPerSecond (4) per second, one retry on 429/5xx; the Data account's access token goes in the access-token header and is never logged; no other chart, order, position or funds path is callable Instrument master — Dhan images.dhan.co: /api-data/api-scrip-master-detailed.csv (doc 05 §7.1) GET 120 s, 100 MB Instrument master — NSE nsearchives.nseindia.com: /content/indices/ind niftytotalmarket list.csv GET 30 s, 5 MB Trading calendar — NSE holidays www.nseindia.com: /api/holiday-master with the query exactly type=trading (a fixed query text; the Dhan ledger above takes two date keys, the sign-in calls one key each; doc 05 §4.1, ADR 0017) GET 20 s, 1 MB; browser-like User-Agent and Accept: application/json, no cookie (none is kept or sent, and nothing from a stored secret), no redirects; a refusal is only a warning Nothing else is needed for the master: no published port, no volume (the file is streamed, never written to disk), no secret; xd-api stays within its 1 GB limit (the 35 MB file is read as a stream). The database image already includes pg trgm, which the migration enables for the search indexes (created by xd-migrate as xd owner)."},{"d":16,"h":"4. Secrets","a":"4-secrets","t":"1. Source of truth: macOS Keychain (xdrishti/<area /<name : DB passwords, owner sign-in, broker vault key, backup key). Broker API keys, secrets and tokens are the exception: they are entered in the app and stored encrypted in the database (item 4). 2. deploy/xd-up.sh reads the Keychain (creating strong random DB passwords on first run) → writes secret files to /Library/Application Support/xDrishti/secrets (private folder) → Compose mounts them as file secrets (ADR 0003). 3. Containers receive only what they need (mounted at /run/secrets/<ConfigKey , read by .NET KeyPerFile provider). The owner's sign-in credentials (xdrishti/auth/owner-email owner-name owner-password) are never generated: xd-up.sh asks for them at a terminal (hidden password prompt) or fails with a pointer to scripts/auth-secrets.sh. They are mounted only into xd-migrate (creates the owner when none exists) and the on-demand xd-owner-reset (profile tools, xd-up.sh --owner-reset), never into the running xd-api. 4. Broker credentials (ADR 0008, replacing the credential references of ADR 0007): the owner enters each account's API key and secret in the app (write-only, step-up); the API stores them in accounts.broker secrets encrypted with AES-256-GCM (account id + secret name as associated data, key version per row). The key material is the Keychain item xdrishti/broker/vault-key: xd-up.sh creates it (32 random characters) only when no such item exists — an item that exists but cannot be read stops the start instead of being replaced, because another key makes every stored secret unreadable — and writes the secret file broker vault key, mounted only into xd-api as /run/secrets/BrokerVault Key (the 256-bit key is derived from it with HKDF-SHA256). Back it up with the database. The former scripts/broker-secrets.sh, secrets/brokers.index and the read-only secrets/brokers mount are gone (xd-up.sh deletes leftover files). 5. Never in images, compose files, environment dumps, logs or LLM context."},{"d":16,"h":"5. Operations","a":"5-operations","t":"Topic Approach Start at boot launchd agent → podman machine start → xd-up.sh; services run with no user logged in to the app Missed schedules On start, services catch up missed runs (e.g. EOD fetch while the Mac slept) and reconcile desired state Schedules Stored in the database (platform.service schedules, runs in platform.service runs), edited on the Services screen with dependency validation and applied at the scheduler's next look (at most a minute) without restart. Built (P1-13, ADR 0018): one ServiceScheduler hosted in xd-api; the Hangfire jobs of xd-worker follow when that container exists (a deployment change, not a design one) Database migrations EF Core migrations applied by the one-shot xd-migrate service (API image, migrate command, as xd owner) before xd-api/xd-worker start; apps connect as least-privilege xd app; the migrations create market.undo csv bars, the one SECURITY DEFINER function (owned by xd owner, executable by xd app only) through which the app removes bars when the owner undoes a CSV load (doc 05 §9) Start / stop deploy/xd-up.sh (secrets → build → start → wait healthy → print URLs), deploy/xd-down.sh (--purge deletes data) URLs (local) App http://localhost:8080 · API docs /api/docs · Seq http://localhost:5341 · PostgreSQL localhost:5433 (dev override) Upgrades Versioned image tags; roll back by switching tag Backups Nightly pg dump + models + config → restic on external SSD; monthly restore test Health ASP.NET health checks per container; failures → in-app notification Logs Serilog → Seq; container log rotation Disk Timescale compression; tick data (later) 50–100 GB/year → external SSD volume CSV inbox Host folder data/inbox/ bind-mounted into xd-worker; processed files moved to data/processed/<job-id / CSV folder (P1-17a) Host folder data/Stocks CSV/ (XD CSV DIR) bind-mounted read-only at /data/csv into xd-api (Data CsvFolder); xd-up.sh creates it when missing; nothing is written there (ADR 0016) Market hours xd-feed runs all day and connects between 09:00 and 15:35 on trading days (the window of the service live-feed, editable on the Services screen); it heartbeats outside the window. Mac must be awake (the owner's job; xd-up.sh holds no keep-awake) Remote access Off by default; optional WireGuard on home router Dev loop dotnet watch and Vite dev server on the host against containerised db/llm/ml; same images for \"prod\""},{"d":16,"h":"6. Costs","a":"6-costs","t":"Item Cost Podman Desktop, .NET, React, PostgreSQL/TimescaleDB, Caddy, Seq (single user), LLM runtime & models ₹0 Dhan Data API (one account) ≈ ₹590/month incl. GST NSE files (optional reconciliation) ₹0 External SSD + UPS (one-time, recommended) ≈ ₹10–18k ISP static IP Only when automated orders are enabled (P12); decision Q6: later, revisit at the P11 exit Cloud ₹0 — none used"},{"d":17,"h":"18 — Roadmap","a":"","t":"Part-time ( 10–12 h/week) with an AI coding assistant. Each phase ends with an exit criterion. Portfolio monitoring comes early because it is useful on its own while the trading engine is being built. Phase Deliverables Exit criterion P0 Foundations (2w) Answer open questions (doc 01 §5); review the clickable prototype (docs/prototype/); Podman machine (libkrun); repo skeleton: .NET solution, React (Vite) app, compose.yaml with xd-db; Keychain→secrets script; Dhan trial (Data API, API key/secret token flow, 1-minute history for 20 symbols); CSV profile for your files + sample import; xd-llm GPU benchmark; ML.NET spike on linux/arm64 Data-quality note written; stack starts with one command P1 Data platform & instruments (5w) Dhan adapter (instrument master, history, accounts); CSV import (profiles, preview, validation, upsert, undo, folder watch, CLI); Timescale hypertables + continuous aggregates (5m…1h, 1D/1W/1M); corporate actions; calendar; context data; master list, tracked instruments, baskets (manual, rule-based, system); EOD fetch + backfill jobs; quality checks; Data quality window (validation service, stored-vs-recomputed aggregates, repair); React shell with login (password; re-entry for sensitive actions), Accounts (list/detail tabs, role matrix, risk profiles, wizard), Services screen with schedule configuration & dependency checks , Instruments, Baskets and Data/Import screens; commands/desired-state + audit Your CSV history imported; 5 unattended EOD fetches; all timeframes match a hand-check on 10 samples P2 Portfolio monitor & live feed (4w) Account sync (holdings, positions, trade history, ledger — trade history and ledger sync built in P2-13 (ADR 0025); dividends entered by the owner and charges in net returns and net XIRR are built in P2-14 (ADR 0026); a dividend payout file (CSV) is recognised by its headers, matched to holdings by scrip name with remembered corrections, previewed and imported with Undo — built in P2-15 (ADR 0028); positions are not built); self-healing syncs and actionable notifications are built in P2-16 (ADR 0027: quiet retries with back-off, catch-up after a reconnect, one earlier year of trade history read per day, attention items with buttons in the bell and as pop-ups); Dhan's downloaded reports — the global transaction report (bill-level trades with charges), DP-charges sheets and the demat holding statement, as CSV, .xls or .xlsx — are previewed and imported with Undo in P2-17 (ADR 0029; Dhan's own trades take precedence over the report's bill rows); the review follow-ups of P2-13 … P2-17 are closed in P2-18 (ADR 0030: read-only notices, 30-day retention of resolved items, a bell item for dropped rows, structured failure kinds, a kind-aware sync guard, one phrase table, an .xlsx guard); transactions, FIFO lots, positions, buckets; CSV/manual trade import; XIRR , returns, benchmark; daily valuations; xd-feed live feed for the active set + quote polling for holdings; Portfolio screen (per-account tiles, Action center , price alerts, group by bucket/basket/account/sector, tree-grid, charts) Portfolio values and XIRR match a spreadsheet check for 3 accounts/buckets; live P&L updates during market hours P3 Simulator, DSL & first strategies (5w) Streaming indicators/features ( built in P3-01 , ADR 0031: 19 indicators and session fields, a TimeframeBuilder and a FeatureFrame over closed bars only, tested for look-ahead (the frame never shows the forming bar; a prefix property guards against hidden state) and cross-checked against Skender; ADX, MACD, Bollinger, Supertrend, candle patterns and regime features follow in P3-01b); DSL parser → expression trees + look-ahead lint; event-driven simulator (conditional entries, stops, square-off, max hold, gaps, costs, slippage, MAE/MFE); ORB, VWAP PULLBACK, BASE BREAKOUT; scans scoped by basket 20 random trades hand-verified against charts P4 Exits & exit learning (3w) All exit policies incl. pyramiding; same-entries comparison; per-cell exit choi"},{"d":17,"h":"Open issues log","a":"open-issues-log","t":"Findings from the first real runs that are not fixed yet. Each entry says what was seen, why it matters and the direction to take; none blocks the current work. New entries go at the bottom with the next number; closed ones are marked Closed with the story that fixed them, never deleted. Figures from the owner's own accounts are kept out of this file (they live in the local, uncommitted analysis under data/dhan/). Issue Seen Why it matters Direction Status ISS-01 Dhan's trade-history API misses whole days. Several multi-stock \"bulk\" days that Dhan's own transaction report lists are absent from the API answer (only some trades of the period come back). First scheduled trades run after the mapper fix (2026-10-15). Lots cannot be rebuilt for the holdings whose buys or sells fall on those days; dates and returns stay approximate. Fill the gaps from the downloaded reports (P2-17 Import files ); find out whether the API pages, filters or time limits explain it (compare an API day with its report day) and record the finding in ADR 0025. Open ISS-02 Many holdings cannot be rebuilt (quantity mismatch / negative opening: the stored sells are larger than the buys the app knows). Same run; the holdings are listed in the run counts. Their lots keep the single default-dated lot, so XIRR and net returns for them are approximate; the bell item \"trades don't explain a holding\" will show. Import the transaction reports first (ISS-01), then re-run the rebuild; for IPO allotments, demergers and transfers (shares with no trade at all) decide how to record an opening position by hand. Open ISS-03 Dropped rows are not explained. A run reports how many rows it dropped but not why (no symbol, unmapped product, bad number…). Same run; a handful of rows dropped. The owner cannot tell whether the loss matters; the mapper-version re-read cannot be judged. Done (2026-10-15): every trade or ledger row a mapper drops carries a reason code from a closed set (BrokerDropReasons: unreadable row, missing id, bad id, missing side, bad quantity, bad price, bad time, bad date, missing amount, bad amount, zero amount, failed checks, store refused, other). The run counts carry droppedByReason (rows per code, adding up to dropped) and droppedRows (at most five locations: request window, page, row index, reason - never a name, amount, id or narration); the status of each kind shows the latest run's two fields; the Portfolio sync line says how many rows could not be read and why, with a Details disclosure for the locations; the audit entry and the log list the reasons as reason×count. missing symbol unresolved stays a skip (no symbol), not a drop. Not done there: the bell item for drops (ISS-09, closed later by P2-18) and the reason on needs reread (ISS-10). See doc 12 §3 and ADR 0025 / 0027 amendments. Closed ISS-04 Deepening history rarely changes a verdict. In the reconstruction model a sell fits or not mainly by what is held now, so reading an earlier year seldom fixes negative opening. Review of P2-16; confirmed by the first run (the earlier years returned no equity trades). Nightly calls with little benefit. Keep (bounded, harmless) but stop deepening once a year returns no equity trades; revisit whether the rule helps at all after ISS-01/02 are solved. Open ISS-05 Derivatives, commodity and intraday trading is not modelled. The sync and the importers count and skip those rows (a large share of all rows). Skip counts of the first run. Realised results of that trading (and its charges) are outside every figure in the app. Decide whether the app should track it (positions and intraday P&L story, P&L reconciliation against Dhan's statements); until then the Portfolio screen says what is left out. Open ISS-06 Report matching rules are unverified against real files. DP charges are matched to ledger charges by account, day and amount (and a depository label); the client-code check learns the code from the first accepted report; ISIN-less rows rely on name matching. Desig"},{"d":18,"h":"1. Research risks","a":"1-research-risks","t":"Pitfall Countermeasure Overfitting Walk-forward OOS only, ≤ 4 params, plateaus, min samples, paper stage Multiple-testing luck Test ledger, deflated thresholds, consistency across folds/years Look-ahead bias Closed-bar-only DSL, point-in-time features, single code path, automated tests Survivorship bias Historical constituents incl. delisted stocks Intrabar ambiguity Resolve on 1-minute path; if still ambiguous assume the worse outcome Unrealistic fills Gap fills at open, slippage model, no fills at locked circuits Cost blindness Costs in every simulation; max cost R filter Regime dependence Regime-conditioned stats, drift monitor, auto-pause ML leakage Purged & embargoed walk-forward"},{"d":18,"h":"2. Market risks","a":"2-market-risks","t":"Risk Countermeasure Overnight gaps (swing) Heat cap, event exits, max hold ≤ 5 sessions Correlated losses Sector/correlation limits, net exposure caps Liquidity/slippage Turnover filters; live slippage measured Extreme days Daily loss limit, gap/VIX filter, max-drawdown halt Short constraints Swing shorts via futures only F&O ban, surveillance, margin changes Nightly exclusion filters"},{"d":18,"h":"3. Behavioural risks","a":"3-behavioural-risks","t":"Risk Countermeasure Skipping/adding trades Auto-journal + discipline report quantifies deviations Tweaking after every loss Changes only via hypothesis loop; versioned config Over-trusting LLM prose Numbers only from tools; LLM never decides trades Scaling too early Paper ≥ 12 weeks, then reduced-size live"},{"d":18,"h":"4. Operational risks","a":"4-operational-risks","t":"Risk Countermeasure Bad data → wrong plan Quality gates; fail closed Misconfigured schedules (e.g. pre-open before cutoff, nightly before data) Dependency & timing rules block invalid schedules; timeline view; catch-up Automation over-reach (system changes too much on its own) Autonomy levels; weekly cap on automatic changes; always-ask list (risk increases, new strategies, live execution, accounts); change log with revert; automatic rollback after 60-trade monitoring Overfitting through repeated experiments Required improvement rises with trials (multiple-testing guard); 12 OOS folds; judged after live gap and on total R; shadow before apply Smart rules filtering too much (e.g. too many no-trade days) or learned on noise Evidence shown per rule; review flag when a rule removes 50% of trades or its skipped trades are not worse than taken ones; rules learned from walk-forward data, not only live trades Learning from plan prices that cannot be achieved Grade on actual fills; slippage model per liquidity bucket; capacity score Alert fatigue (too many action items) Smarter thresholds (relative to account size), severity filter, snooze/done, per-account focus Plan not reviewed in time Unreviewed tickets lapse (safe default); reminders before cutoff; phone review via PWA Account role misconfiguration (no data account, wrong trading account) Validation rules (exactly one primary Data account), password re-entry, audit, Services screen warnings Unauthorised access Login (strong password), lockout, idle timeout, LAN-only, audit of logins CSV import errors (wrong timezone, bar-end labels, adjusted vs raw prices, duplicates) Import profiles, preview before commit, session-boundary checks, conflict log, undo per import job Live-feed overload or disconnects Live data only for the active set (cap max ws instruments); auto-reconnect; EOD official data replaces live bars Incomplete portfolio history (API history window, other brokers) CSV/manual transaction import; reconciliation of computed vs broker holdings each night Misleading XIRR on short holdings Absolute return below min days for xirr; XIRR flagged \"short period\" Silent job failure Hangfire retries + in-app notification + Seq alerts Data loss Nightly restic backups; monthly restore test Secret leakage Keychain → Podman secrets; never in config/logs/LLM Broker API changes / token expiry Adapter isolation, retries, fallback provider, token status on Accounts screen Mac asleep/offline Power settings, UPS, wired network; broker-side stops once execution exists VM disk/memory exhaustion Machine sizing, Timescale compression, disk alerts, external SSD for ticks Runaway automated orders (later) Pre-trade checks, ≤ 2 orders/s, idempotency, kill switch, reconciliation freeze Third-party agent vulnerabilities (Hermes) Optional only; hardened container; pinned version; CVE checks"},{"d":18,"h":"5. Compliance (India)","a":"5-compliance-india","t":"- Personal use only : one user, own accounts. Sharing or selling recommendations could require SEBI Research Analyst / Investment Adviser registration — out of scope by design. - SEBI retail algo framework (mandatory since 1 April 2026) applies when orders are placed via broker APIs: registered app on a whitelisted static IP , daily login/2FA, broker as principal. Own strategies staying ≤ 10 orders/second per exchange are tagged with the broker's generic algo ID; above that, exchange approval is needed. v1 places no orders; Phase 12 caps at 2 orders/s. Re-check circulars before Phase 12. Static-IP decision (Q6, 2026-10-09): not needed until Phase 12; ask the ISP and re-check circulars at the P11 exit. - Data APIs used within the broker's terms. - Forex/CFD trading via offshore platforms (e.g. MT5 brokers) is not part of this system."},{"d":18,"h":"6. Expectations","a":"6-expectations","t":"Good systematic setups typically show 40–60% win rates and +0.1R to +0.4R expectancy after costs. Backtests showing 80% wins and +1R/trade are almost certainly overfit or leaking. Losing weeks and multi-week drawdowns are normal; the Monte Carlo report shows what to expect. The value is consistency, risk control and learning from evidence."},{"d":19,"h":"20 — Extended Modules (later phases)","a":"","t":"Designed now so v1 leaves room for them; built after v1 is proven. Module Phase Container / project A. Automated order placement P12 xd-exec / XDrishti.Execution B. Tick & order-book data Recorder from P7, features P13 xd-feed / XDrishti.Feed C. Options & statistical arbitrage P14 Engine modules D. Fundamentals & events P15 Worker jobs + assistant extraction HFT / latency arbitrage Not pursued —"},{"d":19,"h":"A. Automated order placement","a":"a-automated-order-placement","t":"Modes per account: paper → manual live → assisted (system prepares exact order / super-order parameters) → semi auto (one-tap confirm in UI) → auto live (within hard limits, every action notified). - Broker-side stop first — protection never depends on the Mac staying up. - Pre-trade checks: same limits as planning + price band vs LTP, freeze quantity, margin. - Idempotent client order ids; order-update stream + end-of-day reconciliation; mismatch ⇒ freeze. - Kill switch: ours (stop new, cancel pending, optional exit-all) + broker's where available; triggered by max drawdown, daily loss, feed loss or a button. - Prerequisites: ISP static IP whitelisted at Dhan; daily login/2FA; ≤ 2 orders/s; Dhan sandbox testing first."},{"d":19,"h":"B. Tick & order-book data","a":"b-tick-order-book-data","t":"- Dhan live feed + 20-level depth (up to 50 instruments/connection) and 200-level depth (NSE equity & derivatives) over WebSocket (binary). No historical depth from brokers ⇒ record our own. - Extends the v1 xd-feed (which already streams the active set): adds 20-level depth subscriptions for a configured recording list; writes to Timescale hypertables (compressed); 50–100 GB/year. - Start recording in P7 so ≥ 6 months exist by P13. - Uses: order-book imbalance, depth pressure near trigger, aggressive volume delta, spread → meta-model features and realistic slippage. Kept only if OOS improves. - HFT/latency arbitrage not pursued: home latency vs co-located firms, order-rate limits and costs leave no retail edge."},{"d":19,"h":"C. Options & statistical arbitrage","a":"c-options-statistical-arbitrage","t":"- Data: Dhan option chain API (OI, Greeks, IV, bid/ask; 1 request / 3 s per underlying-expiry) and expired options data API for backtests; own chain snapshots (5-min for indices, EOD for stocks). - Families: directional expression (ITM/ATM options for our signals), defined-risk debit spreads (swing), index credit spreads / iron condors in range regimes, event/IV-aware positioning. - New learning dimension instrument expression (cash / future / option buy / debit spread …). - Simulation from historical option prices; Black-Scholes with recorded IV only as fallback; premium and underlying-based exits; exit before expiry by default; liquidity filters (index options first). - \"Slow\" statistical arbitrage: PAIRS (cointegration mean reversion) and BASIS (cash-futures) on 5m+ bars — two-leg, so practical with module A."},{"d":19,"h":"D. Fundamentals & events","a":"d-fundamentals-events","t":"- Data: exchange filings (results XBRL/PDF), shareholding (promoter holding, pledges), announcements, results calendar — stored point-in-time by filing timestamp . - Uses: swing universe quality filter; features (growth, margin trend, surprise vs last 4 quarters, valuation percentile); PEAD (post-earnings drift) swing family; event-risk filter. - Assistant extracts structured fields from PDFs/announcements, validated against XBRL; outputs are features measured like any other, never trade decisions."},{"d":20,"h":"21 — Glossary","a":"","t":"Term Meaning Account role What an account is used for: Data, Trading, Portfolio (one account can have several) Action center List of portfolio positions needing a decision, generated by rules per account Active set Instruments streamed live: open positions, armed tickets, context indices, pinned baskets Approval Your decision on a proposed ticket or suggestion: approve, modify or reject Armed ticket Approved ticket that passed the pre-open check and is being watched live ATR Average True Range — average bar range; scales stops/targets to volatility Auto-approval Ticket approved by the pre-open service at the cutoff because it meets your autonomy rule (P(win) threshold and every check passing) Autonomy level How much the system decides alone: advisory, supervised (default) or autonomous within limits Basket Named group of tracked instruments (manual, rule-based or system); an instrument can be in many Bias timeframe Higher timeframe deciding the allowed direction for a lower-timeframe entry Bucket (portfolio) Exclusive grouping of positions so portfolio totals add up CAGR Compound annual growth rate Calibration Adjusting model scores so predicted probabilities match observed frequencies Capacity score How reliably a cell's tickets actually get filled (partial fills, rejections, missed triggers); used in selection Cell (setup cell) Unit of learning: strategy × timeframe × side × style × exit policy × regime Champion / challenger Current model/parameters vs a candidate running in shadow until proven better Common random numbers Replaying candidate and current settings on the same trades/days so only the change differs Conditional ticket Trade defined by trigger, validity window, stop, exit, size — executed only if triggered Confluence Several strategies/timeframes agreeing on the same symbol and side Continuous aggregate TimescaleDB materialised view that keeps derived bars (5m, 15m…) up to date Control plane The UI/API where you record what you want; services do the work CPR Central Pivot Range from the previous day's high/low/close Data quality score Share of tracked instruments passing validation (warnings count half) Deflated Sharpe Sharpe ratio adjusted for the number of strategies tried Drift Live/paper performance moving away from backtest expectations DSL The strategy rules language used by built-ins and the Strategy Lab Embargo / purge Removing overlap between train and test data to prevent leakage Entry window (learned) Best entry hour for a setup from walk-forward results; used as the ticket's validity window Equity-curve throttle Risk is cut (default × 0.5) while the system's own equity is below its N-trade average Execution gap Difference between results at plan prices and at actual fills (slippage, missed triggers, overrides, charges) Expectancy (E[R]) Average result per trade in R after costs Experiment (replay) Changing a learning knob and re-running walk-forward to compare with the current model before anything goes live Fail closed On error, produce no plan rather than a possibly wrong one Failover (data) Automatic switch to the standby Data account when the primary fails FIFO lots Sells consume the oldest buys first; basis for realised P&L Frozen-model counterfactual What a copy of last year's model would have done on the same days — the baseline for measuring learning progress Graded on fills Results computed from executed prices, not plan prices; plan-price R is kept to measure the execution gap Hangfire .NET background-job library (schedules, retries) using PostgreSQL storage Heat Sum of open risk across positions as % of capital Import profile Saved column mapping for a CSV file format Lapsed A proposed ticket not reviewed before the cutoff — never armed Learning health 0–100 score from data quality, calibration, execution, stability and evidence Lens How results are grouped: all trades, by basket, by instrument, by trade, by strategy, by account Lesson Record of a detected problem: what went wrong → diagno"},{"d":20,"h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","t":"- Van K. Tharp — Trade Your Way to Financial Freedom (R-multiples, expectancy, sizing) - Marcos López de Prado — Advances in Financial Machine Learning (triple barrier, meta-labeling, purged CV) - Kevin Davey — Building Winning Algorithmic Trading Systems (walk-forward, Monte Carlo, incubation)"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Account role","alts":["Account role"],"def":"What an account is used for: Data, Trading, Portfolio (one account can have several)"},{"term":"Action center","alts":["Action center"],"def":"List of portfolio positions needing a decision, generated by rules per account"},{"term":"Active set","alts":["Active set"],"def":"Instruments streamed live: open positions, armed tickets, context indices, pinned baskets"},{"term":"Approval","alts":["Approval"],"def":"Your decision on a proposed ticket or suggestion: approve, modify or reject"},{"term":"Armed ticket","alts":["Armed ticket"],"def":"Approved ticket that passed the pre-open check and is being watched live"},{"term":"ATR","alts":["ATR"],"def":"Average True Range — average bar range; scales stops/targets to volatility"},{"term":"Auto-approval","alts":["Auto-approval"],"def":"Ticket approved by the pre-open service at the cutoff because it meets your autonomy rule (P(win) threshold and every check passing)"},{"term":"Autonomy level","alts":["Autonomy level"],"def":"How much the system decides alone: advisory, supervised (default) or autonomous within limits"},{"term":"Basket","alts":["Basket"],"def":"Named group of tracked instruments (manual, rule-based or system); an instrument can be in many"},{"term":"Bias timeframe","alts":["Bias timeframe"],"def":"Higher timeframe deciding the allowed direction for a lower-timeframe entry"},{"term":"Bucket (portfolio)","alts":["Bucket","portfolio"],"def":"Exclusive grouping of positions so portfolio totals add up"},{"term":"CAGR","alts":["CAGR"],"def":"Compound annual growth rate"},{"term":"Calibration","alts":["Calibration"],"def":"Adjusting model scores so predicted probabilities match observed frequencies"},{"term":"Capacity score","alts":["Capacity score"],"def":"How reliably a cell's tickets actually get filled (partial fills, rejections, missed triggers); used in selection"},{"term":"Cell (setup cell)","alts":["Cell","setup cell"],"def":"Unit of learning: strategy × timeframe × side × style × exit policy × regime"},{"term":"Champion / challenger","alts":["Champion","challenger"],"def":"Current model/parameters vs a candidate running in shadow until proven better"},{"term":"Common random numbers","alts":["Common random numbers"],"def":"Replaying candidate and current settings on the same trades/days so only the change differs"},{"term":"Conditional ticket","alts":["Conditional ticket"],"def":"Trade defined by trigger, validity window, stop, exit, size — executed only if triggered"},{"term":"Confluence","alts":["Confluence"],"def":"Several strategies/timeframes agreeing on the same symbol and side"},{"term":"Continuous aggregate","alts":["Continuous aggregate"],"def":"TimescaleDB materialised view that keeps derived bars (5m, 15m…) up to date"},{"term":"Control plane","alts":["Control plane"],"def":"The UI/API where you record what you want; services do the work"},{"term":"CPR","alts":["CPR"],"def":"Central Pivot Range from the previous day's high/low/close"},{"term":"Data quality score","alts":["Data quality score"],"def":"Share of tracked instruments passing validation (warnings count half)"},{"term":"Deflated Sharpe","alts":["Deflated Sharpe"],"def":"Sharpe ratio adjusted for the number of strategies tried"},{"term":"Drift","alts":["Drift"],"def":"Live/paper performance moving away from backtest expectations"},{"term":"DSL","alts":["DSL"],"def":"The strategy rules language used by built-ins and the Strategy Lab"},{"term":"Embargo / purge","alts":["Embargo","purge"],"def":"Removing overlap between train and test data to prevent leakage"},{"term":"Entry window (learned)","alts":["Entry window","learned"],"def":"Best entry hour for a setup from walk-forward results; used as the ticket's validity window"},{"term":"Equity-curve throttle","alts":["Equity-curve throttle"],"def":"Risk is cut (default × 0.5) while the system's own equity is below its N-trade average"},{"term":"Execution gap","alts":["Execution gap"],"def":"Difference between results at plan prices and at actual fills (slippage, missed triggers, overrides, charges)"},{"term":"Expectancy (E[R])","alts":["Expectancy"],"def":"Average result per trade in R after costs"},{"term":"Experiment (replay)","alts":["Experiment","replay"],"def":"Changing a learning knob and re-running walk-forward to compare with the current model before anything goes live"},{"term":"Fail closed","alts":["Fail closed"],"def":"On error, produce no plan rather than a possibly wrong one"},{"term":"Failover (data)","alts":["Failover","data"],"def":"Automatic switch to the standby Data account when the primary fails"},{"term":"FIFO lots","alts":["FIFO lots"],"def":"Sells consume the oldest buys first; basis for realised P&L"},{"term":"Frozen-model counterfactual","alts":["Frozen-model counterfactual"],"def":"What a copy of last year's model would have done on the same days — the baseline for measuring learning progress"},{"term":"Graded on fills","alts":["Graded on fills"],"def":"Results computed from executed prices, not plan prices; plan-price R is kept to measure the execution gap"},{"term":"Hangfire","alts":["Hangfire"],"def":".NET background-job library (schedules, retries) using PostgreSQL storage"},{"term":"Heat","alts":["Heat"],"def":"Sum of open risk across positions as % of capital"},{"term":"Import profile","alts":["Import profile"],"def":"Saved column mapping for a CSV file format"},{"term":"Lapsed","alts":["Lapsed"],"def":"A proposed ticket not reviewed before the cutoff — never armed"},{"term":"Learning health","alts":["Learning health"],"def":"0–100 score from data quality, calibration, execution, stability and evidence"},{"term":"Lens","alts":["Lens"],"def":"How results are grouped: all trades, by basket, by instrument, by trade, by strategy, by account"},{"term":"Lesson","alts":["Lesson"],"def":"Record of a detected problem: what went wrong → diagnosis → fix → result, with revert"},{"term":"Loss tag","alts":["Loss tag"],"def":"Automatic reason attached to a losing trade (gap through stop, gave back open profit, against regime, …)"},{"term":"MAE / MFE","alts":["MAE","MFE"],"def":"Maximum adverse / favourable excursion during a trade"},{"term":"Market gate","alts":["Market gate"],"def":"Learned rule deciding whether tomorrow is a normal, reduced or no-trade day (VIX, breadth, events)"},{"term":"Master list","alts":["Master list"],"def":"All instruments from Dhan's instrument master"},{"term":"MCP","alts":["MCP"],"def":"Model Context Protocol — standard way to expose tools to an LLM"},{"term":"Meta-labeling","alts":["Meta-labeling"],"def":"ML deciding whether to take a trade proposed by a rule strategy"},{"term":"Meta-labelling","alts":["Meta-labelling"],"def":"A second model that decides whether to take a signal and how big, trained on your graded trades"},{"term":"MIS / CNC","alts":["MIS","CNC"],"def":"Intraday margin / delivery product types (Indian brokers)"},{"term":"ML.NET","alts":["ML.NET"],"def":"Microsoft's machine-learning library for .NET; trains and scores our models in-process"},{"term":"NR7","alts":["NR7"],"def":"Narrowest range of the last 7 bars"},{"term":"OOS","alts":["OOS"],"def":"Out-of-sample — data not used for fitting"},{"term":"ORB","alts":["ORB"],"def":"Opening Range Breakout"},{"term":"Paper trading","alts":["Paper trading"],"def":"Following the system without real money to validate it"},{"term":"PAV calibration","alts":["PAV calibration"],"def":"Pool-Adjacent-Violators — isotonic calibration of model probabilities"},{"term":"Pivot builder","alts":["Pivot builder"],"def":"Report that crosses any two dimensions with a chosen metric"},{"term":"Pre-trade checklist","alts":["Pre-trade checklist"],"def":"Automatic checks shown on each proposed ticket before you approve"},{"term":"Profit factor","alts":["Profit factor"],"def":"Gross profit / gross loss"},{"term":"Pyramiding","alts":["Pyramiding"],"def":"Adding to a winner at set levels while capping total risk at the initial 1R"},{"term":"R / R-multiple","alts":["R-multiple"],"def":"Result divided by initial risk"},{"term":"Regime","alts":["Regime"],"def":"Market state (trend × volatility) used to condition statistics"},{"term":"Reserve list","alts":["Reserve list"],"def":"Next-best tickets used if a primary is invalidated"},{"term":"Rollback (automatic)","alts":["Rollback","automatic"],"def":"Undoing an applied learning change when live results over the monitoring window fall below the previous version"},{"term":"Run-after chain","alts":["Run-after chain"],"def":"A service scheduled to run when another service finishes successfully"},{"term":"Same bet","alts":["Same bet"],"def":"Two tickets or positions whose 60-day returns are correlated above the limit; the weaker one goes to reserve"},{"term":"Service identity","alts":["Service identity"],"def":"Background services act as the system, not as a logged-in user"},{"term":"Square-off","alts":["Square-off"],"def":"Closing an intraday position before session end"},{"term":"Step-up auth","alts":["Step-up auth"],"def":"Password re-entry before sensitive actions (role, credential, risk and autonomy changes)"},{"term":"Stored vs recomputed","alts":["Stored vs recomputed"],"def":"Validation comparing saved aggregate bars with bars rebuilt from 1-minute data"},{"term":"Suggestions inbox","alts":["Suggestions inbox"],"def":"AI/engine proposals awaiting your accept/reject"},{"term":"Time stop","alts":["Time stop"],"def":"Exit if no progress within N bars/sessions"},{"term":"Tracked instrument","alts":["Tracked instrument"],"def":"Instrument you've switched on for data collection, scans and analytics"},{"term":"Triple barrier","alts":["Triple barrier"],"def":"Labelling by which is hit first: target, stop or time limit"},{"term":"VWAP","alts":["VWAP"],"def":"Volume-weighted average price of the session"},{"term":"Walk-forward","alts":["Walk-forward"],"def":"Repeated train-on-past / test-on-next-period evaluation"},{"term":"Weekly learning digest","alts":["Weekly learning digest"],"def":"Weekly summary of progress vs the frozen model, automatic changes, items needing you and loss causes"},{"term":"XIRR","alts":["XIRR"],"def":"Annualised return computed from dated cash flows (buys, sells, dividends, current value)"}]};
